window.PPL_DATA={"version": 1, "cards": [{"id": "d1c1", "day": 1, "dayTitle": "What a Private Pilot Certificate Lets You Do", "title": "What the certificate is · What you can do", "body": "<h3>What the certificate is</h3><p>A <b>private pilot certificate</b> is the FAA license that lets you act as <b>pilot in command</b> (PIC: the person with final authority and responsibility for the flight) of an aircraft. Yours will carry a <b>category</b> and <b>class</b> rating: <i>airplane, single-engine land</i>, meaning a fixed-wing airplane with one engine that takes off from and lands on the ground rather than water.</p><p>The <b>FAA</b> (Federal Aviation Administration) writes the rules, and the rules live in the <b>FARs</b> (Federal Aviation Regulations, formally Title 14 of the Code of Federal Regulations). Part 61 covers pilot certification; Part 91 covers general operating and flight rules. You will hear those two numbers constantly.</p><h3>What you can do</h3><p>With the certificate you can fly a small airplane almost anywhere in the United States, day or night, and you can carry passengers: family, friends, a coworker who wants to see the lake from above. You can fly for fun, for travel, or for business as long as the flying itself is incidental to that business.</p><p>There is one big money rule. A private pilot may not fly <b>for compensation or hire</b>, meaning nobody can pay you to fly them or their cargo. You may, however, split the operating expenses of a flight with your passengers in equal shares (this is in 14 CFR 61.113). If you and two friends fly to lunch and the fuel, oil, and rental cost is $300, each of you can pay $100. You cannot have them pay the whole thing.</p>", "words": 243, "figs": []}, {"id": "d1c2", "day": 1, "dayTitle": "What a Private Pilot Certificate Lets You Do", "title": "What you cannot do (yet) · The path, in four pieces", "body": "<h3>What you cannot do (yet)</h3><p>Without more training you cannot fly in clouds or in weather that requires flying by instruments alone; that takes an <b>instrument rating</b>. You cannot fly for pay; that takes a <b>commercial certificate</b>. Some airplanes need extra endorsements (a logbook sign-off from an instructor), such as high-performance (more than 200 horsepower), complex, or tailwheel airplanes. These are later chapters in a flying life, not obstacles today.</p><h3>The path, in four pieces</h3><p>Think of the certificate as four boxes you have to check. First, a <b>medical certificate</b>: at least a third-class medical from an FAA-designated doctor called an <b>AME</b> (Aviation Medical Examiner). Get this early so there are no surprises. Second, <b>flight time</b>: the training hours in the airplane that Part 61 requires, which we cover tomorrow.</p><p>Third, the <b>knowledge test</b> (often called \"the written,\" though it is on a computer): a multiple-choice exam at a testing center covering the ground school you are starting now. Fourth, the <b>checkride</b>, formally the practical test, given by an FAA examiner, usually a <b>DPE</b> (Designated Pilot Examiner). It has two parts: an <b>oral</b> portion where you talk through knowledge and scenarios on the ground, and a <b>flight</b> portion where you demonstrate maneuvers. Both are graded against the <b>ACS</b> (Airman Certification Standards), the FAA document that lists exactly what you must know and do.</p>", "words": 213, "figs": []}, {"id": "d2c1", "day": 2, "dayTitle": "The Hours: Part 61 Requirements and Eligibility", "title": "Who is eligible · The 40-hour minimum · Inside the 20 hours of dual", "body": "<h3>Who is eligible</h3><p>To earn a private pilot certificate (14 CFR 61.103) you must be at least <b>17 years old</b>, be able to read, speak, write, and understand <b>English</b>, and hold at least a <b>third-class medical certificate</b>. You also need a student pilot certificate before you solo, a passed knowledge test, logbook endorsements from your instructor, and the flight experience below. (You can solo as young as 16, but the certificate itself waits until 17.)</p><h3>The 40-hour minimum</h3><p>Part 61.109 requires at least <b>40 hours</b> of flight time for an airplane single-engine rating. Of that, at least <b>20 hours</b> must be <b>dual</b> (flight training with an instructor aboard, also called a CFI, Certified Flight Instructor) and at least <b>10 hours</b> must be <b>solo</b> (you alone in the airplane). The rest can be either.</p><h3>Inside the 20 hours of dual</h3><p>The dual time must include <b>3 hours of cross-country</b> training. A <b>cross-country</b> (XC) for this purpose means a flight with a landing at a point more than 50 nautical miles straight-line from where you started. It must include <b>3 hours of night</b> training, which itself includes one night cross-country of over <b>100 nautical miles</b> total distance and <b>10 takeoffs and landings to a full stop</b> at an airport, each involving a flight in the traffic pattern.</p><p>It also requires <b>3 hours of instrument training</b>, flying the airplane by reference to the instruments alone (usually wearing a view-limiting hood or foggles), so you could get yourself out of an accidental cloud. Finally, <b>3 hours of practical-test preparation</b> with an instructor within the <b>preceding 2 calendar months</b> before the checkride.</p>", "words": 255, "figs": []}, {"id": "d2c2", "day": 2, "dayTitle": "The Hours: Part 61 Requirements and Eligibility", "title": "Inside the 10 hours of solo · Reality versus minimums, and Part 141", "body": "<h3>Inside the 10 hours of solo</h3><p>The solo time must include <b>5 hours of solo cross-country</b>. One of those flights must be at least <b>150 nautical miles total</b>, with <b>full-stop landings at three points</b>, and one leg of at least <b>50 nautical miles</b> straight-line between the takeoff and landing points. You also need <b>3 takeoffs and landings to a full stop</b> at an airport with an operating <b>control tower</b>, each involving a flight in the traffic pattern.</p><h3>Reality versus minimums, and Part 141</h3><p>Forty hours is the legal floor, not the typical result. Most people finish nearer <b>60 to 75 hours</b>, because weather, scheduling gaps, and simply learning at a human pace all add time. That is normal and not a sign of anything wrong.</p><p>Flight schools certificated under <b>Part 141</b> run an FAA-approved, structured syllabus with stage checks, and the minimum there is <b>35 hours</b>. Part 61 training is more flexible in order and scheduling. Either path leads to the same certificate and the same checkride standards.</p>", "words": 156, "figs": []}, {"id": "d3c1", "day": 3, "dayTitle": "How a Wing Makes Lift", "title": "The wing bends the air · Camber and angle of attack · Speed and AOA trade off", "body": "<h3>The wing bends the air</h3><p>A wing works by turning the air that flows past it <b>downward</b>. Push air down, and by Newton's third law the air pushes the wing up. That upward reaction is <b>lift</b>. You may also hear about pressure: the air flowing over the curved top of the wing has lower pressure than the air underneath. That is the same event described differently. The pressure difference and the downward-turned air go together; they are not competing explanations.</p><h3>Camber and angle of attack</h3><p>Two things let a wing turn the air. <b>Camber</b> is the curve of the wing's cross-section (the <b>airfoil</b>). <b>Angle of attack</b>, or AOA, is the angle between the wing's <b>chord line</b> (an imaginary straight line from the front edge to the back edge of the wing) and the <b>relative wind</b> (the airflow the wing actually meets, which is opposite to the direction the wing is moving).</p><p>Notice that angle of attack is not where the nose is pointing compared with the horizon. That is <b>pitch attitude</b>. An airplane can be nose-high with a low AOA in a climb, or nose-level with a high AOA when slow and sinking. The wing only cares about the air it meets.</p><h3>Speed and AOA trade off</h3><p>Lift grows with airspeed and with angle of attack. Fly faster and the wing gets the same lift at a smaller AOA. Fly slower and you need more AOA to hold up the same weight. (Lift actually grows with the square of speed, so speed is powerful: double the speed gives about four times the lift at the same AOA.) Flaps add camber, which lets the wing make the lift it needs at a lower speed.</p>", "words": 268, "figs": [{"day": 3, "sec": 0, "num": "3", "src": "img/fig3-lift.svg", "caption": "The wing turns the passing air downward; the reaction is lift. Angle of attack is measured between the chord line and the relative wind.", "w": 480.0, "h": 290.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d3c2", "day": 3, "dayTitle": "How a Wing Makes Lift", "title": "The critical angle and the stall", "body": "<p>This only works up to a point. At the <b>critical angle of attack</b>, roughly <b>16 to 18 degrees on a typical light trainer</b> (an approximation; it varies by wing), the air can no longer follow the curved top of the wing. It separates, turns into a turbulent mess, and lift drops sharply. That is a <b>stall</b>. It has nothing to do with the engine stopping.</p><p>Here is the key line to memorize: <b>a wing can stall at any airspeed and any attitude if the critical angle of attack is exceeded.</b> The published stall speed is just the speed at which that happens in one specific condition (wings level, a certain weight, 1 G). Pull hard in a steep turn and you can stall well above it. The only cure is to reduce the angle of attack.</p>", "words": 136, "figs": []}, {"id": "d4c1", "day": 4, "dayTitle": "The Four Forces", "title": "Meet the forces · Two kinds of drag", "body": "<h3>Meet the forces</h3><p><b>Lift</b> acts upward, perpendicular to the relative wind, and comes mostly from the wings. <b>Weight</b> acts straight down toward the center of the earth: the airplane, fuel, people, and bags. <b>Thrust</b> pulls the airplane forward, made by the engine and propeller. <b>Drag</b> resists motion through the air, pulling backward.</p><h3>Two kinds of drag</h3><p><b>Parasite drag</b> is the air resisting the airplane's shape: the landing gear, antennas, the friction of air on the skin. It grows as you go faster. <b>Induced drag</b> is the by-product of making lift; it is largest at high angle of attack, which means at slow speeds. Add them and you get a total drag curve that is high when very slow, high when very fast, and lowest in between. The speed with the least total drag is where the airplane glides farthest, which is why airplanes publish a best-glide speed.</p><h3>Balance in steady flight</h3><p>In <b>straight-and-level, unaccelerated flight</b> (constant speed, constant altitude, constant heading), <b>lift equals weight</b> and <b>thrust equals drag</b>. Nothing is speeding up, slowing down, climbing, or descending, so the forces must be balanced. Unaccelerated is the important word: balance means steady, not stopped.</p><h3>Power changes speed (at first)</h3><p>Add power in level flight and thrust exceeds drag, so the airplane accelerates. As it speeds up, parasite drag rises until drag again equals thrust, and you settle at a new, faster speed. Reduce power and the opposite happens. To hold altitude while speed changes, you also adjust pitch, because more speed makes more lift at the same angle of attack.</p><h3>Pitch trades speed for altitude</h3><p>Raise the nose without adding power and you start climbing, but some of the weight now acts backward along the flight path, like a car rolling up a hill. The airplane slows. You traded speed for altitude. To climb and keep your speed, you must add power. Lower the nose without reducing power and you descend and speed up. A handy pilot shorthand: <b>pitch plus power equals performance</b>. Set an attitude and a power setting, and the airplane will settle into a predictable speed and climb or descent.</p>", "words": 331, "figs": [{"day": 4, "sec": 0, "num": "4", "src": "img/fig4-forces.svg", "caption": "The four forces. In straight-and-level, unaccelerated flight, lift equals weight and thrust equals drag.", "w": 480.0, "h": 300.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d5c1", "day": 5, "dayTitle": "Turns and Load Factor", "title": "What actually turns the airplane · Why you need back pressure · Load factor", "body": "<h3>What actually turns the airplane</h3><p>The rudder does not turn the airplane. Banking does. When you roll into a bank with the ailerons, the lift from the wings tilts with it. Part of that lift still points up, holding the airplane in the sky; the other part points sideways, toward the inside of the turn. That sideways part, the <b>horizontal component of lift</b>, pulls the airplane around the turn.</p><h3>Why you need back pressure</h3><p>Because some lift now points sideways, there is less pointing up. If you do nothing, the vertical part is smaller than weight and you start to descend. To hold altitude, you add a little back pressure, raising the angle of attack and making more total lift. In a steep turn you may also add power, since more AOA means more induced drag.</p><h3>Load factor</h3><p><b>Load factor</b> is the total lift the wings are making divided by the airplane's weight, measured in <b>G</b>. Straight and level is 1 G. In a level, coordinated turn the load factor depends only on bank angle. At <b>30 degrees</b> it is about <b>1.15 G</b>. At <b>45 degrees</b> about <b>1.4 G</b>. At <b>60 degrees</b> it is <b>2 G</b>: you and the wings feel twice your normal weight. Above 60 it climbs very quickly.</p><p>Airplanes are built to handle limited loads. A <b>normal category</b> airplane is designed for <b>+3.8 G and -1.52 G</b>. A <b>utility category</b> airplane is designed for <b>+4.4 G and -1.76 G</b>. Many trainers are certified in both, with utility limits only at lower weights. Exceeding those limits can bend or break the structure.</p>", "words": 252, "figs": [{"day": 5, "sec": 2, "num": "5", "src": "img/fig5-turn.svg", "caption": "Left: banking tilts lift, splitting it into a vertical part and a horizontal part that turns you. Right: load factor in a level turn depends only on bank angle.", "w": 480.0, "h": 270.0, "pos": 2}]}, {"id": "d5c2", "day": 5, "dayTitle": "Turns and Load Factor", "title": "Load factor raises stall speed · Coordination", "body": "<h3>Load factor raises stall speed</h3><p>More G means the wing must carry more load, so it needs more angle of attack at any speed, and it reaches the critical angle sooner. Stall speed rises with the <b>square root of the load factor</b>. At 2 G (a 60-degree bank), stall speed goes up by about <b>41 percent</b>. If your airplane stalls at 48 knots wings level, expect about 68 knots in a level 60-degree bank. This is why steep, slow turns near the ground, like an overshot turn onto final, are so dangerous.</p><h3>Coordination</h3><p>In a well-flown turn, the airplane's nose points along the flight path instead of skidding sideways or slipping in. The small <b>ball</b> in the turn coordinator (a glass tube with a ball, like a carpenter's level) shows this. If the ball is off center, \"step on the ball\": press the rudder pedal on the side the ball has moved to. Coordination matters most near stall speed, as you will see tomorrow.</p>", "words": 158, "figs": []}, {"id": "d6c1", "day": 6, "dayTitle": "Stalls and Spins", "title": "Warning signs · Recovery: reduce the angle of attack", "body": "<h3>Warning signs</h3><p>Airplanes warn you before they stall. You will notice <b>mushy, less effective controls</b> as the airflow slows, a <b>high nose attitude</b> for the power you have, the airspeed low and falling, the <b>stall warning horn</b> or light (it is triggered by a sensor on the wing's leading edge that detects the changing airflow at high AOA), and often a <b>buffet</b>, a shaking as disturbed air from the wing hits the tail. Trust the warnings; they arrive a few knots before the stall itself.</p><h3>Recovery: reduce the angle of attack</h3><p>The FAA's recovery steps, simplified, are: first, <b>reduce the angle of attack</b> by easing the control forward (pitch nose-down until the stall warning stops). This is the one step that actually fixes the stall. Second, <b>level the wings</b> with coordinated aileron and rudder. Third, <b>add power</b> as needed (full power for most practice stalls). Then return to the desired flight path, avoiding a second stall by not pulling back too hard as you recover.</p><p>Why level the wings after unloading? Because at high AOA, using aileron alone can make one wing stall deeper than the other. Unload first, then roll.</p><h3>The kinds of stalls you will practice</h3><p>A <b>power-off stall</b> mimics the approach and landing: power at idle, configured for landing, slowly raising the nose. A <b>power-on stall</b> mimics takeoff and departure climb: climb power, steadily increasing pitch. An <b>accelerated stall</b> happens above normal stall speed because of load factor, like pulling hard in a steep turn. And a <b>cross-controlled stall</b> happens when aileron and rudder are pushed in opposite directions, the classic skid in the base-to-final turn. The private pilot ACS asks you to demonstrate power-on and power-off stalls and to know about the others.</p>", "words": 272, "figs": [{"day": 6, "sec": 0, "num": "6", "src": "img/fig6-stall.svg", "caption": "Raising the angle of attack adds lift until the critical angle, where the airflow separates from the top of the wing and the wing stalls.", "w": 480.0, "h": 220.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d6c2", "day": 6, "dayTitle": "Stalls and Spins", "title": "What a spin is", "body": "<p>A <b>spin</b> is an aggravated stall in which one wing is more stalled than the other, so the airplane autorotates, descending in a corkscrew. It takes <b>two ingredients: a stall plus yaw</b> (the nose swinging sideways). That is why coordination matters near the stall: uncoordinated flight supplies the yaw. No stall, no spin.</p><p>The common recovery memory aid is <b>PARE</b>: <b>P</b>ower to idle, <b>A</b>ilerons neutral, <b>R</b>udder full opposite the direction of rotation, <b>E</b>levator briskly forward to break the stall. Once rotation stops, neutralize the rudder and smoothly recover from the dive. Always follow your airplane's POH (Pilot's Operating Handbook, the airplane's official manual), because the exact spin procedure belongs to the manufacturer. Spin training is not required for a private pilot, but spin awareness is.</p>", "words": 125, "figs": []}, {"id": "d7c1", "day": 7, "dayTitle": "Axes, Stability, Trim, and Left-Turning Tendencies", "title": "Three axes, three controls · Stability", "body": "<h3>Three axes, three controls</h3><p>An airplane rotates around three imaginary lines, all passing through its <b>center of gravity</b> (CG, the balance point). The <b>longitudinal axis</b> runs nose to tail; rotation around it is <b>roll</b>, controlled by the <b>ailerons</b> (hinged surfaces on the outer back edge of each wing). The <b>lateral axis</b> runs wingtip to wingtip; rotation around it is <b>pitch</b>, controlled by the <b>elevator</b> on the horizontal tail. The <b>vertical axis</b> runs top to bottom; rotation around it is <b>yaw</b>, controlled by the <b>rudder</b> on the vertical tail, moved with your feet.</p><p>When you roll into a turn, the aileron that goes down raises that wing's lift, and also its induced drag. That extra drag pulls the nose briefly away from the turn. This is <b>adverse yaw</b>, and the fix is a touch of rudder in the direction of the turn. That is what coordination means.</p><h3>Stability</h3><p><b>Static stability</b> is the airplane's initial tendency after being disturbed. <b>Positive</b> static stability means it tends to return to where it was, <b>neutral</b> means it stays in the new position, and <b>negative</b> means it keeps diverging. <b>Dynamic stability</b> is what happens over time: do the oscillations die out (positive), continue (neutral), or grow (negative)? Trainers are designed to be positively stable, which is why they are forgiving.</p><p>In pitch, a trainer's CG sits ahead of the wing's center of lift, so the nose naturally wants to drop; the horizontal tail pushes slightly down to balance it, like a seesaw. In roll, <b>dihedral</b> (wings angled slightly upward from root to tip) helps the airplane level itself. In yaw, the vertical fin works like a weathervane, keeping the nose pointed into the relative wind.</p>", "words": 274, "figs": [{"day": 7, "sec": 0, "num": "7", "src": "img/fig7-axes.svg", "caption": "Top view. The three axes all pass through the center of gravity; each control moves the airplane around one axis.", "w": 480.0, "h": 340.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d7c2", "day": 7, "dayTitle": "Axes, Stability, Trim, and Left-Turning Tendencies", "title": "Where the CG sits matters · Trim · Left-turning tendencies", "body": "<h3>Where the CG sits matters</h3><p>A <b>forward CG</b> makes the airplane more stable but heavier in pitch: the tail must push down harder, so stall speed is a bit higher, cruise a bit slower, and the flare for landing can run out of elevator. An <b>aft CG</b> makes the airplane less stable, with lower stall speed and slightly faster cruise, but it can make stall and spin recovery difficult or impossible if it is behind the limit. This is why weight and balance (Day 11) is a safety issue, not a formality.</p><h3>Trim</h3><p>A <b>trim tab</b> is a small adjustable surface, usually on the elevator, that holds the control in place so you do not have to keep pushing or pulling. You set the attitude you want with the yoke, then trim away the pressure. A well-trimmed airplane nearly flies itself, freeing you to look outside and think.</p><h3>Left-turning tendencies</h3><p>In most American trainers the propeller turns clockwise as seen from the pilot's seat. That creates four effects that all pull the nose left, strongest at <b>high power, low airspeed, and high angle of attack</b>, which describes takeoff and climb.</p><p><b>Torque</b>: the engine spins the propeller one way, so the airplane tries to roll the other way, to the left (Newton's third law again). <b>Spiraling slipstream</b>: the air thrown back by the propeller corkscrews around the fuselage and hits the left side of the vertical tail, yawing the nose left. <b>P-factor</b>: at high angle of attack, the propeller blade moving down on the right side takes a bigger bite of air than the rising blade on the left, so the right side makes more thrust, yawing the nose left. <b>Gyroscopic precession</b>: a spinning propeller is a gyroscope, and a force applied to it acts 90 degrees later in the direction of rotation; it matters mostly in tailwheel airplanes when the tail comes up on takeoff. The cure for all four: <b>right rudder</b>.</p>", "words": 314, "figs": []}, {"id": "d8c1", "day": 8, "dayTitle": "Airspeeds and V-Speeds", "title": "Four kinds of speed · V-speeds", "body": "<h3>Four kinds of speed</h3><p><b>Indicated airspeed</b> (IAS) is what the needle shows. It is what the wing \"feels,\" which is why stall and climb speeds are given as indicated. <b>Calibrated airspeed</b> (CAS) is IAS corrected for small instrument and installation errors; the POH has a table, and the difference is usually small except at slow speeds. <b>True airspeed</b> (TAS) is your actual speed through the air mass, which is CAS corrected for altitude and temperature. Because air is thinner up high, TAS is greater than IAS at altitude, roughly 2 percent more per 1,000 feet as a rule of thumb. <b>Groundspeed</b> (GS) is TAS adjusted for wind: your speed over the ground, which is what matters for fuel and arrival time.</p><h3>V-speeds</h3><p>A <b>V-speed</b> is a standard reference speed (the V stands for velocity). The key ones for your trainer: <b>V<sub>S0</sub></b>, stall speed in the landing configuration (flaps down). <b>V<sub>S1</sub></b>, stall speed in a specified configuration, usually clean (flaps up). <b>V<sub>R</sub></b>, rotation speed, when you lift the nosewheel on takeoff. <b>V<sub>X</sub></b>, best angle of climb: the most altitude in the shortest <i>distance</i>, for clearing obstacles. <b>V<sub>Y</sub></b>, best rate of climb: the most altitude in the shortest <i>time</i>, your normal climb.</p><p><b>V<sub>FE</sub></b> is the maximum flap-extended speed. <b>V<sub>NO</sub></b> is the maximum structural cruising speed; do not exceed it except in smooth air. <b>V<sub>NE</sub></b> is the never-exceed speed. <b>V<sub>A</sub></b>, design maneuvering speed, is the speed at or below which the airplane should stall before a single full control deflection or a strong gust can overstress it. Slow to V<sub>A</sub> or below in turbulence. V<sub>A</sub> <i>decreases</i> as weight decreases, because a lighter airplane is more easily accelerated by a gust. Best glide speed (often written V<sub>G</sub>) gives the farthest glide with the engine out.</p>", "words": 286, "figs": []}, {"id": "d8c2", "day": 8, "dayTitle": "Airspeeds and V-Speeds", "title": "The color arcs · Real numbers from one real airplane", "body": "<h3>The color arcs</h3><p>The airspeed indicator is color-coded. The <b>white arc</b> is the flap operating range, from V<sub>S0</sub> at the bottom to V<sub>FE</sub> at the top. The <b>green arc</b> is the normal operating range, from V<sub>S1</sub> to V<sub>NO</sub>. The <b>yellow arc</b> is the caution range, from V<sub>NO</sub> to V<sub>NE</sub>: smooth air only. The <b>red line</b> is V<sub>NE</sub>. V<sub>A</sub>, V<sub>X</sub>, and V<sub>Y</sub> are <i>not</i> marked; you learn them from the POH and placards.</p><h3>Real numbers from one real airplane</h3><p>As an example, the Cessna 172S POH lists (in knots indicated): V<sub>S0</sub> 40, V<sub>S1</sub> 48, V<sub>R</sub> 55, V<sub>X</sub> 62, V<sub>Y</sub> 74, best glide 68, V<sub>FE</sub> 85 with flaps beyond 10 degrees (110 at 10 degrees), V<sub>NO</sub> 129, V<sub>NE</sub> 163, and V<sub>A</sub> 105 at 2,550 pounds, dropping to 90 at 1,900 pounds. Other models and even other 172 years differ. <b>Your airplane's POH is the authority</b>; memorize its numbers, not these.</p>", "words": 139, "figs": []}, {"id": "d9c1", "day": 9, "dayTitle": "The Atmosphere and Density Altitude", "title": "The standard atmosphere · Pressure altitude", "body": "<h3>The standard atmosphere</h3><p>To compare performance on different days, engineers use a reference called the <b>International Standard Atmosphere</b>. At sea level it is <b>15 degrees Celsius</b> (59 °F) and <b>29.92 inches of mercury</b> (1013.2 hectopascals) of pressure. Temperature drops about <b>2 °C per 1,000 feet</b> of altitude (the standard <b>lapse rate</b>), and in the lower atmosphere pressure drops about <b>1 inch of mercury per 1,000 feet</b>. Real days are rarely standard, and that difference is what we care about.</p><h3>Pressure altitude</h3><p><b>Pressure altitude</b> is the altitude your altimeter shows when its setting window is set to 29.92. It is your height in the standard atmosphere based on pressure alone. On the ground you can compute it: <b>pressure altitude = field elevation + (29.92 − current altimeter setting) × 1,000</b>. Low pressure means a higher pressure altitude. Performance charts start with pressure altitude.</p><h3>Density altitude</h3><p><b>Density altitude</b> is pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature. It is the altitude in the standard atmosphere where the air would be as thin as it is right now. Think of it as \"the altitude the airplane thinks it is at.\" Air gets thinner, and density altitude goes up, with <b>higher elevation, higher temperature, and higher humidity</b> (water vapor is actually lighter than the dry air it displaces). Pilots say <b>high, hot, and humid</b> is bad.</p><p>A handy rule of thumb: density altitude rises about <b>120 feet for every 1 °C</b> the temperature is above standard for that altitude. Your E6B flight computer, an app, or the POH charts do it precisely.</p><h3>Why thin air hurts</h3><p>Thin air hurts three ways at once. The <b>wing</b> makes less lift at a given true airspeed, so you need a higher true airspeed (and longer runway) to fly. The <b>engine</b> makes less power because each breath of air holds less oxygen (normally aspirated engines lose power steadily with altitude). And the <b>propeller</b>, which is just a rotating wing, makes less thrust. The result: longer takeoff roll, slower climb, and higher groundspeed on landing. The indicated airspeeds you use for rotation and approach stay the same; the airplane simply has to go faster over the ground to reach them.</p>", "words": 348, "figs": [{"day": 9, "sec": 2, "num": "9", "src": "img/fig9-da.svg", "caption": "High, hot, and humid air is thinner, which raises density altitude and reduces performance.", "w": 480.0, "h": 290.0, "pos": 2}]}, {"id": "d10c1", "day": 10, "dayTitle": "Performance Charts, Crosswinds, and Ground Effect", "title": "What the charts tell you · Wind and surface corrections", "body": "<h3>What the charts tell you</h3><p>The POH performance section has charts or tables for takeoff distance, landing distance, climb rate, cruise speed and fuel burn, and range. Takeoff and landing charts usually give two numbers: the <b>ground roll</b> (the distance on the runway) and the <b>total distance to clear a 50-foot obstacle</b>. The second one is the one that keeps you out of the trees.</p><p>You enter a chart with <b>pressure altitude</b> and <b>temperature</b> (together they capture density altitude), plus weight, then apply corrections for wind and runway surface. When your conditions fall between rows, you <b>interpolate</b>: pick the value proportionally between the two closest entries. When in doubt, round to the worse number.</p><h3>Wind and surface corrections</h3><p>A <b>headwind</b> shortens takeoff and landing distances because you start with some airspeed before you move. A <b>tailwind</b> lengthens them dramatically. Grass, soft, or wet surfaces lengthen them too, and an uphill slope lengthens takeoff. The chart notes tell you how much. For example, Cessna 172 charts say to decrease distances 10 percent for each 9 knots of headwind, increase them 10 percent for each 2 knots of tailwind, and, for a dry grass runway, increase distances by 15 percent of the ground-roll figure. Note how much more a tailwind costs than a headwind gives.</p><p>Remember that charts were made by test pilots in a new airplane. Many instructors teach adding a personal safety margin, such as 50 percent, on top of the chart number.</p>", "words": 234, "figs": []}, {"id": "d10c2", "day": 10, "dayTitle": "Performance Charts, Crosswinds, and Ground Effect", "title": "Crosswind component · Ground effect", "body": "<h3>Crosswind component</h3><p>Runways rarely line up perfectly with the wind. The part of the wind blowing straight across the runway is the <b>crosswind component</b>. The POH has a chart, but a quick estimate works well: if the wind is <b>30 degrees</b> off the runway, crosswind is about <b>half</b> the wind speed; <b>45 degrees</b>, about <b>70 percent</b>; <b>60 degrees</b> or more, about <b>90 percent</b> or nearly all of it. The POH also lists a <b>maximum demonstrated crosswind component</b> (15 knots for many 172s). That is what a test pilot showed was manageable, not a structural limit, and your personal limit as a student should be much lower.</p><h3>Ground effect</h3><p>When the wing is within about one <b>wingspan</b> of the ground, the ground interferes with the wingtip vortices and the downward-flowing air. That reduces <b>induced drag</b> and makes the wing a bit more efficient. This is <b>ground effect</b>.</p><p>On landing, if you arrive too fast, ground effect makes the airplane <b>float</b> down the runway. On takeoff, the airplane may lift off at a speed too slow to climb once it rises out of ground effect, and then settle back. Soft-field technique deliberately uses ground effect: lift off early, then accelerate level just above the runway to V<sub>X</sub> or V<sub>Y</sub> before climbing.</p>", "words": 205, "figs": []}, {"id": "d11c1", "day": 11, "dayTitle": "Weight and Balance", "title": "Why it matters · The vocabulary · The method · Fuel burn moves the CG", "body": "<h3>Why it matters</h3><p>An overweight airplane needs a longer takeoff, climbs poorly, stalls at a higher speed, and puts more stress on the structure. A CG outside the limits changes how the airplane handles, as you saw on Day 7: too far forward and you may not be able to flare; too far aft and stall or spin recovery may be impossible. Both checks are required for every flight with any meaningful load, and examiners love to ask about them.</p><h3>The vocabulary</h3><p>The <b>datum</b> is an imaginary vertical reference line chosen by the manufacturer, often the front of the firewall (the wall between the engine and the cabin). The <b>arm</b> is the horizontal distance, in inches, from the datum to an item. The <b>moment</b> is weight times arm, in pound-inches. The <b>center of gravity</b> is the total moment divided by the total weight. <b>Empty weight</b> comes from the airplane's weight and balance record (it includes unusable fuel and, in modern airplanes, full oil). <b>Useful load</b> is maximum weight minus empty weight: everything you can add in people, bags, and fuel.</p><p>Useful weights to know: aviation gasoline weighs <b>6 pounds per gallon</b>; oil about <b>7.5 pounds per gallon</b>.</p><h3>The method</h3><p>Make a table with a line for each item: empty airplane, front seats, rear seats, fuel, baggage. For each, write weight and arm, then multiply to get moment. Add up the weights and the moments. Divide total moment by total weight to get the CG. Then check two things: is total weight at or below maximum, and does the CG fall inside the envelope (the allowed range, shown as a graph or table in the POH)? Many POHs use a moment-index graph to save arithmetic, but the idea is identical.</p><h3>Fuel burn moves the CG</h3><p>As you burn fuel, weight goes down and the CG moves <i>away</i> from the fuel tanks. If the tanks sit behind the CG, as in today's example, the CG moves forward as fuel burns. Always check that you are within limits at <b>takeoff and at landing</b>. Also remember that if you have to shift weight, moving something toward the back moves the CG aft, and the reverse.</p>", "words": 349, "figs": [{"day": 11, "sec": 2, "num": "11", "src": "img/fig11-wb.svg", "caption": "Arm is measured from the datum. The loaded CG must fall between the forward and aft limits (numbers from today's hypothetical example).", "w": 480.0, "h": 300.0, "pos": 2}]}, {"id": "d12c1", "day": 12, "dayTitle": "The Engine and Propeller", "title": "A simple, tough engine · Ignition: two magnetos", "body": "<h3>A simple, tough engine</h3><p>Most trainers use a <b>reciprocating</b> (piston) engine with four or six horizontally opposed cylinders, air-cooled by air entering the front of the cowling. Each cylinder follows the <b>four-stroke cycle</b>: <b>intake</b> (the piston moves down, drawing in fuel and air), <b>compression</b> (the piston moves up, squeezing the mixture), <b>power</b> (the spark plugs fire, the burning mixture forces the piston down), and <b>exhaust</b> (the piston moves up, pushing out the burned gases). The pistons turn a crankshaft that spins the propeller directly.</p><h3>Ignition: two magnetos</h3><p>The spark comes from <b>magnetos</b>, small engine-driven generators that make their own electricity. There are two, each feeding its own spark plug in every cylinder, so there are two plugs per cylinder. This gives redundancy and a more complete burn. Because magnetos do not need the battery, <b>the engine keeps running even if the airplane's electrical system fails</b>. During the pre-takeoff <b>run-up</b> you check each magneto separately and look for a small, smooth RPM drop. If a magneto's ground wire (the P-lead) breaks, that magneto stays \"hot\" even with the key off, so always treat a propeller as live.</p>", "words": 179, "figs": []}, {"id": "d12c2", "day": 12, "dayTitle": "The Engine and Propeller", "title": "Fuel and air: the mixture and carburetor ice · Detonation and preignition", "body": "<h3>Fuel and air: the mixture and carburetor ice</h3><p>The <b>mixture</b> control (usually red) sets the ratio of fuel to air. As you climb, the air thins but the fuel flow does not, so the mixture gets too rich; you <b>lean</b> it to restore the right ratio, following the POH. Too rich wastes fuel and can foul the plugs; too lean runs hot and can damage the engine. Pulling the mixture all the way to idle cutoff is how you shut the engine down.</p><p>Many trainers have a <b>carburetor</b>, which mixes fuel and air in a narrow throat called a venturi. Air speeds up and its pressure drops there, which cools it, and evaporating fuel cools it further, so ice can form even on a warm day. The FAA notes carburetor ice is most likely when the temperature is below about 70 °F (21 °C) and relative humidity is above about 80 percent, but it can form in temperatures as high as 100 °F with humidity as low as 50 percent. In a fixed-pitch propeller airplane the first sign is a <b>drop in RPM</b>, then roughness. Applying <b>carburetor heat</b> sends warm air into the carburetor; RPM drops a little more at first (the warm air is less dense, enriching the mixture), then rises as the ice melts. Fuel-injected engines do not have carburetor ice.</p><h3>Detonation and preignition</h3><p><b>Detonation</b> is the fuel-air charge exploding suddenly instead of burning smoothly. It is caused by excessive heat and pressure in the cylinder: using a lower fuel grade than specified, too lean a mixture, or high power with high engine temperatures. Remedies: richen the mixture, reduce power, and increase airspeed for cooling. <b>Preignition</b> is the mixture igniting too early from a hot spot, such as a glowing carbon deposit. Both can damage an engine quickly. High oil temperature is the main cockpit clue to overheating.</p>", "words": 299, "figs": []}, {"id": "d12c3", "day": 12, "dayTitle": "The Engine and Propeller", "title": "Propeller and oil", "body": "<p>A <b>fixed-pitch propeller</b> has blades set at one angle; the <b>tachometer</b> (RPM gauge) is your power indicator, controlled by the throttle. The blades are twisted, with a larger angle near the hub and a smaller angle at the tip, because the tip travels much faster and needs less angle to produce even thrust along the blade. More advanced airplanes have a <b>constant-speed propeller</b>, which needs a complex endorsement.</p><p>Engine oil <b>lubricates, cools, seals, and cleans</b>. After start, oil pressure should rise into the green within the time your POH specifies (often about 30 seconds); if it does not, shut down. Check oil quantity on every preflight.</p>", "words": 106, "figs": []}, {"id": "d13c1", "day": 13, "dayTitle": "Fuel, Electrical, and Vacuum Systems", "title": "The fuel system · Sumping and fuel grades", "body": "<h3>The fuel system</h3><p>In a high-wing trainer like a Cessna 172, fuel flows from the wing tanks to the engine mostly by <b>gravity</b>. Low-wing airplanes, like a Piper Cherokee, need an <b>engine-driven fuel pump</b> backed up by an <b>electric boost pump</b>. Along the way are a <b>fuel selector</b> (left, right, both, or off, depending on the airplane), a <b>strainer</b> that catches water and debris, a <b>primer</b> that squirts a little fuel into the cylinders for starting, and <b>vents</b> that let air into the tanks as fuel leaves. A blocked vent can starve the engine, so check them on preflight.</p><p>Not all fuel in the tanks is <b>usable</b>; a small amount is <b>unusable</b> and cannot reach the engine in all attitudes. Plan using only usable fuel.</p><h3>Sumping and fuel grades</h3><p>Before the first flight of the day and after each refueling, you <b>sump</b> the tanks: drain a little fuel from each low point into a clear cup. Water is heavier than fuel, so it sinks and shows up as a bubble or a clear blob at the bottom. Keep draining until it is gone. You are also checking for sediment and for the right color.</p><p>Most trainers burn <b>100LL</b> (100 octane, low lead) aviation gasoline, dyed <b>blue</b>. Jet fuel (Jet A) is clear to straw-colored and smells like kerosene; putting it in a piston engine can cause catastrophic failure. If the proper grade is unavailable, you may use the next <b>higher</b> grade if the airplane is approved for it, never a lower one, which risks detonation.</p>", "words": 248, "figs": []}, {"id": "d13c2", "day": 13, "dayTitle": "Fuel, Electrical, and Vacuum Systems", "title": "The electrical system · The vacuum system and cabin heat", "body": "<h3>The electrical system</h3><p>Trainers have a 14- or 28-volt direct current system. An engine-driven <b>alternator</b> powers everything in flight and recharges the <b>battery</b>, which starts the engine and serves as backup. The <b>master switch</b> is often split into two halves: BAT (battery) and ALT (alternator). An <b>ammeter</b> or <b>loadmeter</b> shows whether the battery is charging or discharging, and <b>circuit breakers</b> or fuses protect each circuit from overload. Many airplanes also have an <b>avionics master</b> to protect radios during engine start.</p><p>If the alternator fails, you will typically see a low-voltage warning light and a discharge indication. The battery will run things for only a limited time, so turn off nonessential equipment and land as soon as practical. The engine keeps running regardless, thanks to the magnetos.</p><h3>The vacuum system and cabin heat</h3><p>In many traditional trainers an engine-driven <b>vacuum pump</b> sucks air through the <b>attitude indicator</b> and <b>heading indicator</b>, spinning their gyros. A <b>suction gauge</b> shows whether it is working, usually in a green range. If it fails, those two instruments slowly become unreliable, while the electrically powered turn coordinator keeps working. That split is deliberate redundancy.</p><p>One more system worth knowing: cabin heat in most trainers comes from air warmed around the exhaust pipes. A cracked exhaust can leak <b>carbon monoxide</b> into the cabin, which we will discuss on Day 40. Many pilots carry a CO detector.</p>", "words": 219, "figs": []}, {"id": "d14c1", "day": 14, "dayTitle": "Pitot-Static Instruments", "title": "Pitot and static · The altimeter and altimeter settings", "body": "<h3>Pitot and static</h3><p>The <b>pitot tube</b>, usually under a wing, faces into the wind and measures <b>ram air pressure</b>. The <b>static ports</b>, usually small holes on the side of the fuselage, measure the still, surrounding air pressure. The <b>airspeed indicator</b> uses both: it compares ram pressure to static pressure. The <b>altimeter</b> and the <b>vertical speed indicator</b> (VSI) use static pressure only. Many airplanes have <b>pitot heat</b> to keep ice out of the tube.</p><h3>The altimeter and altimeter settings</h3><p>Inside the altimeter is a stack of sealed, flexible wafers that expand as outside pressure drops with altitude, moving the needles. Because weather changes pressure, you dial the current local <b>altimeter setting</b> into the small <b>Kollsman window</b>. The rule (14 CFR 91.121): below 18,000 feet MSL, use the current setting of a station within 100 nautical miles along your route; at or above 18,000 feet, use 29.92.</p><p>Remember <b>\"high to low, look out below.\"</b> If you fly from high pressure into low pressure without resetting, the altimeter reads higher than you really are. The same is true flying into colder-than-standard air: the airplane is lower than indicated. Both errors put you closer to the ground than you think.</p><h3>Five kinds of altitude</h3><p><b>Indicated altitude</b> is what the altimeter reads when correctly set. <b>True altitude</b> is your actual height above mean sea level (MSL); charted elevations are true altitudes. <b>Absolute altitude</b> is height above the ground directly below you (AGL, above ground level). <b>Pressure altitude</b> is the reading with 29.92 set, and <b>density altitude</b> is pressure altitude corrected for temperature (Day 9).</p><h3>The VSI</h3><p>The VSI compares current static pressure with pressure trapped in a case that leaks slowly. The difference shows rate of climb or descent in feet per minute. It gives an immediate <b>trend</b> but the exact <b>rate</b> lags by several seconds, so do not chase the needle.</p>", "words": 293, "figs": [{"day": 14, "sec": 0, "num": "14", "src": "img/fig14-pitot.svg", "caption": "The pitot tube feeds only the airspeed indicator; the static port feeds the airspeed indicator, altimeter, and VSI.", "w": 480.0, "h": 300.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d14c2", "day": 14, "dayTitle": "Pitot-Static Instruments", "title": "When things get blocked", "body": "<p>If the <b>pitot tube's ram opening is blocked</b> (say, by ice) but its drain hole stays open, the airspeed drops to zero. If <b>both the ram opening and the drain hole are blocked</b>, the airspeed indicator acts like an altimeter, reading higher as you climb and lower as you descend, which can fool you badly. Only the airspeed indicator is affected by a pitot blockage.</p><p>If the <b>static ports are blocked</b>, all three instruments are affected: the altimeter freezes at the altitude where the block happened, the VSI goes to zero, and the airspeed reads <b>lower than actual in a climb</b> and <b>higher than actual in a descent</b>. Many airplanes have an <b>alternate static source</b> inside the cabin. Because cabin air pressure is slightly lower in an unpressurized airplane, using it makes the altimeter and airspeed read a little high and the VSI briefly show a climb.</p>", "words": 147, "figs": []}, {"id": "d15c1", "day": 15, "dayTitle": "Gyro Instruments and the Magnetic Compass", "title": "Two gyro principles · The three gyro instruments", "body": "<h3>Two gyro principles</h3><p>A spinning wheel has <b>rigidity in space</b>: it resists changes to the direction it points. That lets an instrument hold a stable reference as the airplane moves around it. A gyro also has <b>precession</b>: push on it and the reaction appears 90 degrees later in the direction of spin. Over time, friction causes unwanted precession, which is why some gyro instruments drift.</p><h3>The three gyro instruments</h3><p>The <b>attitude indicator</b> shows pitch and bank against an artificial horizon, with marks at 10, 20, 30, and 60 degrees of bank. It is the centerpiece of instrument flying. The <b>heading indicator</b> shows heading on a compass-like card without the magnetic compass's errors, but it drifts from precession, so you <b>realign it with the magnetic compass about every 15 minutes</b>, in straight-and-level, unaccelerated flight. In a classic trainer, both are usually vacuum-powered.</p><p>The <b>turn coordinator</b> shows rate of roll and rate of turn, with marks for a <b>standard-rate turn</b>: 3 degrees per second, which takes 2 minutes for a full circle. It is usually electric, so it still works if the vacuum pump fails. Its <b>inclinometer</b>, the ball, shows coordination. Note that the miniature airplane in a turn coordinator does not show bank angle directly, only rate of turn.</p>", "words": 201, "figs": []}, {"id": "d15c2", "day": 15, "dayTitle": "Gyro Instruments and the Magnetic Compass", "title": "The magnetic compass and its errors · Glass cockpits", "body": "<h3>The magnetic compass and its errors</h3><p>The compass is simple and needs no power, but it is imperfect. <b>Variation</b> is the angle between true north (the geographic pole) and magnetic north; it changes with location and is printed on charts as dashed magenta isogonic lines. <b>Deviation</b> is error from the airplane's own magnetic fields (engine, radios); a <b>compass correction card</b> near the compass lists it.</p><p>Then there are <b>magnetic dip</b> errors, caused by the earth's field pulling the compass card downward. On east or west headings in the northern hemisphere, <b>acceleration</b> makes the compass show a turn toward north and <b>deceleration</b> toward south: <b>ANDS</b>, Accelerate North, Decelerate South. In turns, when turning from or through north the compass <b>lags</b>, and through south it <b>leads</b>: <b>UNOS</b>, Undershoot North, Overshoot South, meaning roll out before the compass reaches a north heading and after it passes a south heading. The compass also bounces in turbulence. Read it only in steady, level flight.</p><h3>Glass cockpits</h3><p>Many trainers now have a <b>glass cockpit</b>: screens such as a <b>PFD</b> (primary flight display) that show attitude, airspeed, altitude, and heading together. They get attitude from an <b>AHRS</b> (attitude and heading reference system, solid-state sensors instead of spinning gyros) and air data from an <b>ADC</b> (air data computer, still fed by the pitot and static ports). The principles from today and yesterday still apply; failures usually show as a red X. Learn your system's backup instruments.</p>", "words": 230, "figs": []}, {"id": "d16c1", "day": 16, "dayTitle": "Gauges I: Airspeed Indicator, Altimeter, and Vertical Speed", "title": "How to read any round gauge", "body": "<p>Almost every gauge in a light airplane follows the same visual language. A <b>needle</b> moves over a <b>scale</b>. <b>Colored arcs</b> show ranges: <b>green</b> is normal operation, <b>yellow</b> is caution, <b>red</b> radial lines are limits you must not exceed, and <b>white</b> on the airspeed indicator is the flap range. Numbers on the face are often in units you multiply (RPM \"x100,\" vertical speed \"x1000\").</p><p>You do not read gauges like a book. In a good <b>scan</b> you glance, check that the needle is where it should be (often simply \"in the green\"), and move on. The skill is noticing when something is <i>not</i> where it belongs. The airplane-specific numbers in these four lessons are <b>typical trainer values</b> (mostly from a Cessna 172S) so the pictures look real. Your airplane's POH and the markings on its own gauges are the authority.</p>", "words": 139, "figs": []}, {"id": "d16c2", "day": 16, "dayTitle": "Gauges I: Airspeed Indicator, Altimeter, and Vertical Speed", "title": "Airspeed indicator", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> indicated airspeed (IAS), the airplane's speed through the air as sensed by pitot pressure minus static pressure. Most modern trainers read in knots; some older ones read in miles per hour with a smaller knots scale inside.</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> read the number under the needle. The colored markings are required on the instrument and tell you the key speeds. The bottom of the <b>white arc</b> is V<sub>SO</sub>, the power-off stall speed in the landing configuration; the top is V<sub>FE</sub>, the maximum speed with flaps extended. The bottom of the <b>green arc</b> is V<sub>S1</sub>, the stall speed in a specified configuration (usually flaps up); the top is V<sub>NO</sub>, maximum structural cruising speed. The <b>yellow arc</b> runs from V<sub>NO</sub> to V<sub>NE</sub> and is for smooth air only. The <b>red line</b> is V<sub>NE</sub>, never exceed. In a Cessna 172S these are typically 40, 85, 48, 129, and 163 knots. Important speeds such as V<sub>A</sub>, V<sub>X</sub>, V<sub>Y</sub>, and best glide are <b>not marked</b>; you learn them from the POH (Day 8).</p><p><b>Why you look at it:</b> airspeed is your margin above the stall and below structural limits. On final approach it is the gauge you watch most.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings:</b> with the pitot tube blocked and its drain open, the needle drops toward zero; with both blocked, it acts like an altimeter, rising as you climb and falling as you descend. A blocked static port makes it read low in a climb and high in a descent (Day 14). An airspeed that does not \"come alive\" on the takeoff roll is a reason to abort while you still have runway. Turn on pitot heat if you might meet visible moisture near freezing.</p>", "words": 282, "figs": [{"day": 16, "sec": 1, "num": "16a", "src": "img/fig16a-asi.svg", "caption": "Airspeed indicator. Markings are typical of a Cessna 172S, in knots indicated airspeed; your airplane's POH is the authority.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d16c3", "day": 16, "dayTitle": "Gauges I: Airspeed Indicator, Altimeter, and Vertical Speed", "title": "Altimeter", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> pressure altitude corrected by the setting you dial in, shown as feet above mean sea level when the setting is current.</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> a typical altimeter has three hands. The <b>long hand</b> shows hundreds of feet (one trip around is 1,000 feet). The <b>short, wide hand</b> shows thousands. The <b>thin hand with a triangle</b> at its tip shows tens of thousands. Read the short hand first, then the long hand: short hand between 3 and 4, long hand on 5, thin hand just past 0, is <b>3,500 feet</b>. Many altimeters show a <b>striped (crosshatched) flag</b> below 10,000 feet as a warning that you are low; it disappears as you climb above 10,000. The <b>Kollsman window</b> shows the altimeter setting in inches of mercury; turn the knob to set it.</p><p><b>Why you look at it:</b> to fly assigned and cruising altitudes, to fly the traffic pattern at the right height, and to stay above terrain and obstacles.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings and errors:</b> before takeoff, set the current altimeter setting and check that the altimeter reads close to field elevation; a big difference means a wrong setting or a bad instrument. Each 1 inch of setting error is about 1,000 feet of altitude error, and flying from high to low pressure or into cold air without resetting makes it read <b>higher</b> than you really are. A blocked static port freezes the altimeter at the altitude where the blockage occurred. Misreading the hands (for example, taking the long hand for thousands) is a classic error, so read the short hand first every time.</p>", "words": 264, "figs": [{"day": 16, "sec": 2, "num": "16b", "src": "img/fig16b-alt.svg", "caption": "Altimeter reading 3,500 feet with 30.12 set in the Kollsman window.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d16c4", "day": 16, "dayTitle": "Gauges I: Airspeed Indicator, Altimeter, and Vertical Speed", "title": "Vertical speed indicator", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> rate of climb or descent in feet per minute, from how fast static pressure is changing.</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> zero is usually at the 9 o'clock position. Needle above zero is climb, below is descent. The numbers are in thousands of feet per minute, so a needle at .5 on the upper scale is a 500-foot-per-minute climb. The <b>trend</b> (which way the needle starts to move) shows almost at once, but the exact <b>rate</b> lags by about 6 to 9 seconds.</p><p><b>Why you look at it:</b> to set a steady climb or descent rate (for example, 500 feet per minute down to pattern altitude), and as a quick cross-check of level flight. Do not chase the needle; set a pitch attitude and let the needle settle.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings:</b> on the ground it should read zero; if it does not, the reading is an offset to apply in flight (some have a zero-adjust screw). A blocked static port makes it read zero no matter what the airplane is doing. Rough air makes it swing; average the swings rather than chasing them.</p>", "words": 184, "figs": [{"day": 16, "sec": 3, "num": "16c", "src": "img/fig16c-vsi.svg", "caption": "Vertical speed indicator showing a 500-foot-per-minute climb.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d17c1", "day": 17, "dayTitle": "Gauges II: Attitude Indicator, Turn Coordinator, Heading Indicator, and Compass", "title": "Attitude indicator", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> the airplane's pitch and bank relative to the horizon. In many trainers its gyro is driven by the vacuum system; in others it is electric.</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> the <b>miniature airplane</b> is fixed to the case. Behind it, the <b>horizon card</b> (sky above, ground below) stays level with the real horizon. Pitch is the miniature airplane's position against the <b>pitch lines</b>, usually marked every 5 degrees. Bank is read at the top: a pointer on the moving card points to the fixed <b>bank scale</b>, marked at 10, 20, 30, 45 (often small triangles), and 60 degrees. In the picture the airplane is banked about 15 degrees right with the nose level. A knob lets you set the miniature airplane on the horizon line in level flight.</p><p><b>Why you look at it:</b> it is the center of the scan, the one instrument that shows attitude directly. When you cannot see the real horizon, it is the instrument you fly by.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings:</b> when the vacuum system fails, the gyro slowly winds down and the display tilts or drifts, often gradually enough to fool you. Electric versions may show an OFF flag. Small errors after steep turns or accelerations are normal and settle out. Always cross-check it against the turn coordinator, heading indicator, altimeter, and VSI: if the attitude indicator shows a turn but the turn coordinator and heading indicator do not, suspect the attitude indicator.</p>", "words": 238, "figs": [{"day": 17, "sec": 0, "num": "17a", "src": "img/fig17a-ai.svg", "caption": "Attitude indicator in a 15-degree right bank with the nose level.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d17c2", "day": 17, "dayTitle": "Gauges II: Attitude Indicator, Turn Coordinator, Heading Indicator, and Compass", "title": "Turn coordinator and its older cousin", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> the rate of roll and rate of turn, plus coordination. Its gyro is usually electric, which makes it a backup when the vacuum system fails.</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> the small airplane symbol banks as the airplane rolls and turns. With its wingtip on the upper marks, the wings are level and there is no turn. With a wingtip on the lower <b>L</b> or <b>R</b> mark, the airplane is in a <b>standard-rate turn</b>: 3 degrees per second, a full circle in 2 minutes (the \"2 MIN\" on the face). The face says \"no pitch information\" because the small airplane does <b>not</b> show pitch or bank angle. Below it, the <b>inclinometer</b> ball shows coordination: ball centered is coordinated; if it is off center, step on the ball (press the rudder on the side the ball has moved to).</p><p>The older <b>turn-and-slip indicator</b> does the same job with a vertical needle that deflects left or right to show the rate of turn, with the same ball below. Its gyro is mounted so it senses only yaw (turn), so it does not show roll rate the way a turn coordinator does.</p><p><b>Why and when it matters:</b> it is your coordination check in every turn and climb, and your bank backup if the attitude indicator fails. <b>Abnormal:</b> some show an OFF flag on electrical failure; a turn coordinator frozen level while the airplane is clearly turning has probably lost power.</p>", "words": 239, "figs": [{"day": 17, "sec": 1, "num": "17b", "src": "img/fig17b-tc.svg", "caption": "Turn coordinator in a coordinated, standard-rate right turn.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d17c3", "day": 17, "dayTitle": "Gauges II: Attitude Indicator, Turn Coordinator, Heading Indicator, and Compass", "title": "Heading indicator", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> heading, from a gyro that holds its direction in space. It is easier to read than the compass because it does not bounce or swing in turns, but it has no idea where north is until you tell it.</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> read the number under the <b>lubber line</b> at the top, in front of the fixed airplane symbol. The card shows numbers every 30 degrees with the last zero dropped (3 is 030, 12 is 120) and marks every 5 degrees. Many also have marks every 45 degrees on the case to help with pattern turns. The picture reads 060.</p><p><b>Why you look at it:</b> to hold headings and make accurate turns to new headings. Set it to the magnetic compass before takeoff, during straight, level, unaccelerated flight, using the knob.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings:</b> because of precession, it drifts slowly; recheck it against the compass about every 15 minutes. Drift much faster than usual, or a card that spins or tilts, can mean a failing gyro or low vacuum, so check the suction gauge (Day 19). Some airplanes have a magnetically slaved heading indicator or an electronic display that corrects itself; know which you have.</p>", "words": 199, "figs": [{"day": 17, "sec": 2, "num": "17c", "src": "img/fig17c-hi.svg", "caption": "Heading indicator showing a heading of 060.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d17c4", "day": 17, "dayTitle": "Gauges II: Attitude Indicator, Turn Coordinator, Heading Indicator, and Compass", "title": "Magnetic compass", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> heading relative to magnetic north, with no power needed. It is the backup for everything else, and required for VFR flight.</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> read the number under the <b>lubber line</b> in the window. The card seems to read backward: when you are heading north, the 3 (030) appears to the <b>left</b> of the N, even though you would turn right to reach it. So turn toward the heading you want, then read the compass once it settles. A <b>compass correction card</b> nearby lists the deviation for this airplane, for example \"For 30, steer 029.\"</p><p><b>Why you look at it:</b> to set the heading indicator, and to navigate if the gyros fail. <b>Abnormal readings and limits:</b> it is accurate only in straight, level, unaccelerated flight; acceleration and turning errors (ANDS, UNOS, Day 15) make it swing. Before flight, check that it is full of fluid, swings freely, and reads close to the known runway heading. Electrical equipment can add deviation, so some cards show values with radios on.</p>", "words": 172, "figs": [{"day": 17, "sec": 3, "num": "17d", "src": "img/fig17d-compass.svg", "caption": "Magnetic compass heading north, with an example compass correction card.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d18c1", "day": 18, "dayTitle": "Gauges III: Engine Instruments", "title": "Tachometer", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> engine speed in revolutions per minute (RPM). The face usually reads in hundreds (\"x100\").</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> read the needle against the scale; 24 means 2,400 RPM. A typical Cessna 172S shows a <b>green arc</b> from 2,100 to 2,700 RPM and a <b>red line</b> at 2,700. A small <b>hour meter</b> in the face records \"tach time,\" which is used for maintenance intervals and often for rental billing.</p><p><b>Why you look at it:</b> in an airplane with a <b>fixed-pitch propeller</b>, RPM is your power indication. You set cruise power by RPM, check the magnetos and carburetor heat by RPM drops during the run-up (Day 12), and watch it for overspeed in descents.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings:</b> an unexplained, gradual loss of RPM in a carbureted engine suggests carburetor ice. A rough engine with a fluctuating needle needs attention. Going past the red line, easy to do in a fast descent with power on, can damage the engine. A needle that drops to zero while the engine is clearly running points to a failed tachometer, not a stopped engine.</p>", "words": 179, "figs": [{"day": 18, "sec": 0, "num": "18a", "src": "img/fig18a-tach.svg", "caption": "Tachometer for a fixed-pitch propeller, with typical Cessna 172S markings.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d18c2", "day": 18, "dayTitle": "Gauges III: Engine Instruments", "title": "Manifold pressure gauge", "body": "<p><b>What it measures:</b> the absolute pressure in the engine's intake manifold, in inches of mercury. It appears in airplanes with a <b>constant-speed propeller</b>, where RPM alone no longer shows power.</p><p><b>How to read it:</b> read inches under the needle. With the engine stopped it reads outside air pressure, about 30 inches at sea level and less at higher fields. At idle it is low; at full throttle in a normally aspirated engine it is a little below outside pressure, and it falls about 1 inch per 1,000 feet as you climb. Green arcs and limits vary by engine, so the arc in the picture is only an example.</p><p><b>Why you look at it:</b> with a constant-speed propeller, the <b>throttle</b> sets manifold pressure and the <b>propeller control</b> sets RPM; the pair of numbers (for example, 23 inches and 2,300 RPM) is your power setting from the POH. A common habit to avoid high manifold pressure at low RPM: when adding power, increase RPM first, then manifold pressure; when reducing power, reduce manifold pressure first, then RPM.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings:</b> an unexpected drop can mean induction icing or a blocked air filter; failure to reach the expected value on takeoff is a reason to abort. Use the POH power chart for your airplane.</p>", "words": 210, "figs": [{"day": 18, "sec": 1, "num": "18b", "src": "img/fig18b-mp.svg", "caption": "Manifold pressure gauge, used with a constant-speed propeller. The green arc is an example only.", "w": 480.0, "h": 262.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d18c3", "day": 18, "dayTitle": "Gauges III: Engine Instruments", "title": "Oil pressure and oil temperature", "body": "<p><b>What they measure:</b> the pressure of the oil the pump delivers through the engine (pounds per square inch) and the oil's temperature (°F). They are often side by side or combined in one dual gauge.</p><p><b>How to read them:</b> typical Cessna 172S markings are an oil pressure <b>red line at 20 psi</b> (minimum, idle), <b>green from 50 to 90 psi</b>, and a <b>red line at 115 psi</b>; and an oil temperature <b>green from 100 to 245 °F</b> with a <b>red line at 245 °F</b>. Read them as a pair.</p><p><b>Why you look at them:</b> oil pressure is the first gauge to check after start. It should rise into the normal range within about 30 seconds (check your POH; cold weather can take longer). If it does not, shut the engine down. Oil temperature rises slowly over several minutes, so it is a longer-term trend gauge.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings:</b> low oil pressure with <b>rising oil temperature</b> is a classic sign of oil loss or an impending engine failure: reduce power and land as soon as possible, picking a landing spot. High oil temperature alone can come from low oil quantity, a long climb at low airspeed, or hot weather; increase airspeed, enrich the mixture, or reduce power. A sudden drop to zero pressure with normal temperature may be a gauge failure, but never assume so; treat it as real until you are on the ground.</p>", "words": 233, "figs": [{"day": 18, "sec": 2, "num": "18c", "src": "img/fig18c-oil.svg", "caption": "Oil temperature and oil pressure gauges with typical Cessna 172S markings.", "w": 480.0, "h": 250.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d18c4", "day": 18, "dayTitle": "Gauges III: Engine Instruments", "title": "Exhaust gas temperature and cylinder head temperature", "body": "<p><b>EGT</b> measures exhaust gas temperature just after it leaves a cylinder. Many simple EGT gauges have <b>no numbers</b>, only marks (often every 25 °F), and a movable <b>reference pointer</b>. That is fine, because what you use is the <b>change</b> in EGT as you lean. <b>How to use it:</b> at a cruise power the POH allows leaning, slowly pull the mixture back and watch EGT rise to a <b>peak</b>, set the reference pointer there, then enrich by the amount the POH specifies (or operate at peak if it allows). A rich mixture runs cooler; as you lean toward peak, EGT rises.</p><p><b>CHT</b> measures the temperature of a cylinder head (in some airplanes one cylinder, in others all of them on an engine monitor). Its red line (500 °F is a common limit for Lycoming engines) protects against detonation and overheating. <b>Why you look at it:</b> high CHT in a long climb or with a lean mixture means the engine needs more cooling: enrich, lower the nose for more airspeed, or reduce power. Very rapid power reductions in a fast descent can cool the cylinders too quickly (shock cooling), so reduce power gradually.</p><p><b>Abnormal readings:</b> one cylinder's EGT dropping sharply on an engine monitor can mean a fouled plug or ignition problem; a cylinder's EGT or CHT climbing far above the others can mean detonation or an intake leak. Many newer airplanes show EGT and CHT for every cylinder as bar graphs on an engine monitor or glass display; the ideas are the same.</p>", "words": 252, "figs": [{"day": 18, "sec": 3, "num": "18d", "src": "img/fig18d-egt.svg", "caption": "Exhaust gas temperature (left) and cylinder head temperature (right). Markings are examples.", "w": 480.0, "h": 250.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d19c1", "day": 19, "dayTitle": "Gauges IV: Fuel, Electrical, Vacuum, Clock, and Flaps", "title": "Fuel quantity, fuel flow, and fuel pressure", "body": "<p><b>Fuel quantity:</b> there is a gauge for <b>each tank</b> (91.205 requires one), often two needles in one case marked LEFT and RIGHT, reading from E (empty) to F (full) or in gallons. A red mark at E means only unusable fuel remains. <b>How to use them:</b> they are a cross-check, not your fuel plan. Certification only requires the gauges to read zero when just unusable fuel is left, so in between they can be quite inaccurate. Always <b>check the tanks visually</b> (or with a dipstick) before flight and plan fuel by time and burn rate (Day 35). Watch for a difference between tanks: an unexpected imbalance can mean a leak, a cap left off, or a selector problem.</p><p><b>Fuel flow</b> (gallons per hour) is common on fuel-injected engines and on engine monitors. It shows the burn rate directly, helps with leaning, and lets you check your planned fuel burn. <b>Fuel pressure</b> gauges appear on fuel-injected engines and on airplanes with engine-driven or electric fuel pumps, such as many low-wing trainers. A drop in fuel pressure or flow can mean a failing pump, a tank running dry, or vapor lock: turn on the electric boost pump if the airplane has one, switch to a tank with fuel, and follow the POH checklist.</p>", "words": 211, "figs": [{"day": 19, "sec": 0, "num": "19a", "src": "img/fig19a-fuel.svg", "caption": "Fuel quantity gauges for each tank, and a fuel flow gauge.", "w": 480.0, "h": 220.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d19c2", "day": 19, "dayTitle": "Gauges IV: Fuel, Electrical, Vacuum, Clock, and Flaps", "title": "Ammeter, loadmeter, and voltmeter", "body": "<p><b>Center-zero ammeter:</b> shows current flowing <b>into</b> the battery (charge, needle right of zero) or <b>out of</b> it (discharge, needle left). Right after start it shows a strong charge as the alternator refills the battery, then settles near zero or slightly positive. A steady <b>discharge</b> in flight means the alternator is not carrying the load and the battery is draining.</p><p><b>Loadmeter:</b> some airplanes have a meter that reads from zero upward, showing how much current the alternator is supplying. A reading of zero with the engine running means the alternator is not producing power. <b>Voltmeter:</b> shows system voltage. With the alternator working, a 14-volt system reads about 14 volts and a 28-volt system about 28; a reading at battery voltage or falling means the alternator is off line. Many airplanes also have a low-voltage warning light.</p><p><b>Why and what to do:</b> after an alternator failure the battery may last only a limited time. Turn off nonessential equipment, keep the radios you need, follow the POH checklist (which may include resetting the alternator circuit breaker or switch once), and land as soon as practical (Day 13). A very high voltage or a large, steady charge can mean a regulator failure; the POH procedure usually turns the alternator off.</p>", "words": 206, "figs": [{"day": 19, "sec": 1, "num": "19b", "src": "img/fig19b-elec.svg", "caption": "Center-zero ammeter and voltmeter in a typical 14-volt system.", "w": 480.0, "h": 230.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d19c3", "day": 19, "dayTitle": "Gauges IV: Fuel, Electrical, Vacuum, Clock, and Flaps", "title": "Suction (vacuum) gauge · Clock or timer, and the flap position indicator", "body": "<h3>Suction (vacuum) gauge</h3><p><b>What it measures:</b> the suction the engine-driven vacuum pump provides to spin the attitude and heading indicator gyros, in inches of mercury. A typical trainer's normal range is about 4.5 to 5.5 inches; check your POH.</p><p><b>How to use it:</b> check it during the run-up and include it in your scan, because a vacuum failure is quiet. <b>Abnormal readings:</b> a <b>low</b> reading means the gyros may spin too slowly and drift or tilt (yesterday's mini scenario); cross-check with the electric turn coordinator and the compass. A <b>high</b> reading can damage the gyros. Some airplanes add a low-vacuum warning light or a backup vacuum source.</p><h3>Clock or timer, and the flap position indicator</h3><p>A <b>panel clock</b> is required for IFR flight, but it is useful on every flight: timing legs for dead reckoning, checking when to switch tanks, and timing standard-rate turns. Many airplanes have a digital clock with a stopwatch, or use the timer on a GPS or glass display.</p><p>The <b>flap position indicator</b> shows where the flaps actually are. In a Cessna 172S, electric flaps have a switch with detents at 0, 10, 20, and 30 degrees and a scale beside it. Piper Cherokees use a manual lever between the seats with notches (typically 0, 10, 25, and 40 degrees), where the lever position is the indicator. <b>Why it matters:</b> confirm the setting matches what you selected, especially for takeoff and go-arounds, and never extend flaps above V<sub>FE</sub>. If the indicator and the flaps disagree, or one flap does not move, keep the flaps where they are and follow the POH.</p>", "words": 254, "figs": [{"day": 19, "sec": 2, "num": "19c", "src": "img/fig19c-suction.svg", "caption": "Suction (vacuum) gauge with a typical normal range.", "w": 480.0, "h": 250.0, "pos": 0}, {"day": 19, "sec": 3, "num": "19d", "src": "img/fig19d-clockflap.svg", "caption": "Panel clock and flap position indicator.", "w": 480.0, "h": 230.0, "pos": 1}]}, {"id": "d20c1", "day": 20, "dayTitle": "Airports: Runways, Signs, Lights, and the Pattern", "title": "Runways and markings · Signs", "body": "<h3>Runways and markings</h3><p>A runway's number is its <b>magnetic heading</b> rounded to the nearest 10 degrees, with the last zero dropped. Runway 36 points to about 360 degrees, north; the other end is runway 18. Parallel runways add L, C, or R. Runway markings are <b>white</b>; taxiway markings are <b>yellow</b>.</p><p>A <b>displaced threshold</b>, marked with white arrows leading to a white bar, is pavement you may use for <b>taxi, takeoff, and landing rollout</b> from the other direction, but not for touching down. Yellow <b>chevrons</b> mark a blast pad or overrun area not usable for normal taxi, takeoff, or landing. The <b>runway holding position marking</b> is four yellow lines across the taxiway, two solid and two dashed. Stop on the <b>solid</b> side unless cleared onto the runway. If you are on the dashed side (exiting the runway), you may cross to get clear.</p><h3>Signs</h3><p><b>Mandatory instruction signs</b> have <b>white letters on red</b>: runway holding positions (such as \"18-36\"), ILS critical areas, and \"no entry.\" Do not pass them without a clearance at a towered airport. <b>Location signs</b> are <b>yellow letters on black</b> and tell you which taxiway or runway you are on. <b>Direction signs</b> are <b>black letters on yellow</b> with arrows pointing to other taxiways. A <b>runway distance remaining</b> sign is a white number on black, in thousands of feet. Keep an airport diagram handy, and remember the Chart Supplement (the FAA directory of airports) has the details. Days 21 and 22 cover the markings and signs in detail, with drawings.</p>", "words": 246, "figs": []}, {"id": "d20c2", "day": 20, "dayTitle": "Airports: Runways, Signs, Lights, and the Pattern", "title": "Lights and beacons · The traffic pattern · Wake turbulence", "body": "<h3>Lights and beacons</h3><p>Runway edge lights are <b>white</b> (on instrument runways, the last 2,000 feet are yellow). Threshold lights are <b>green</b> as you approach and runway end lights <b>red</b>. Taxiway edge lights are <b>blue</b>, taxiway centerlines green. A rotating <b>airport beacon</b> flashing <b>white and green</b> marks a lighted land airport; if it is on in daytime in controlled airspace, the weather may be below basic VFR minimums.</p><p>Visual glide slope lights help on approach. A <b>PAPI</b> has four lights in a row: <b>two white and two red</b> means on glide path; more white is high, more red is low. A two-bar <b>VASI</b>: <b>\"red over white, you're all right.\"</b> At many nontowered fields, <b>pilot-controlled lighting</b> turns on when you click the mic on the published frequency: 7 times within 5 seconds for high intensity, 5 for medium, 3 for low.</p><h3>The traffic pattern</h3><p>The <b>traffic pattern</b> is a rectangle flown around the runway: <b>upwind</b> (parallel to the runway in the landing direction), <b>crosswind</b>, <b>downwind</b> (parallel, opposite direction), <b>base</b>, and <b>final</b>. Turns are to the <b>left</b> unless the airport specifies right traffic. The FAA's recommended pattern altitude for most piston airplanes is <b>1,000 feet AGL</b>, unless the airport publishes something else. At a nontowered airport the recommended entry is a <b>45-degree</b> angle to the downwind leg, at pattern altitude, abeam midfield. A <b>segmented circle</b> near the windsock shows pattern direction with L-shaped indicators.</p><h3>Wake turbulence</h3><p>Every wing producing lift sheds swirling <b>wingtip vortices</b>. They are strongest from airplanes that are <b>heavy, clean (gear and flaps up), and slow</b>, which describes a big jet on takeoff or landing. Vortices sink and drift with the wind; a light <b>quartering tailwind</b> can hold them right over the touchdown zone. When landing behind a larger airplane, stay <b>above its flight path</b> and land <b>beyond</b> its touchdown point. When taking off behind one, lift off <b>before</b> its rotation point and climb above its path. If in doubt, wait a few minutes.</p>", "words": 316, "figs": [{"day": 20, "sec": 3, "num": "20", "src": "img/fig20-pattern.svg", "caption": "A standard left-hand traffic pattern, seen from above, with the recommended 45-degree entry at a nontowered airport.", "w": 480.0, "h": 300.0, "pos": 1}]}, {"id": "d21c1", "day": 21, "dayTitle": "Runway Markings", "title": "Runway numbers", "body": "<p>The big number at the end of a runway is its <b>magnetic</b> direction, divided by 10 and rounded to the nearest whole number, read from the approach direction. A runway pointing 087 degrees magnetic is <b>runway 9</b> (U.S. runways do not use a leading zero); the other end, pointing 267, is <b>runway 27</b>. The two ends always differ by 18. The number is painted so it reads upright to the pilot landing on that end. Parallel runways add a letter: two parallels are <b>L</b> and <b>R</b>, three are <b>L</b>, <b>C</b>, and <b>R</b>.</p><p>Because magnetic north slowly moves, an airport sometimes has to repaint its runways with new numbers, and a chart or airport diagram printed before the change can show the old ones. <b>Why you look:</b> lined up for takeoff, glance at the number at your feet and at the heading indicator. They should agree within a few degrees. That habit is the simplest defense against taking off on the wrong runway, which has caused fatal accidents.</p>", "words": 167, "figs": []}, {"id": "d21c2", "day": 21, "dayTitle": "Runway Markings", "title": "Centerline, side stripes, and the threshold", "body": "<p>The <b>centerline</b> is a line of white stripes and gaps down the middle of the runway (the standard is 120-foot stripes and 80-foot gaps), for alignment on takeoff and landing. Wider instrument runways also have <b>side stripes</b>, continuous white lines that mark the runway edges against the shoulder or the grass. Paved shoulders beside a runway, if marked, have yellow diagonal stripes: not for use by aircraft.</p><p><b>Threshold stripes</b> are the white stripes, like piano keys, across the start of the runway. They tell you where the runway available for landing begins. They come in two layouts: <b>eight stripes</b> of the same size, or a number that <b>shows the runway width</b>: 4 stripes for 60 feet, 6 for 75, 8 for 100, 12 for 150, and 16 for 200. Not every runway has them. Runways come in three marking types: a <b>visual</b> runway needs only the number and centerline, a <b>nonprecision instrument</b> runway adds threshold stripes and an aiming point, and a <b>precision instrument</b> runway adds touchdown zone markings and side stripes as well.</p>", "words": 174, "figs": []}, {"id": "d21c3", "day": 21, "dayTitle": "Runway Markings", "title": "Aiming point and touchdown zone", "body": "<p>The <b>aiming point</b> is a pair of broad white rectangles, one on each side of the centerline, about <b>1,000 feet</b> past the threshold. It gives landing airplanes, and especially jets flying a glide path, a visual point to aim at. In a trainer on a long runway it is a useful target. On a short runway you will often plan to touch down earlier, but never before the threshold.</p><p><b>Touchdown zone markings</b> appear on precision instrument runways. They are pairs of rectangular bars on both sides of the centerline every <b>500 feet</b>, coded by the number of bars: <b>three</b> at 500 feet, the aiming point at 1,000, <b>two</b> at 1,500 and 2,000, and <b>one</b> at 2,500 and 3,000. You can count them on the rollout to judge how much runway is behind you. On a runway too short for a full set from both ends, the bars nearest the middle are left off. Figure 21a puts all of these on one drawing.</p>", "words": 162, "figs": [{"day": 21, "sec": 2, "num": "21a", "src": "img/fig21a-runway.svg", "caption": "Top-down view of the approach end of a precision instrument runway 9 with a taxiway entrance. Colors are named in the labels: runway paint is white, taxiway paint is yellow, and the mandatory sign is red with white numbers.", "w": 480.0, "h": 500.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d21c4", "day": 21, "dayTitle": "Runway Markings", "title": "Displaced threshold, relocated threshold, blast pad, and closed runway", "body": "<p>A <b>displaced threshold</b> is a landing threshold placed somewhere other than the beginning of the paved runway, usually to keep landing airplanes higher over an obstacle. A <b>10-foot white threshold bar</b> marks the new start of the landing runway. Before it, <b>white arrows</b> down the centerline and <b>white arrowheads</b> across the runway mark the displaced area. You <b>may</b> use that pavement for <b>taxiing, the takeoff roll in either direction, and the landing rollout from the other direction</b>. You may <b>not</b> touch down on it when landing toward the arrows.</p><p>A <b>relocated threshold</b> is different. Construction or maintenance has closed part of the runway, so the landing threshold has moved. The closed part is <b>not available for takeoff or landing in either direction</b>, and the runway in the other direction is shortened as well. The markings vary: often there is a white threshold bar at the new threshold, while the old paint may or may not be covered. That is why the <b>NOTAM</b> (for example, \"9/27 W 900 CLSD\") is how you know about it (Day 43). Do not confuse the two. Arrows mean the pavement can be used for some things; a relocated threshold means it cannot.</p><p><b>Yellow chevrons</b> (V shapes pointing toward the runway) mark a <b>blast pad or stopway</b>: pavement lined up with the runway that is <b>unusable for landing, takeoff, and taxiing</b>. If a blast pad comes right before a displaced threshold, a 3-foot <b>yellow demarcation bar</b> separates them; it is yellow because it is not on the runway. A <b>closed runway</b> has a <b>yellow X</b> at each end, plus one every 1,000 feet if it is closed permanently. A lighted yellow X may stand on the runway ends instead. Some closures show no X at all, so check NOTAMs and ATIS. Figure 21b shows these runway ends side by side.</p>", "words": 304, "figs": [{"day": 21, "sec": 3, "num": "21b", "src": "img/fig21b-ends.svg", "caption": "Three runway ends you must be able to tell apart. Yellow marks pavement you may not use for that purpose; white arrows mark pavement you may use for some things.", "w": 480.0, "h": 432.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d21c5", "day": 21, "dayTitle": "Runway Markings", "title": "The holding position marking, seen from the taxiway", "body": "<p>Where a taxiway meets a runway, four <b>yellow</b> lines run across the taxiway: <b>two solid and two dashed</b>. This is the <b>runway holding position marking</b>, the hold line. The <b>solid lines are on your side</b> as you approach the runway, and that is where you <b>stop</b>, with no part of the airplane past them, unless you have a clearance to enter or cross. At a towered airport you need an explicit clearance from ATC to cross it. At a nontowered airport you stop, look both ways and up the approach path, announce, and cross only when no one is landing or taking off.</p><p>Large airports warn you that a hold line is coming. The <b>enhanced taxiway centerline</b> adds parallel <b>yellow dashes on both sides</b> of the normal solid centerline for up to <b>150 feet</b> before the hold line. When you see it, slow down and confirm your clearance before you get there. A red sign with white runway numbers (\"9-27\") stands beside the hold line. Tomorrow we look at that sign and the rest of the taxiway system.</p>", "words": 178, "figs": []}, {"id": "d22c1", "day": 22, "dayTitle": "Taxiway Markings and Airport Signs", "title": "Taxiway centerline and edges", "body": "<p>The <b>taxiway centerline</b> is a single <b>continuous yellow line</b>, 6 to 12 inches wide. Keep the nosewheel on it. Being on the centerline does not guarantee your wingtips clear other airplanes or objects, so on ramps and near hangars you still have to look. Where a taxiway curves onto another taxiway or a runway, the centerline curves with it as a lead-in line.</p><p><b>Taxiway edge markings</b> show where the taxiway ends when the paved surface keeps going. A <b>continuous double yellow line</b> means the pavement beyond is <b>not for aircraft</b>. It may be a shoulder that looks solid but may not hold your weight. A <b>dashed double yellow line</b> means the pavement beyond <b>is meant for aircraft</b>, such as an apron, and you may cross it. Shoulders that might be mistaken for taxiway can also carry yellow stripes painted across them. In both cases the yellow line marks the edge; it is not a centerline.</p>", "words": 155, "figs": []}, {"id": "d22c2", "day": 22, "dayTitle": "Taxiway Markings and Airport Signs", "title": "Holding position markings", "body": "<p>There are three hold lines to know. The <b>runway holding position marking</b> (yesterday's four lines, two solid and two dashed) marks where you stop before a runway. Stop with the whole airplane behind the solid lines unless you are cleared onto or across. At a towered airport, every runway crossing needs its own explicit clearance; \"taxi to runway 9\" does not clear you across any other runway on the way. Always read back hold-short instructions.</p><p>The <b>ILS critical area holding position marking</b> looks like a ladder across the taxiway: two solid yellow lines 2 feet apart, joined by pairs of short lines 10 feet apart. A red sign reading <b>ILS</b> stands beside it. It is usually farther from the runway than the runway hold line, so your airplane does not disturb the instrument landing signal. You hold there only when ATC tells you to \"hold short of the ILS critical area,\" usually in low weather. Otherwise you taxi on to the regular hold line. The <b>taxiway/taxiway holding position marking</b> is a single <b>dashed</b> yellow line across a taxiway. You stop there when told to \"hold short of taxiway B.\" Where there is no line, stop well clear of the intersecting taxiway. Figure 22a shows all three.</p>", "words": 206, "figs": [{"day": 22, "sec": 1, "num": "22a", "src": "img/fig22a-intersection.svg", "caption": "A made-up intersection. You are taxiing east on Taxiway A toward runway 9-27. Every marking shown is yellow; the signs are named by color in the labels.", "w": 480.0, "h": 556.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d22c3", "day": 22, "dayTitle": "Taxiway Markings and Airport Signs", "title": "Leaving the runway: the runway boundary", "body": "<p>The same four-line hold marking does a second job when you are exiting. From the runway side you see the <b>dashed</b> lines first. You are <b>not clear of the runway</b> until <b>every part of the airplane</b>, tail included, has crossed the <b>solid</b> lines. So after landing, taxi completely past the marking, then stop. If you cannot clear quickly, do not stop on the runway with traffic behind you. At a towered airport, do not turn onto another runway or cross one without a clearance.</p><p>To help, some airports put a <b>runway boundary sign</b> beside the marking, facing the runway: <b>black on yellow</b>, with a picture of the hold line on it. When you pass it on the way out, you are clear. An <b>ILS critical area boundary sign</b>, with a picture of the ladder marking, tells you the same thing about the ILS critical area.</p>", "words": 145, "figs": []}, {"id": "d22c4", "day": 22, "dayTitle": "Taxiway Markings and Airport Signs", "title": "Signs, and where you stop", "body": "<p>The sign colors from Day 20, tied to what you do. <b>Red with white letters = mandatory: stop.</b> These are the runway holding position sign (\"9-27\"), the runway approach area hold (\"9-APCH\"), the ILS critical area sign (\"ILS\"), and the no-entry sign (a white circle and bar). Each stands at a hold line, usually on the left side of the taxiway, and you do not pass it without a clearance at a towered field. On a hold sign the numbers are arranged like the runway: \"9-27\" means the runway 9 threshold is to your left and the runway 27 threshold is to your right.</p><p><b>Black with yellow letters and a yellow border = location: where you are.</b> These are taxiway location signs (\"B\") and runway location signs. A location sign is often mounted beside the red hold sign, so you know both which runway is ahead and which taxiway you are on. <b>Yellow with black letters and an arrow = direction: where to go.</b> Direction signs stand on your left before an intersection and point to the taxiways leading out of it. In an array, the location sign sits in the middle: turns to the left are shown left of it, and straight-ahead or right turns to the right. Destination signs (\"FUEL\" with an arrow) and information signs are also black on yellow. The runway distance remaining sign, a white number on black, shows thousands of feet of runway left.</p><p>Where upright signs are not enough, mostly at big airports, the same signs are <b>painted on the pavement</b>. A <b>red rectangle with white runway numbers</b> before a hold line is a painted hold sign; it sits left of the centerline on the holding side. <b>Yellow with black letters and an arrow</b> is a painted direction sign, on the left of the centerline for a left turn and on the right for a right turn. <b>Black with yellow letters</b> is a painted location sign, right of the centerline. Treat a painted sign exactly like the upright one. Figure 22b collects them.</p>", "words": 340, "figs": [{"day": 22, "sec": 3, "num": "22b", "src": "img/fig22b-signs.svg", "caption": "The sign families and the painted signs that back them up. Red means stop and get a clearance; black tells you where you are; yellow tells you where to go.", "w": 480.0, "h": 304.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d22c5", "day": 22, "dayTitle": "Taxiway Markings and Airport Signs", "title": "Two more markings worth knowing", "body": "<p>The <b>nonmovement area boundary</b> is a pair of yellow lines, <b>one solid and one dashed</b>, between the ramp and the taxiways. The <b>dashed side faces the movement area</b>, which at a towered airport is controlled by ground control; the <b>solid side is the ramp</b>. At a towered field, call ground before you cross from solid to dashed. <b>Geographic position markings</b> are circles at big airports with a pink center inside black and white rings and a number, sometimes with a letter. They mark reporting points on low-visibility taxi routes. You will rarely use them in a trainer, but now you will know one when you see it.</p>", "words": 107, "figs": []}, {"id": "d23c1", "day": 23, "dayTitle": "Airspace I: The Classes, Entry Rules, and Equipment", "title": "Controlled versus uncontrolled · Class A: the high-altitude highway", "body": "<h3>Controlled versus uncontrolled</h3><p><b>Controlled airspace</b> (Classes A, B, C, D, and E) is airspace where ATC (air traffic control) provides service to IFR flights (flights under Instrument Flight Rules), and in some classes to VFR flights too. <b>Uncontrolled airspace</b> is Class G. \"Controlled\" does not mean you always need permission; it means certain rules and services apply. For each class, a VFR pilot (Visual Flight Rules, flying by looking outside) needs three answers: <b>how big is it, what do I need to get in, and what equipment must the airplane have?</b> Tomorrow adds the fourth: the weather minimums.</p><p>Two equipment terms first. A <b>Mode C transponder</b> answers ATC radar with a code and your altitude. <b>ADS-B Out</b> (Automatic Dependent Surveillance-Broadcast) broadcasts your GPS position, altitude, and speed to ATC and to other aircraft. Since 2020, ADS-B Out is required wherever a Mode C transponder is required (14 CFR 91.215 and 91.225).</p><h3>Class A: the high-altitude highway</h3><p><b>Class A</b> runs from <b>18,000 feet MSL up to and including FL600</b> (flight level 600, about 60,000 feet) over the contiguous United States. Everyone in it flies IFR: you need an instrument rating, an IFR clearance, a two-way radio, a Mode C transponder, and ADS-B Out, and you set the altimeter to 29.92. As a private pilot without an instrument rating, you will not go there.</p>", "words": 214, "figs": []}, {"id": "d23c2", "day": 23, "dayTitle": "Airspace I: The Classes, Entry Rules, and Equipment", "title": "Class B: the busiest airports", "body": "<p><b>Class B</b> surrounds the nation's busiest airports. It typically runs from the surface to <b>10,000 feet MSL</b> and is individually tailored, usually shaped like an <b>upside-down wedding cake</b>: a small core at the surface, with wider layers (shelves) starting at higher floors. To enter you need an explicit <b>ATC clearance</b>: you must hear the words \"cleared into the Class Bravo\" (radar contact alone is not a clearance). You must hold at least a private pilot certificate, or be a student pilot with the specific instructor endorsement (61.95). A short list of the very busiest Class B airports does not allow student pilots to take off or land there at all (listed in Part 91, Appendix D).</p><p>Equipment: a two-way radio, a Mode C transponder, and ADS-B Out. Two more Class B facts that show up on tests: under a Class B shelf, or in a VFR corridor through it, the speed limit is <b>200 knots</b>; and within <b>30 nautical miles</b> of the primary airport, the <b>Mode C veil</b> (Day 25) requires a transponder and ADS-B Out even outside the Class B itself.</p>", "words": 182, "figs": []}, {"id": "d23c3", "day": 23, "dayTitle": "Airspace I: The Classes, Entry Rules, and Equipment", "title": "Class C: busy, with radar · Class D: a towered airport", "body": "<h3>Class C: busy, with radar</h3><p><b>Class C</b> surrounds medium-busy airports with a control tower and radar approach control. The typical shape has a <b>core of 5 nautical miles</b> radius from the surface to <b>4,000 feet above the airport</b>, and a <b>shelf out to 10 nautical miles</b> from <b>1,200 to 4,000 feet above the airport</b>. Around it is an <b>outer area</b>, normally 20 nm, where approach control offers radar service but participation is not required.</p><p>To enter, you must <b>establish two-way radio communication</b> with ATC before you cross the boundary. That means ATC has answered using your call sign. \"Cessna 12345, stand by\" counts; \"Aircraft calling, stand by\" does not. If you depart a small satellite airport underneath it, call as soon as practicable after takeoff. Equipment: two-way radio, Mode C transponder, and ADS-B Out, both inside Class C and above it up to 10,000 feet MSL.</p><h3>Class D: a towered airport</h3><p><b>Class D</b> surrounds an airport with an operating control tower. It typically runs from the surface to about <b>2,500 feet above the airport</b>, with a radius tailored to the airport (often a few nautical miles). Like Class C, you must <b>establish two-way radio communication</b> before entering, and if you depart a satellite airport inside it, call the tower as soon as practicable. Class D itself requires only a two-way radio, not a transponder (unless you are also inside a Mode C veil). The tower sequences you with other traffic but does not separate VFR airplanes from each other. When a part-time tower closes, the airspace usually becomes Class E or G; the Chart Supplement tells you which.</p>", "words": 258, "figs": []}, {"id": "d23c4", "day": 23, "dayTitle": "Airspace I: The Classes, Entry Rules, and Equipment", "title": "Class E and Class G: the everyday sky", "body": "<p><b>Class E</b> is all controlled airspace that is not A, B, C, or D. It comes in several pieces: <b>surface areas</b> around some nontowered airports with instrument approaches; areas starting at <b>700 feet AGL</b> near such airports, so IFR traffic stays in controlled airspace while descending; areas starting at <b>1,200 feet AGL</b>, which covers most of the country; the <b>Victor airways</b> (Day 27); and above FL600. Over the contiguous U.S., wherever it does not start lower, Class E begins at <b>14,500 feet MSL</b>. It extends up to, but not including, 18,000 feet MSL. There are no VFR entry requirements, though Mode C and ADS-B Out are required in Class E at and above 10,000 feet MSL, except at or below 2,500 feet AGL.</p><p><b>Class G</b> is uncontrolled airspace: whatever is left under the Class E floor, usually from the surface up to 700 or 1,200 feet AGL, and higher in remote areas. No entry requirements and no equipment requirements. Tomorrow you will see that it also has the most relaxed weather minimums.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Typical size</th><th>VFR entry</th><th>Equipment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>18,000 MSL to FL600</td><td>IFR only (instrument rating, IFR clearance)</td><td>Radio, Mode C, ADS-B Out</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Surface to about 10,000 MSL, layered</td><td>Explicit ATC clearance; private certificate or student endorsement</td><td>Radio, Mode C, ADS-B Out</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>5 nm core to 4,000 AGL; 10 nm shelf 1,200 to 4,000 AGL</td><td>Two-way radio communication established</td><td>Radio, Mode C, ADS-B Out</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Surface to about 2,500 AGL, tailored radius</td><td>Two-way radio communication established</td><td>Two-way radio</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>Usually from 700 or 1,200 AGL (or surface) to below 18,000 MSL</td><td>None</td><td>None for VFR, except Mode C and ADS-B Out at or above 10,000 MSL (not at or below 2,500 AGL)</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>Surface to the Class E floor</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">Typical values; each airport's real dimensions are on the sectional chart. AGL heights for Class C and D are measured above the airport elevation.</p>", "words": 172, "figs": [{"day": 23, "sec": 5, "num": "23", "src": "img/fig23-airspace.svg", "caption": "A typical side view of the airspace classes. Real dimensions vary by airport; always check the sectional chart.", "w": 480.0, "h": 334.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d24c1", "day": 24, "dayTitle": "Airspace II: VFR Weather Minimums and Special VFR", "title": "Why the minimums exist · The common pattern: 3-152 · Class G and its exceptions", "body": "<h3>Why the minimums exist</h3><p>VFR flying depends on <b>see and avoid</b>. An IFR airplane can pop out of a cloud at any moment, and at airliner speeds you need time to spot each other. So the rules (14 CFR 91.155) require a minimum <b>flight visibility</b> (how far you can see forward from the cockpit) and a minimum distance from clouds. \"<b>Clear of clouds</b>\" means just that: not in them, with no specific distance. Visibility is in <b>statute miles</b> (SM); cloud clearance is in feet, or miles for horizontal distance at high altitude.</p><h3>The common pattern: 3-152</h3><p>Most of the time below 10,000 feet MSL you will use one set of numbers: <b>3 statute miles</b> visibility, and <b>500 feet below, 1,000 feet above, 2,000 feet horizontal</b> from clouds. Pilots remember it as \"3-152.\" It applies in <b>Class C, Class D, and Class E below 10,000 MSL</b>, and in Class G at night.</p><p><b>Class B</b> is different: <b>3 SM and clear of clouds</b>, because ATC is already separating everyone inside it. Class A has no VFR minimums because VFR is not allowed there.</p><h3>Class G and its exceptions</h3><p>Class G has the most relaxed minimums, and two exceptions to know cold. <b>By day at or below 1,200 feet AGL</b>: <b>1 SM and clear of clouds</b>. <b>At night at or below 1,200 feet AGL</b>: 3 SM and 3-152. But there is a night exception: you may fly with <b>1 SM and clear of clouds</b> when you are in an airport traffic pattern within <b>1/2 mile of the runway</b>. Above 1,200 feet AGL and below 10,000 MSL in Class G: by day <b>1 SM</b> with 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal; at night <b>3 SM</b> with the same clearances.</p>", "words": 272, "figs": [{"day": 24, "sec": 2, "num": "24", "src": "img/fig24-clouds.svg", "caption": "Cloud clearance and visibility, below and at or above 10,000 feet MSL.", "w": 480.0, "h": 318.0, "pos": 2}]}, {"id": "d24c2", "day": 24, "dayTitle": "Airspace II: VFR Weather Minimums and Special VFR", "title": "The 10,000-foot rule · Basic VFR in surface areas and Special VFR", "body": "<h3>The 10,000-foot rule</h3><p>At and above <b>10,000 feet MSL</b>, there is no 250-knot speed limit, so airplanes are faster and you need more room to see them. In Class E at or above 10,000 MSL, and in Class G above 1,200 feet AGL and at or above 10,000 MSL, the minimums become <b>5 SM visibility, 1,000 feet below, 1,000 feet above, and 1 statute mile horizontal</b> from clouds. Pilots call it \"5-111.\"</p><h3>Basic VFR in surface areas and Special VFR</h3><p>Near airports, one more rule (91.155(c) and (d)) applies within the <b>surface area</b> of Class B, C, D, or E airspace (the part that touches the ground around the airport). You may not fly VFR beneath the ceiling when the <b>ceiling is less than 1,000 feet</b>, and you may not take off, land, or enter the traffic pattern unless <b>ground visibility is at least 3 SM</b> (or flight visibility, if ground visibility is not reported). The <b>ceiling</b> is the lowest broken or overcast layer.</p><p><b>Special VFR</b> (91.157) is the way out of that box. With an ATC clearance, you may operate within a surface area, below 10,000 feet MSL, with <b>1 SM flight visibility and clear of clouds</b>; to take off or land, ground visibility must be at least 1 SM (or flight visibility, if ground visibility is not reported). You must <b>request</b> it; ATC will not offer it. At <b>night</b> (sunset to sunrise), Special VFR requires an <b>instrument rating and an IFR-equipped airplane</b>. Some busy airports do not allow fixed-wing Special VFR at all; the sectional marks them \"NO SVFR.\" Student pilots cannot use it, because student solo requires 3 SM by day. Legal does not mean wise: 1 mile and clear of clouds is very little room.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Airspace</th><th>Visibility</th><th>Cloud clearance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Class A</td><td>Not applicable</td><td>Not applicable (IFR only)</td></tr><tr><td>Class B</td><td>3 SM</td><td>Clear of clouds</td></tr><tr><td>Class C, D</td><td>3 SM</td><td>500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal</td></tr><tr><td>Class E below 10,000 MSL</td><td>3 SM</td><td>500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal</td></tr><tr><td>Class E at/above 10,000 MSL</td><td>5 SM</td><td>1,000 below, 1,000 above, 1 SM horizontal</td></tr><tr><td>Class G, 1,200 AGL or less, day</td><td>1 SM</td><td>Clear of clouds</td></tr><tr><td>Class G, 1,200 AGL or less, night</td><td>3 SM</td><td>500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal*</td></tr><tr><td>Class G, above 1,200 AGL below 10,000 MSL, day</td><td>1 SM</td><td>500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal</td></tr><tr><td>Class G, above 1,200 AGL below 10,000 MSL, night</td><td>3 SM</td><td>500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal</td></tr><tr><td>Class G, above 1,200 AGL and at/above 10,000 MSL</td><td>5 SM</td><td>1,000 below, 1,000 above, 1 SM horizontal</td></tr><tr><td>Special VFR (surface area, with clearance)</td><td>1 SM</td><td>Clear of clouds (night: instrument rating and IFR airplane)</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">* Night exception in Class G: 1 SM and clear of clouds in an airport traffic pattern within 1/2 mile of the runway. Surface areas of B, C, D, E also require a 1,000 ft ceiling and 3 SM ground visibility for normal VFR.</p>", "words": 278, "figs": []}, {"id": "d25c1", "day": 25, "dayTitle": "Airspace III: Special Use Areas, TFRs, and Transponder Rules", "title": "Special use airspace · Other airspace areas", "body": "<h3>Special use airspace</h3><p><b>Special use airspace</b> confines activities that are hazardous or sensitive. <b>Prohibited areas</b> (marked P-, such as P-56 over the White House and National Mall) are off-limits, period. <b>Restricted areas</b> (R-) contain invisible hazards like artillery firing, guided missiles, or aerial gunnery; you may not enter when they are active without permission from the controlling agency. <b>Warning areas</b> (W-) contain the same kinds of hazards but over international waters, beginning 3 nm offshore, so the U.S. cannot prohibit entry; you are warned instead.</p><p><b>Military operations areas</b> (MOAs) separate military training, such as dogfighting practice, from IFR traffic. VFR flight is legal, but use extreme caution and consider contacting the controlling agency or Flight Service for status. <b>Alert areas</b> (A-) have a high volume of pilot training or unusual activity; no permission needed, just extra vigilance, and all pilots in them share responsibility for collision avoidance. <b>Controlled firing areas</b> (CFAs) contain hazardous activity that stops when a spotter aircraft, radar, or ground lookout sees an aircraft approaching, so they are <b>not charted</b>. The chart margin lists each charted area's altitudes, times of use, and controlling agency.</p><h3>Other airspace areas</h3><p><b>Military training routes</b> are low-level, high-speed corridors. Routes with four-digit numbers (such as VR1234) are flown at or below 1,500 feet AGL; three-digit routes include segments above 1,500 feet AGL. IR routes are flown IFR, VR routes VFR, but either way the jets are fast and low. <b>Parachute jumping areas</b> are marked on the sectional with a parachute symbol. <b>National security areas</b> ask pilots to voluntarily avoid flying through them, and a NOTAM can temporarily prohibit flight there. Around Washington, D.C., the <b>Special Flight Rules Area</b> requires special FAA training before you fly VFR within 60 nm of the DCA navigation station (91.161).</p>", "words": 287, "figs": []}, {"id": "d25c2", "day": 25, "dayTitle": "Airspace III: Special Use Areas, TFRs, and Transponder Rules", "title": "TFRs at a useful level · The Mode C veil and transponder rules", "body": "<h3>TFRs at a useful level</h3><p>A <b>temporary flight restriction</b> (TFR) closes airspace for a time, published as an FDC NOTAM. The common kinds and their regulations: <b>disaster and hazard</b> areas such as wildfires, so firefighting aircraft can work (91.137); <b>presidential and VIP</b> movements (91.141); <b>space flight operations</b> such as rocket launches (91.143); and <b>aerial demonstrations and major sporting events</b> such as air shows (91.145). Some standing restrictions, like the one over large stadiums, come from special security instructions instead. Day 43 covers how to read the NOTAM and the stadium TFR's dimensions and timing.</p><p>TFRs are <b>not printed on the sectional chart</b>; they change too often. Check them in your briefing, on the FAA's TFR website, or in your EFB's graphical layer before every flight and again before the return. If you are ever intercepted by military aircraft, follow the interceptor's signals and try to contact them on 121.5 MHz.</p><h3>The Mode C veil and transponder rules</h3><p>Within <b>30 nautical miles</b> of a Class B primary airport, from the <b>surface to 10,000 feet MSL</b>, you need a Mode C transponder and ADS-B Out, even outside the Class B itself. That area is the <b>Mode C veil</b>, drawn on the sectional as a thin magenta circle labeled \"MODE C 30 NM.\" A narrow exception lets aircraft not originally certificated with an electrical system, such as some antique airplanes, gliders, and balloons, fly in the veil outside Class B and C airspace and below the Class B ceiling or 10,000 MSL, whichever is lower.</p><p>Putting 91.215 and 91.225 together, Mode C and ADS-B Out are required: in <b>Class A, B, and C</b>; in the <b>Mode C veil</b>; <b>above the ceiling and within the lateral limits of Class B or C</b> up to 10,000 MSL; and in <b>Class E at and above 10,000 MSL</b>, except at or below 2,500 feet AGL (plus a special area over the Gulf of Mexico). ADS-B Out comes in two flavors: <b>1090ES</b>, which works everywhere, and <b>UAT</b> (978 MHz), which works below 18,000 MSL only. <b>ADS-B In</b> (receiving traffic and weather) is helpful but not required. VFR pilots squawk <b>1200</b> unless ATC assigns a code.</p>", "words": 345, "figs": []}, {"id": "d26c1", "day": 26, "dayTitle": "Sectional Charts I: Reading the Chart", "title": "Scale, currency, and the legend · North, variation, and the grid", "body": "<h3>Scale, currency, and the legend</h3><p>A <b>sectional aeronautical chart</b> is drawn at a scale of <b>1:500,000</b>: one inch on the chart is about 7 nautical miles on the ground. Busy areas around Class B airports also have a <b>Terminal Area Chart</b> (TAC) at 1:250,000, twice the detail. Sectionals are updated every 56 days, so check the effective date on the cover and use a current one. The <b>legend</b>, printed on the chart's front panel, explains every symbol; when the examiner points at something you do not recognize, saying \"I'd check the legend\" is a fine start. All elevations on the chart are in feet <b>MSL</b> unless marked otherwise.</p><h3>North, variation, and the grid</h3><p>The chart's grid lines are <b>latitude and longitude</b>. The north-south lines (meridians) point to <b>true north</b>, the geographic pole, so courses measured on the chart are true courses. Your compass points to <b>magnetic north</b>. The difference is <b>variation</b>, shown by dashed magenta <b>isogonic lines</b> labeled, for example, \"3°E.\" The <b>compass roses</b> printed around VOR stations are aligned to magnetic north, which is a handy cross-check.</p><p>Each grid box (a <b>quadrangle</b>, 30 minutes of latitude by 30 minutes of longitude) shows a large bold <b>Maximum Elevation Figure</b> (MEF), written as a big digit for thousands and a smaller one for hundreds: a big 1 next to a small 7 means 1,700 feet MSL. The MEF is based on the highest terrain or obstacle in that box, adjusted for possible error and rounded up to the next 100 feet. It is a quick way to see how high you must be to clear everything in that area.</p>", "words": 258, "figs": []}, {"id": "d26c2", "day": 26, "dayTitle": "Sectional Charts I: Reading the Chart", "title": "Terrain and obstacles", "body": "<p>Terrain is shown with <b>contour lines</b> and color tints that grow from green in the lowlands toward tan and brown at high elevations, with the highest spots marked by a dot and an elevation. Obstacles have their own symbols. A <b>single obstruction</b> is an inverted V with a dot; a <b>group obstruction</b> is two inverted Vs together. Obstacles 1,000 feet AGL and taller use a larger symbol, lighting is shown with small flash marks, and \"UC\" means under construction.</p><p>Next to each obstacle are two numbers. The <b>bold number on top is the height of the top above sea level (MSL)</b>; the number in <b>parentheses below is its height above the ground (AGL)</b>. For example, 1249 over (350) is a tower whose top is 1,249 feet MSL, standing 350 feet tall on ground about 900 feet high. For terrain clearance, compare your altimeter with the MSL number. For 91.119 minimum altitudes, think in AGL.</p>", "words": 154, "figs": []}, {"id": "d26c3", "day": 26, "dayTitle": "Sectional Charts I: Reading the Chart", "title": "Airport symbols and data blocks", "body": "<p>Color tells you about the tower: <b>blue</b> airports have an operating control tower; <b>magenta</b> airports do not. Shape tells you about the runways. A circle with the runway pattern drawn inside means <b>hard-surfaced runways from 1,500 to 8,069 feet</b>. A runway pattern alone, with no circle, means hard-surfaced runways longer than 8,069 feet. A plain <b>filled circle</b> means the runways are <b>not hard-surfaced</b> (grass, dirt, gravel). <b>Tick marks</b> around the circle mean fuel is available and the field is attended during normal working hours. A small <b>star</b> above the symbol marks a rotating beacon operating from sunset to sunrise. An \"R\" in a circle is a private airport.</p><p>The <b>data block</b> beside the airport gives its name and identifier, then frequencies: <b>CT</b> followed by the tower frequency, <b>ATIS</b> frequency, and a <b>C in a solid circle</b> marking the CTAF. The last line reads, for example, <b>900 L 50 122.95</b>: field elevation 900 feet MSL, runway lighting (L, sunset to sunrise; *L means limitations, so check the Chart Supplement), longest runway 5,000 feet (in hundreds), and UNICOM 122.95. \"RP\" lists runways with right traffic, and \"NO SVFR\" means fixed-wing Special VFR is not allowed.</p>", "words": 193, "figs": [{"day": 26, "sec": 3, "num": "26", "src": "img/fig26-chart.svg", "caption": "A made-up sectional snippet showing the symbols from today's lesson: Class D around a towered airport, a nontowered airport with a 700 AGL Class E floor, a soft-surface strip, obstacles, an isogonic line, and an MEF.", "w": 480.0, "h": 400.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d26c4", "day": 26, "dayTitle": "Sectional Charts I: Reading the Chart", "title": "How airspace is drawn", "body": "<p>You met these on Day 23; here is how each looks. <b>Class B</b>: <b>solid blue</b> lines, each shelf labeled like a fraction in hundreds of feet MSL, so 80 over 40 means 4,000 to 8,000 feet MSL. <b>Class C</b>: <b>solid magenta</b> lines, labeled the same way (40 over SFC means surface to 4,000 MSL). <b>Class D</b>: a <b>dashed blue</b> line, with its ceiling in a small box in hundreds of feet MSL ([34] means up to and including 3,400 MSL; a minus sign, like [-30], means up to but not including). <b>Class E to the surface</b>: a <b>dashed magenta</b> line.</p><p>Class E with a floor of <b>700 feet AGL</b> is shown by a <b>fuzzy magenta</b> band, with the shaded side toward the area where the 700-foot floor applies. A <b>fuzzy blue</b> band marks where Class E with a <b>1,200-foot AGL</b> floor ends next to Class G that extends higher. Everywhere else the Class E floor is 1,200 AGL by default. Special use areas are outlined in hatched lines: blue hatching for prohibited, restricted, and warning areas; magenta hatching for MOAs and alert areas. The Mode C veil is a thin magenta circle.</p>", "words": 192, "figs": []}, {"id": "d27c1", "day": 27, "dayTitle": "Sectional Charts II: Planning a Route with the Chart", "title": "Drawing the line and measuring the course · Choosing checkpoints", "body": "<h3>Drawing the line and measuring the course</h3><p>Draw a straight line from departure to destination. To measure its <b>true course</b>, lay the straight edge of your <b>plotter</b> (a clear ruler with a protractor) along the line, then slide it until the small center hole sits on a <b>meridian</b> (a north-south longitude line) near the <b>middle of the route</b>. Read the course where the meridian crosses the protractor scale. The scale shows two numbers, such as 060 and 240; pick the one that matches the general direction you are flying. Measure near the middle because meridians converge toward the pole, so the angle changes slightly along a long line. For a course that runs nearly north-south, use a line of latitude and the plotter's small inner scale instead.</p><p>For distance, use the plotter's <b>sectional scale</b> in nautical miles (not the TAC scale or statute miles). You can also check it against the latitude scale: each minute of latitude along a meridian is one nautical mile. Then apply variation from the nearest isogonic line to get the magnetic course: east is least, west is best (Day 35).</p><h3>Choosing checkpoints</h3><p><b>Pilotage</b> means navigating by landmarks, so pick checkpoints you cannot miss: a large lake with a distinctive shape, a highway intersection, a town with a railroad running through it, a river bend. Space them about 10 to 15 nm apart and slightly to one side of the line so you can see them past the nose. Prefer features that are unique; one small town looks like another, but a town next to a lake and a highway is hard to mistake. Tall towers are useful at a distance but hard to see up close in haze. ATC also uses magenta flag symbols on the chart as <b>VFR checkpoints</b>, and VFR waypoints (four-pointed stars with VP identifiers) can go into a GPS.</p>", "words": 299, "figs": [{"day": 27, "sec": 0, "num": "27", "src": "img/fig27-plotter.svg", "caption": "Measuring a true course with a plotter. The chart and route are hypothetical.", "w": 480.0, "h": 290.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d27c2", "day": 27, "dayTitle": "Sectional Charts II: Planning a Route with the Chart", "title": "Airspace and obstacles along the line · Victor airways, special use, and TFRs", "body": "<h3>Airspace and obstacles along the line</h3><p>Walk the line with your finger and ask at each point: what airspace am I in at my planned altitude? Will I pass under a Class B shelf or through a Class C shelf, and do I need a clearance or communication? Am I inside a Mode C veil? Where does Class E start? Then look at the <b>MEFs</b> of every quadrangle you cross and the <b>obstacles</b> near the line, and pick an altitude that clears them comfortably, meets the 91.119 minimum altitudes, stays below any airspace you want to avoid, and follows the VFR cruising altitude rule (91.159) if you will be more than 3,000 feet AGL.</p><h3>Victor airways, special use, and TFRs</h3><p>The light blue lines between VOR stations labeled V plus a number (V24, for example) are <b>Victor airways</b>, the low-altitude IFR highways. Each is Class E airspace extending <b>4 nm on each side</b> of the centerline, from <b>1,200 feet AGL up to but not including 18,000 feet MSL</b>. VFR pilots may fly along them, but IFR traffic concentrates there, especially near the VORs, so keep a sharp lookout or fly slightly off to the side. Some busy areas also publish <b>VFR flyways</b> on the back of the Terminal Area Chart to help VFR pilots stay clear of Class B.</p><p>Check each <b>special use area</b> near the route against the table in the chart margin: altitudes, times, and controlling agency. Remember what the chart cannot show: <b>TFRs</b> and other NOTAM changes are not printed on it, so the briefing and the TFR list are part of route planning, not a separate step.</p><h3>Cross-check the Chart Supplement</h3><p>The sectional's data block is a summary. The <b>Chart Supplement</b> (the FAA's airport directory, also updated every 56 days and available free online or in your EFB) has the details: every runway's length, width, and surface; traffic pattern direction and altitude; lighting and how to turn it on; fuel types; tower and FSS hours; and remarks such as \"deer on and in vicinity of airport.\" Check it for the departure, the destination, and any airport you might divert to, then check the NOTAMs for anything newer.</p>", "words": 345, "figs": []}, {"id": "d28c1", "day": 28, "dayTitle": "Radio, ATC, and Light Signals", "title": "The basic call · Who you talk to at a towered airport · Nontowered airports", "body": "<h3>The basic call</h3><p>Every first call has four parts: <b>who you are calling, who you are, where you are, and what you want</b>. For example: \"Riverside Ground, Cessna 12345, at the west ramp with information Charlie, ready to taxi, VFR to the north.\" Listen before you talk, think about what you will say, then say it in one clean breath. Use the <b>phonetic alphabet</b> for letters: Alfa, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, Echo, Foxtrot, Golf, Hotel, India, Juliett, Kilo, Lima, Mike, November, Oscar, Papa, Quebec, Romeo, Sierra, Tango, Uniform, Victor, Whiskey, X-ray, Yankee, Zulu. Say \"niner\" for 9.</p><p>Useful words: <b>\"Roger\"</b> means I received your transmission (not yes). <b>\"Wilco\"</b> means I will comply. <b>\"Affirmative\"</b> means yes. <b>\"Unable\"</b> means I cannot do that. <b>Read back</b> runway assignments, altitude assignments, headings, and <b>all hold-short instructions</b>.</p><h3>Who you talk to at a towered airport</h3><p>First, listen to <b>ATIS</b> (Automatic Terminal Information Service), a recorded broadcast of weather, runway in use, and notices, identified by a phonetic letter that changes with each update. Tell ATC you have it (\"with information Charlie\"). Then <b>Ground</b> control handles taxiing, <b>Tower</b> handles the runway and nearby airspace, and <b>Departure</b> or <b>Approach</b> control (radar) handles the surrounding area. Farther out, <b>Center</b> handles larger regions. You must follow ATC clearances and instructions unless you have an emergency (91.123). If you cannot comply, say \"unable.\"</p><h3>Nontowered airports</h3><p>Without a tower, pilots talk to each other on the <b>CTAF</b> (Common Traffic Advisory Frequency), listed on the sectional and in the Chart Supplement. Start and end each call with the airport name: \"Lakeview traffic, Cessna 12345, left downwind runway 18, full stop, Lakeview.\" Announce about 10 miles out, then on entering the pattern, on each leg, and when clear of the runway. Not every airplane has a radio, so keep looking outside. Many such fields have automated weather, <b>AWOS</b> or <b>ASOS</b>, on a frequency you can listen to.</p>", "words": 300, "figs": []}, {"id": "d28c2", "day": 28, "dayTitle": "Radio, ATC, and Light Signals", "title": "Flight following and the transponder · Light gun signals", "body": "<h3>Flight following and the transponder</h3><p><b>Flight following</b> (VFR radar traffic advisories) is a free service where approach or Center watches you on radar and points out traffic. Ask: \"Approach, Cessna 12345, request flight following.\" They will assign a squawk code. Normally VFR pilots squawk <b>1200</b>. Three special codes to memorize: <b>7500</b> hijacking, <b>7600</b> radio communication failure, <b>7700</b> emergency. The emergency radio frequency is <b>121.5 MHz</b>.</p><h3>Light gun signals</h3><p>If your radio fails at a towered airport, squawk 7600 and watch the tower for light signals (see the table). Acknowledge in the air by rocking the wings in daytime, or flashing the landing or navigation lights at night; on the ground, move the ailerons or rudder (day) or flash lights (night).</p><table><thead><tr><th>Light</th><th>On the ground</th><th>In the air</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Steady green</td><td>Cleared for takeoff</td><td>Cleared to land</td></tr><tr><td>Flashing green</td><td>Cleared to taxi</td><td>Return for landing (await steady green)</td></tr><tr><td>Steady red</td><td>Stop</td><td>Give way to other aircraft and continue circling</td></tr><tr><td>Flashing red</td><td>Taxi clear of the runway in use</td><td>Airport unsafe, do not land</td></tr><tr><td>Flashing white</td><td>Return to starting point on airport</td><td>Not used</td></tr><tr><td>Alternating red and green</td><td>Exercise extreme caution</td><td>Exercise extreme caution</td></tr></tbody></table>", "words": 112, "figs": []}, {"id": "d29c1", "day": 29, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory I: The Atmosphere, Pressure, and Wind", "title": "Where weather happens · Why the air moves", "body": "<h3>Where weather happens</h3><p>Nearly all weather happens in the <b>troposphere</b>, the lowest layer of the atmosphere. Its top, the <b>tropopause</b>, is lower over the poles and higher over the equator, roughly 20,000 feet over the poles to around 48,000 feet over the equator. In the troposphere, temperature normally falls as you climb. The <b>jet stream</b>, a narrow band of very strong wind, usually sits near the tropopause, far above where a trainer flies, but it steers the storm systems that bring your weather.</p><p>The air is a mix of about <b>78 percent nitrogen, 21 percent oxygen</b>, and 1 percent other gases, including water vapor. That small, changing amount of water vapor is what makes clouds, fog, and storms possible.</p><h3>Why the air moves</h3><p>The sun heats the earth unevenly: the equator more than the poles, land more than water, dark fields more than snow. Warm air expands and becomes less dense, which creates differences in pressure, and air flows from <b>high pressure toward low pressure</b>. That flow is <b>wind</b>. On weather maps, lines of equal pressure are <b>isobars</b>; the closer together they are (a steep <b>pressure gradient</b>), the stronger the wind.</p><p>The earth's rotation deflects moving air (the <b>Coriolis force</b>, which turns it to the right in the northern hemisphere). So instead of flowing straight from high to low, wind a few thousand feet up blows roughly <b>parallel to the isobars</b>. Near the surface, <b>friction</b> with the ground slows the wind, which weakens the Coriolis turn, so surface wind blows at an angle across the isobars toward the low and is usually lighter than the wind a few thousand feet up.</p>", "words": 264, "figs": []}, {"id": "d29c2", "day": 29, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory I: The Atmosphere, Pressure, and Wind", "title": "Highs and lows · Local winds", "body": "<h3>Highs and lows</h3><p>In the northern hemisphere, air flows <b>clockwise and outward</b> around a <b>high</b>, and <b>counterclockwise and inward</b> around a <b>low</b>. In a high, air sinks and warms, which tends to bring fair weather (sometimes with haze trapped near the ground). In a low, air rises and cools, which brings clouds and precipitation. An elongated high is a <b>ridge</b>; an elongated low is a <b>trough</b>, and troughs often bring weather with them.</p><p>Pressure systems also matter to your altimeter. Flying from a high toward a low without resetting the altimeter makes it read higher than you really are: \"high to low, look out below\" (Day 14).</p><h3>Local winds</h3><p>Some winds come from local heating rather than big pressure systems. Along a coast or a large lake, land heats faster than water during the day, so a <b>sea breeze</b> blows from the water toward the land in the afternoon; at night the land cools faster and a weaker <b>land breeze</b> blows out toward the water. In hills and mountains, a <b>valley breeze</b> flows upslope during the day and a <b>mountain breeze</b> flows downslope at night as cool, dense air drains into the valleys. These winds can change which runway is in use and add bumps near the ground.</p>", "words": 203, "figs": [{"day": 29, "sec": 2, "num": "29", "src": "img/fig29-hl.svg", "caption": "Circulation around high and low pressure in the Northern Hemisphere.", "w": 480.0, "h": 268.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d29c3", "day": 29, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory I: The Atmosphere, Pressure, and Wind", "title": "Moisture and the dew point", "body": "<p>Water changes state: evaporation (liquid to vapor) absorbs heat, and condensation (vapor to liquid, making clouds) releases heat, which can fuel storms. <b>Relative humidity</b> is how much water vapor the air holds compared to the most it could hold at that temperature. The <b>dew point</b> is the temperature to which air must cool to become saturated (100 percent relative humidity).</p><p>When the temperature and dew point get close (a small <b>temperature/dew point spread</b>), expect clouds, fog, or precipitation. A rule of thumb for the height of cumulus cloud bases: <b>the spread in °F divided by 4.4</b>, times 1,000 feet (or the spread in °C divided by 2.5, times 1,000 feet), above the ground. Dew and frost form when the ground cools to the dew point overnight: dew if the dew point is above freezing, frost if it is at or below freezing.</p>", "words": 142, "figs": []}, {"id": "d30c1", "day": 30, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory II: Stability, Air Masses, and Fronts", "title": "Lapse rates · Stable and unstable air", "body": "<h3>Lapse rates</h3><p>A <b>lapse rate</b> is how fast temperature drops as you climb. The <b>standard</b> (average) lapse rate is about <b>2 °C (3.5 °F) per 1,000 feet</b>. Real air varies.</p><p>When a bubble of air (a <b>parcel</b>) rises, it expands and cools; when it sinks, it compresses and warms. Unsaturated air cools at the <b>dry adiabatic lapse rate</b>, about <b>3 °C (5.4 °F) per 1,000 feet</b>. Once the parcel is saturated and forming cloud, condensation releases heat, so it cools more slowly, at the <b>moist adiabatic lapse rate</b>, roughly 1.1 to 2.8 °C per 1,000 feet depending on temperature. \"Adiabatic\" just means the temperature change comes from expansion or compression, not from adding or removing heat.</p><h3>Stable and unstable air</h3><p><b>Stability</b> is the air's resistance to vertical movement. Push a parcel up. If it ends up colder (denser) than the air around it, it sinks back: the air is <b>stable</b>. If it ends up warmer (lighter) than the air around it, it keeps rising on its own: the air is <b>unstable</b>. So stability depends on how fast the surrounding air cools with height. Air that cools only slowly with height (or warms, in an inversion) is stable. Air that cools quickly with height, like air over a sun-baked field on a summer afternoon, is unstable.</p><p>Warming the air from below or cooling it from above makes it less stable; cooling from below or warming from above makes it more stable. Adding moisture makes it less stable.</p>", "words": 240, "figs": [{"day": 30, "sec": 1, "num": "30a", "src": "img/fig30a-stability.svg", "caption": "Push a parcel of air up and compare it with the air around it. Colder means it sinks back (stable); warmer means it keeps rising (unstable).", "w": 480.0, "h": 330.0, "pos": 1}]}, {"id": "d30c2", "day": 30, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory II: Stability, Air Masses, and Fronts", "title": "What each kind of air looks like · Air masses", "body": "<h3>What each kind of air looks like</h3><p>The table below is a favorite on the knowledge test. Learn it as a pair of pictures: stable air is a flat gray layer and a smooth ride in haze; unstable air is puffy, building clouds and a bumpy ride with good visibility between showers.</p><p>A <b>temperature inversion</b> is a layer where temperature <b>increases</b> with altitude, the most stable air of all. The common one is a <b>surface radiation inversion</b> on clear, calm nights, when the ground cools the air touching it. Inversions trap haze, smoke, and fog near the ground, and there can be a sharp wind shear at the top of the inversion (Day 32).</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Stable air</th><th>Unstable air</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Clouds</td><td>Stratiform (layers)</td><td>Cumuliform (heaps, towers)</td></tr><tr><td>Turbulence</td><td>Smooth</td><td>Rough</td></tr><tr><td>Visibility</td><td>Fair to poor (haze, smoke)</td><td>Good, except in showers</td></tr><tr><td>Precipitation</td><td>Steady (drizzle, steady rain)</td><td>Showery</td></tr><tr><td>Fog</td><td>Likely when the air cools near the surface</td><td>Uncommon</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">Classic knowledge-test pairs (PHAK). Real air can be stable at one level and unstable at another.</p><h3>Air masses</h3><p>An <b>air mass</b> is a huge body of air with similar temperature and moisture, picked up from the region it sat over (its <b>source region</b>). Air masses are named with two letters: <b>c</b> (continental, dry) or <b>m</b> (maritime, moist), plus <b>P</b> (polar, cold) or <b>T</b> (tropical, warm). So <b>mT</b> is warm and humid air from the Gulf of Mexico, and <b>cP</b> is cold, dry air from Canada.</p><p>Air masses change as they move. <b>Cold air moving over a warmer surface</b> is heated from below and becomes unstable: cumulus, showers, turbulence, good visibility (lake-effect snow is one example). <b>Warm air moving over a colder surface</b> is cooled from below and becomes stable: stratus, fog, poor visibility, smooth air.</p>", "words": 224, "figs": []}, {"id": "d30c3", "day": 30, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory II: Stability, Air Masses, and Fronts", "title": "Fronts", "body": "<p>A <b>front</b> is the boundary between two air masses. Crossing any front you will notice a change in <b>temperature</b>, a <b>wind shift</b>, and a change in <b>pressure</b>; dew point often changes too. On surface charts, a cold front is a blue line with triangles, a warm front a red line with half-circles, a stationary front alternates the two, and an occluded front is purple with both symbols on the same side.</p><p>A <b>cold front</b> is cold air pushing under warm air. It moves fast and has a steep slope, so if the warm air is moist and unstable it brings a narrow band of cumulus clouds, gusty winds, showers and thunderstorms, often with a <b>squall line</b> ahead of it, then rapid clearing, cooler temperatures, and good visibility behind it. A <b>warm front</b> is warm air sliding up over cold air on a gentle slope. Its weather spreads over hundreds of miles ahead of it: cirrus first, then lowering, thickening layers (cirrostratus, altostratus, then nimbostratus), steady rain or drizzle, poor visibility, and fog. If the warm air is unstable, thunderstorms can hide inside those layers. A <b>stationary front</b> barely moves, so its weather can linger for days. An <b>occluded front</b> forms when a cold front catches up with a warm front, combining weather from both.</p>", "words": 214, "figs": [{"day": 30, "sec": 4, "num": "30b", "src": "img/fig30b-front.svg", "caption": "Cold front cross-section: cold air wedges under warm, moist air, lifting it into clouds, showers, and often thunderstorms along the front.", "w": 480.0, "h": 290.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d31c1", "day": 31, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory III: Clouds, Fog, and Thunderstorms", "title": "Clouds · Fog", "body": "<h3>Clouds</h3><p>Clouds are grouped by the height of their bases. <b>Low clouds</b> run from near the surface to about 6,500 feet AGL (stratus, stratocumulus, nimbostratus). <b>Middle clouds</b> run from about 6,500 to 20,000 feet and carry the prefix <b>alto-</b> (altostratus, altocumulus). <b>High clouds</b>, above about 20,000 feet, are made of ice crystals and carry the prefix <b>cirro-</b> (cirrus, cirrostratus, cirrocumulus). <b>Clouds with vertical development</b>, towering cumulus and cumulonimbus, can have low bases and tops above 50,000 feet.</p><p>The name tells you the shape: <b>stratus</b> means layered, <b>cumulus</b> means heaped, <b>nimbus</b> means it produces precipitation. A <b>cumulonimbus</b> is a thunderstorm. Towering cumulus are a warning that the air is unstable and storms may follow. Smooth, lens-shaped <b>lenticular</b> clouds over or downwind of mountains mark mountain wave (Day 32).</p><h3>Fog</h3><p><b>Fog</b> is a cloud whose base is within 50 feet of the ground. It forms when the air cools to its dew point or when moisture is added until the air saturates, so watch for a small and shrinking temperature/dew point spread. It can trap a VFR pilot quickly.</p><p><b>Radiation fog</b> forms on clear, calm or light-wind nights as the ground cools, usually in low spots; it is often shallow (\"ground fog\") and burns off as the sun heats the ground. <b>Advection fog</b> forms when moist air moves over a cooler surface, often along coasts; it needs some wind, can be widespread and deep, and can persist for days. <b>Upslope fog</b> forms when moist, stable air is pushed up sloping terrain and cools; it can also last a long time. <b>Steam fog</b> forms when cold air moves over warmer water and is often turbulent with icing possible. <b>Precipitation-induced fog</b> forms when relatively warm rain falls through cool air, usually ahead of warm fronts. <b>Ice fog</b> forms in very cold temperatures when water vapor turns directly into ice crystals.</p>", "words": 304, "figs": []}, {"id": "d31c2", "day": 31, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory III: Clouds, Fog, and Thunderstorms", "title": "Thunderstorm ingredients and life cycle · Kinds of thunderstorms", "body": "<h3>Thunderstorm ingredients and life cycle</h3><p>A thunderstorm needs three ingredients: <b>sufficient water vapor</b>, an <b>unstable lapse rate</b>, and an <b>initial lifting action</b> (heating of the ground, a front, terrain, or converging winds).</p><p>An ordinary storm cell goes through three stages, often in under an hour. In the <b>cumulus stage</b>, updrafts dominate throughout the cloud, building it upward; it may grow faster than a trainer can climb. The <b>mature stage</b> begins when <b>precipitation reaches the ground</b>. Falling rain drags air down, so there are strong <b>updrafts and downdrafts side by side</b>; this is the most violent stage, with the worst turbulence, lightning, hail, and the <b>gust front</b> of cold air spreading out ahead of the storm at the surface. In the <b>dissipating stage</b>, downdrafts dominate, the storm rains itself out, and the top often spreads into an <b>anvil</b> of ice crystals blown downwind.</p><h3>Kinds of thunderstorms</h3><p>A <b>single-cell</b> (air-mass) storm forms from daytime heating in moist, unstable air and usually lasts a short time. <b>Multicell</b> storms are clusters of cells at different stages, so the cluster lasts much longer than any one cell. A <b>supercell</b> is a single, very powerful rotating storm that can last for hours and produce large hail and tornadoes. A <b>squall line</b> is a line of storms, often well ahead of a cold front, and is one of the most intense hazards to aircraft. <b>Embedded thunderstorms</b> are hidden inside layers of other clouds, which is one more reason VFR pilots stay out of clouds.</p>", "words": 239, "figs": [{"day": 31, "sec": 2, "num": "31", "src": "img/fig31-tstorm.svg", "caption": "The three stages of an ordinary thunderstorm cell. The mature stage, with updrafts and downdrafts side by side, is the most dangerous.", "w": 480.0, "h": 300.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d31c3", "day": 31, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory III: Clouds, Fog, and Thunderstorms", "title": "Hazards and how to avoid them", "body": "<p>Thunderstorms bring severe turbulence (up and down drafts strong enough to damage an airplane), hail (which can be thrown out from the storm into clear air, even under the anvil), lightning, icing, heavy rain, low visibility, rapid pressure changes that make the altimeter unreliable, tornadoes, and violent wind shifts and wind shear at the surface. The AIM's advice includes: do not take off or land with a storm approaching; never fly under a thunderstorm, even if you can see through to the other side; avoid any storm identified as severe or giving an intense radar echo by at least <b>20 nautical miles</b>, especially under the anvil; and treat frequent lightning as a sign of a severe storm.</p><p><b>NEXRAD</b> weather radar shown in the cockpit through ADS-B or satellite can be many minutes old, so it is a strategic tool for staying far away, never a way to pick through gaps between cells.</p>", "words": 152, "figs": []}, {"id": "d32c1", "day": 32, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory IV: Icing, Turbulence, and Wind Shear", "title": "Structural icing: what it needs and why it hurts · Rime, clear, and mixed ice", "body": "<h3>Structural icing: what it needs and why it hurts</h3><p>Structural ice needs two things: <b>visible moisture</b> (cloud, rain, drizzle) and an airplane surface <b>at or below freezing</b>. Cloud droplets can stay liquid well below 0 °C; these <b>supercooled</b> droplets freeze the instant they hit the airplane. Ice adds weight, but worse, it spoils the wing's shape, <b>reducing lift, increasing drag, and raising stall speed</b>; it can also block the pitot tube and coat the windshield and propeller. Trainers are not certified for flight into known icing, and VFR pilots should not be in clouds anyway. The <b>freezing level</b> on forecasts and AIRMET Zulu tells you where the danger starts.</p><h3>Rime, clear, and mixed ice</h3><p><b>Rime ice</b> forms when <b>small</b> supercooled droplets freeze instantly on contact, trapping air. It is rough, milky, and opaque, builds on leading edges, and is typical of <b>stratiform</b> clouds. <b>Clear ice</b> forms when <b>large</b> droplets, as in <b>cumuliform</b> clouds or freezing rain, hit and flow back over the surface before freezing. It is glossy, hard to see, heavy, and hard to remove, so it is the more dangerous. <b>Mixed ice</b> is a combination of the two, for example when snow or ice pellets are caught in clear ice. As a general guide, clear ice is most common just below freezing, roughly 0 to −10 °C, and rime at colder temperatures, but either can form across a range.</p><p><b>Freezing rain</b> is the worst case: rain falling from a warmer layer aloft into below-freezing air near the ground, where it freezes on contact as clear ice. Its tell-tale is <b>ice pellets</b> at the surface, which mean freezing rain is happening above you and there is warmer air aloft. Ice intensity is reported as trace, light, moderate, or severe.</p>", "words": 276, "figs": []}, {"id": "d32c2", "day": 32, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory IV: Icing, Turbulence, and Wind Shear", "title": "Frost and induction ice · Turbulence", "body": "<h3>Frost and induction ice</h3><p><b>Frost</b> forms on a clear, cold night when the airplane's surface cools below freezing and below the dew point. Even frost as thin as sandpaper can disrupt airflow enough to prevent a normal takeoff, so remove all frost, snow, and ice before flight. <b>Induction icing</b>, such as carburetor ice (Day 12), can form in clear air well above freezing; it is a separate hazard from structural ice.</p><h3>Turbulence</h3><p><b>Convective turbulence</b> comes from rising warm air: thermals on sunny afternoons, worst low and under cumulus clouds, usually smooth above their tops. <b>Mechanical turbulence</b> comes from wind flowing over buildings, hills, and trees; it is worse with stronger wind and on the downwind (leeward) side of obstructions, often right on final approach. <b>Mountain wave</b> forms when strong winds blow roughly perpendicular across a ridge in stable air: the air flows in waves downwind for many miles with strong up and down drafts and violent <b>rotor</b> turbulence under the wave crests. Signs are lenticular clouds, rotor clouds, and a cap cloud over the ridge, though the air may be too dry for any cloud. Approach ridges at an angle so you can turn away, and cross with plenty of altitude. <b>Clear air turbulence</b> is high-altitude turbulence away from clouds, often near the jet stream.</p><p>Intensity terms you will see in PIREPs and forecasts: <b>light</b> (slight, erratic changes in altitude or attitude), <b>moderate</b> (greater changes but the airplane stays in positive control, usually with changes in airspeed), <b>severe</b> (large, abrupt changes; the airplane may be momentarily out of control), and <b>extreme</b> (violently tossed about; practically impossible to control, possible structural damage). In turbulence, slow to <b>V<sub>A</sub></b> or below (or the POH's turbulence speed), keep wings level, and accept altitude changes rather than fighting them.</p>", "words": 289, "figs": []}, {"id": "d32c3", "day": 32, "dayTitle": "Weather Theory IV: Icing, Turbulence, and Wind Shear", "title": "Wind shear, microbursts, and wake turbulence", "body": "<p><b>Wind shear</b> is a sudden change in wind speed or direction over a short distance. It is most dangerous near the ground, where a sudden loss of headwind drops your airspeed. Sources include thunderstorms, fronts, mechanical turbulence, and <b>temperature inversions</b>: on a calm, cold morning, the wind just above the inversion may be strong, so you can climb through a sudden shear a few hundred feet up. A <b>microburst</b> is a concentrated downdraft under a convective cloud, with downdrafts that can reach 6,000 feet per minute; it typically lasts 15 minutes or less. Flying through one, you first gain headwind and climb, then hit the downdraft, then a tailwind, and lose airspeed and altitude, a deadly sequence on approach or departure. Watch for virga (rain evaporating before it reaches the ground) and dust rings under convective clouds.</p><p><b>Wake turbulence</b> (Day 20) is man-made: wingtip vortices strongest behind airplanes that are <b>heavy, clean, and slow</b>. They sink below the generating airplane's flight path and drift with the wind; a light quartering tailwind is the worst case because it can hold them over the runway. Stay above a larger airplane's path and land beyond its touchdown point; take off before its rotation point. Helicopters in forward flight make vortices too, and a hovering one blows strong downwash in every direction.</p>", "words": 218, "figs": []}, {"id": "d33c1", "day": 33, "dayTitle": "Weather Products I: Briefings, METARs, and TAFs", "title": "Where to get weather", "body": "<p>You can get an official briefing from <b>Flight Service</b> (online at 1800wxbrief.com or by phone at 1-800-WX-BRIEF), from the National Weather Service's <b>aviationweather.gov</b>, or through an <b>EFB</b> app (electronic flight bag, such as ForeFlight or Garmin Pilot). Flight Service offers a <b>standard</b> briefing (full picture, for planning a flight), an <b>abbreviated</b> briefing (an update or specific items), and an <b>outlook</b> briefing (for a flight six or more hours away). A standard briefing starts with adverse conditions and any \"VFR flight not recommended\" statement, then a synopsis, current conditions, en route and destination forecasts, winds aloft, and NOTAMs. All times in aviation weather are in <b>UTC</b>, called <b>Zulu</b> (Z). Central Daylight Time is UTC minus 5 hours; Central Standard Time is UTC minus 6.</p>", "words": 123, "figs": []}, {"id": "d33c2", "day": 33, "dayTitle": "Weather Products I: Briefings, METARs, and TAFs", "title": "METAR: the current observation", "body": "<p>A <b>METAR</b> is a routine hourly weather observation at an airport; a <b>SPECI</b> is a special one issued when conditions change significantly. Example: <b>KMSP 031753Z 31012G20KT 10SM -RA BKN035 OVC060 14/09 A2992</b>. Read it left to right: Minneapolis, the 3rd of the month at 1753 Zulu; wind from 310 degrees <b>true</b> at 12 knots gusting 20; visibility 10 statute miles; light rain; broken clouds at 3,500 feet AGL and overcast at 6,000 (cloud heights are in hundreds of feet above ground); temperature 14 °C, dew point 9 °C; altimeter 29.92. The <b>ceiling</b> is the lowest broken or overcast layer (or vertical visibility into an obscuration), here 3,500 feet. Winds in written reports are true, while ATIS and tower winds are magnetic, matching the runway numbers.</p><p>Sky cover is in eighths of the sky: <b>FEW</b> 1/8 to 2/8, <b>SCT</b> 3/8 to 4/8, <b>BKN</b> 5/8 to 7/8, <b>OVC</b> 8/8. <b>VV</b> is vertical visibility into an obscuration such as fog (VV002 is a 200-foot ceiling), and <b>CLR</b> means no clouds below 12,000 feet at an automated station. Weather codes: intensity (<b>-</b> light, no sign moderate, <b>+</b> heavy, <b>VC</b> in the vicinity), descriptors (<b>SH</b> showers, <b>TS</b> thunderstorm, <b>FZ</b> freezing, <b>MI</b> shallow, <b>BC</b> patches), and the weather itself (<b>RA</b> rain, <b>DZ</b> drizzle, <b>SN</b> snow, <b>PL</b> ice pellets, <b>GR</b> hail, <b>BR</b> mist, <b>FG</b> fog, <b>HZ</b> haze, <b>FU</b> smoke). <b>AUTO</b> means a fully automated report. After <b>RMK</b> come remarks, such as AO2 (an automated station with a precipitation sensor), SLP (sea level pressure), and a T group with temperature and dew point to tenths of a degree.</p>", "words": 260, "figs": []}, {"id": "d33c3", "day": 33, "dayTitle": "Weather Products I: Briefings, METARs, and TAFs", "title": "Flight categories · TAF: the airport forecast", "body": "<h3>Flight categories</h3><p>Flight categories help at a glance: <b>VFR</b> is ceiling above 3,000 feet and visibility above 5 miles; <b>MVFR</b> (marginal VFR) is ceiling 1,000 to 3,000 feet and/or visibility 3 to 5 miles; <b>IFR</b> is ceiling 500 to below 1,000 and/or visibility 1 to less than 3; <b>LIFR</b> (low IFR) is ceiling below 500 and/or visibility below 1 mile. The worse of ceiling and visibility sets the category. These are labels, not legal minimums: \"MVFR\" can still be legal VFR, and \"VFR\" can still be a poor idea.</p><h3>TAF: the airport forecast</h3><p>A <b>TAF</b> (terminal aerodrome forecast) covers the area within 5 statute miles of the airport, usually for 24 or 30 hours, issued four times a day. Example: <b>KMSP 031720Z 0318/0424 31010KT P6SM SCT040 FM040200 VRB03KT P6SM SKC TEMPO 0410/0414 3SM BR</b>. Valid from the 3rd at 1800Z to the 4th at 2400Z; wind 310 at 10, visibility more than 6 miles (P6SM), scattered clouds at 4,000. <b>FM</b>040200 means from the 4th at 0200Z conditions change to: wind variable at 3, sky clear. <b>TEMPO</b> means temporary fluctuations lasting less than an hour at a time, here 3 miles in mist (BR) between 1000Z and 1400Z. <b>BECMG</b> means a gradual change during the stated period. <b>PROB30</b> or <b>PROB40</b> gives a 30 or 40 percent chance. <b>WS</b> in a TAF means low-level wind shear (not associated with thunderstorms) is forecast.</p>", "words": 224, "figs": []}, {"id": "d33c4", "day": 33, "dayTitle": "Weather Products I: Briefings, METARs, and TAFs", "title": "Second worked pair: a cold front day", "body": "<p>Try these yourself first. METAR: <b>KDSM 041454Z 18008KT 3SM -SHRA BR BKN014 OVC025 17/15 A2988 RMK AO2 SLP115 T01720150</b>. Decode: Des Moines, the 4th at 1454Z (9:54 a.m. CDT); wind 180 true at 8 knots; 3 miles in light rain showers and mist; broken at 1,400 feet (the ceiling) and overcast at 2,500; temperature 17 °C, dew point 15 °C (a 2-degree spread); altimeter 29.88. Remarks: automated station with precipitation sensor, sea level pressure 1011.5 hectopascals, temperature 17.2 °C and dew point 15.0 °C. Category: ceiling 1,400 and visibility 3 are both <b>MVFR</b>.</p><p>TAF: <b>KDSM 041130Z 0412/0512 17010KT 5SM -SHRA BKN020 TEMPO 0414/0418 2SM TSRA BKN010CB FM042000 29015G25KT P6SM SCT040 BECMG 0502/0504 32008KT</b>. Decode: issued the 4th at 1130Z, valid from 1200Z on the 4th to 1200Z on the 5th. Initially wind 170 at 10, 5 miles in light rain showers, broken at 2,000. Temporarily between 1400Z and 1800Z (9 a.m. to 1 p.m. CDT): 2 miles in thunderstorms with moderate rain, broken at 1,000 with cumulonimbus. From 2000Z (3 p.m. CDT): wind 290 at 15 gusting 25, visibility more than 6 miles, scattered at 4,000. That wind shift from south to northwest, with clearing, is the cold front passing. Becoming between 0200Z and 0400Z on the 5th: wind 320 at 8.</p>", "words": 212, "figs": []}, {"id": "d34c1", "day": 34, "dayTitle": "Weather Products II: Advisories, PIREPs, Charts, and Winds Aloft", "title": "Big-picture forecasts and observations", "body": "<h3>Big-picture forecasts and observations</h3><p>The <b>GFA</b> (Graphical Forecasts for Aviation) on aviationweather.gov shows observed and forecast clouds, weather, winds, icing, and turbulence on interactive maps, and it replaced the old text area forecast. The <b>surface analysis chart</b> shows current fronts, highs and lows, and isobars. <b>Prog charts</b> (prognostic charts) show forecast fronts, pressure systems, and precipitation for the hours and days ahead. <b>Radar</b> (NEXRAD) shows precipitation, which marks thunderstorms; remember that in-cockpit radar can be several minutes old. <b>Satellite</b> imagery shows clouds: visible images work only in daylight, while infrared images show cloud-top temperature day and night, with the coldest (brightest) tops being the highest.</p><h3>Advisories: AIRMET, SIGMET, convective SIGMET, CWA</h3><p><b>AIRMETs</b> warn of weather hazardous mainly to light aircraft and are issued every 6 hours: <b>Sierra</b> for IFR conditions (ceilings below 1,000 feet and/or visibility below 3 miles over a large area) and mountain obscuration; <b>Tango</b> for moderate turbulence, sustained surface winds of 30 knots or more, and low-level wind shear; <b>Zulu</b> for moderate icing and freezing levels. <b>SIGMETs</b> warn of hazards to all aircraft and are valid up to 4 hours: severe icing not associated with thunderstorms, severe or extreme turbulence or clear air turbulence not associated with thunderstorms, dust storms or sandstorms lowering visibility below 3 miles, and volcanic ash.</p><p><b>Convective SIGMETs</b> are issued hourly (at 55 minutes past the hour) and are valid for 2 hours. They cover severe thunderstorms (surface winds of 50 knots or more, hail of 3/4 inch or more at the surface, or tornadoes), embedded thunderstorms, lines of thunderstorms, and areas of heavy precipitation from thunderstorms. Any convective SIGMET implies severe or greater turbulence, severe icing, and low-level wind shear. A <b>CWA</b> (Center Weather Advisory) is a short-term warning from an air route traffic control center's weather unit about conditions happening now or within 2 hours.</p>", "words": 294, "figs": []}, {"id": "d34c2", "day": 34, "dayTitle": "Weather Products II: Advisories, PIREPs, Charts, and Winds Aloft", "title": "PIREPs · Winds and temperatures aloft", "body": "<h3>PIREPs</h3><p><b>PIREPs</b> are pilot reports of actual conditions (UA is routine, UUA urgent). They are the only direct report of cloud tops, icing, and turbulence, so read them and give them. Example: <b>UA /OV DSM270015/TM 1645/FL045/TP C172/SK BKN030-TOP055/TA M02/TB LGT/IC LGT RIME/RM IN CLOUDS IFR</b>. Decode: routine report; location 15 nm on the 270 radial (west) of the Des Moines VOR; time 1645Z; altitude 4,500 feet MSL; aircraft type a Cessna 172; sky broken, base 3,000 MSL, tops 5,500; temperature −2 °C; light turbulence; light rime icing; remark: in clouds on an IFR flight. Altitudes in PIREPs are MSL. Give a PIREP through Flight Service or ATC whenever you meet something unexpected, or something good, like smooth air above a layer.</p><h3>Winds and temperatures aloft</h3><p>The winds and temperatures aloft forecast (<b>FB</b>) gives wind direction (true, to the nearest 10 degrees), speed in knots, and temperature in °C at set altitudes: 3,000, 6,000, 9,000, 12,000, 18,000 feet and higher. <b>2714-08</b> at 6,000 feet means 270 degrees at 14 knots, minus 8 °C. <b>9900</b> means light and variable (less than 5 knots). No winds are forecast within 1,500 feet of the station's elevation, and no temperature is given at the 3,000-foot level or for a level within 2,500 feet of the station. Above 24,000 feet temperatures are always below zero, so the minus sign is left off.</p><p>If the direction number is 51 to 86, the wind is 100 knots or more: subtract 50 from the direction and add 100 to the speed (<b>7319</b> means 230 degrees at 119 knots). For a cruise altitude between levels, <b>interpolate</b>. With 6,000 at <b>2715+05</b> and 9,000 at <b>2825+00</b>, at 7,500 feet (halfway) use about 275 degrees at 20 knots and +2 or +3 °C. You will use these winds on Day 35 for planning.</p>", "words": 295, "figs": []}, {"id": "d35c1", "day": 35, "dayTitle": "Navigation I: Charts, Pilotage, and Dead Reckoning", "title": "Charts and units · Pilotage and dead reckoning", "body": "<h3>Charts and units</h3><p>Position on earth is given in <b>latitude</b> (north-south, measured in degrees from the equator) and <b>longitude</b> (east-west, from the prime meridian). One minute of latitude equals one <b>nautical mile</b>, about 6,076 feet or 1.15 statute miles. Aviation speeds are in <b>knots</b>, nautical miles per hour. On the sectional, you draw a line from departure to destination and measure its <b>true course</b> with a <b>plotter</b> (a clear ruler with a protractor), measured from true north at a meridian (a north-south longitude line) near the middle of the route.</p><h3>Pilotage and dead reckoning</h3><p><b>Pilotage</b> is navigating by looking at landmarks on the ground and matching them to the chart: a highway, a lake, a town, a tower. You pick <b>checkpoints</b> every 10 nm or so along the line. <b>Dead reckoning</b> is navigating by calculation: from your known heading, speed, and time, you work out where you should be. Real VFR flying uses both. You fly the planned heading, time each checkpoint, and correct as you go.</p><h3>From true course to compass heading</h3><p>The wind pushes you sideways, so you point the nose slightly into it. That angle is the <b>wind correction angle</b> (WCA), found with an <b>E6B</b> flight computer or app using the forecast winds aloft. True course plus or minus WCA gives <b>true heading</b> (TH). Wind from the right means a right correction (add); wind from the left means a left correction (subtract).</p><p>Next, convert true to magnetic using the <b>variation</b> printed on the chart's isogonic lines: <b>\"East is least, west is best\"</b>, meaning subtract easterly variation and add westerly variation. That gives <b>magnetic heading</b> (MH). Finally, apply the <b>deviation</b> from the compass correction card to get <b>compass heading</b> (CH). The chain: <b>TC ± WCA = TH ± Var = MH ± Dev = CH</b>. The same wind calculation gives your <b>groundspeed</b>.</p>", "words": 291, "figs": []}, {"id": "d35c2", "day": 35, "dayTitle": "Navigation I: Charts, Pilotage, and Dead Reckoning", "title": "Time, fuel, and the rules", "body": "<p>Time equals distance divided by groundspeed. Fuel equals time multiplied by fuel burn per hour. Add fuel for taxi, climb, and reserve. The legal reserve for VFR (91.151): enough to fly to the first point of intended landing and then at normal cruise for <b>30 minutes during the day</b> and <b>45 minutes at night</b>. Most instructors recommend at least an hour as a personal minimum.</p><p>On cruise altitude, 91.159 says that above 3,000 feet AGL, on a <b>magnetic course</b> of 0 to 179 degrees fly at <b>odd thousands plus 500</b> (3,500, 5,500), and on 180 to 359 degrees fly at <b>even thousands plus 500</b> (4,500, 6,500). Finally, file a <b>VFR flight plan</b> with Flight Service and remember to open it after departure and close it on arrival; if you have not closed it within 30 minutes after your estimated arrival time, search procedures begin.</p>", "words": 144, "figs": []}, {"id": "d36c1", "day": 36, "dayTitle": "Navigation II: VOR, GPS, Lost Procedures, and Diversions", "title": "How a VOR works · Reading the VOR indicator · GPS", "body": "<h3>How a VOR works</h3><p>A <b>VOR</b> (VHF omnidirectional range) is a ground station that broadcasts signals on 108.00 to 117.95 MHz. Your receiver can tell which <b>radial</b> you are on. Radials are lines going <b>outward from</b> the station, named by their magnetic direction: the 090 radial runs east from the station. Like all VHF signals, VOR reception is <b>line of sight</b>, so it improves with altitude. Always <b>identify</b> the station by listening to its Morse code before using it.</p><h3>Reading the VOR indicator</h3><p>The indicator has an <b>OBS</b> (omnibearing selector, a knob that sets a course), a <b>CDI</b> (course deviation indicator, a needle), and a <b>TO/FROM</b> flag. Turn the OBS until the needle centers. If the flag says <b>FROM</b>, you are on the radial shown. If it says <b>TO</b>, the course shown will take you to the station. Each dot of needle deflection on a typical VOR is 2 degrees, with full deflection at 10 degrees or more.</p><p>The VOR does not know which way the airplane is pointing; it only knows where you are. To fly toward a station, center the needle with a TO flag and fly that heading, correcting toward the needle. When you pass over the station, the flag flips from TO to FROM. (For IFR flight, 91.171 requires a VOR accuracy check within the preceding 30 days, but it is not required for VFR.)</p><h3>GPS</h3><p>Most trainers now have <b>GPS</b>, and an EFB on a tablet adds a moving map. GPS is wonderful for situational awareness, but it can fail, lose signal, or be programmed wrong. Keep your paper or electronic chart work and your pilotage skills sharp, and always know roughly where you are without the magenta line. The checkride will test navigation without relying entirely on GPS.</p>", "words": 285, "figs": []}, {"id": "d36c2", "day": 36, "dayTitle": "Navigation II: VOR, GPS, Lost Procedures, and Diversions", "title": "When you are lost · Diverting", "body": "<h3>When you are lost</h3><p>It happens to everyone eventually. A common memory aid is the <b>five Cs</b>: <b>Climb</b> (to see farther and improve radio and VOR reception, staying within weather and airspace limits), <b>Communicate</b> (call ATC or Flight Service; they can help), <b>Confess</b> (tell them you are unsure of your position), <b>Comply</b> (follow their help), and <b>Conserve</b> (reduce power to save fuel, and make decisions while you still have options). Meanwhile, look for large features: highways, rivers, cities, and compare them with the chart. Do not wander aimlessly.</p><h3>Diverting</h3><p>Weather, fuel, a sick passenger, or a rough-running engine can all justify a <b>diversion</b> to another airport. Decide early. Pick a suitable airport, turn toward it right away using a rough heading (from the chart, GPS, or by estimating), note the time, then refine: estimate distance, time, and fuel, check the airport information, and tell ATC or Flight Service. The examiner will give you a diversion on the checkride and watch for prompt, organized decisions.</p>", "words": 159, "figs": []}, {"id": "d37c1", "day": 37, "dayTitle": "Regulations I: You as Pilot in Command", "title": "91.3 and 91.103: the two big ones · Student pilot limits", "body": "<h3>91.3 and 91.103: the two big ones</h3><p><b>14 CFR 91.3</b> says the pilot in command is <b>directly responsible for, and is the final authority as to, the operation of the aircraft</b>. In an <b>in-flight emergency</b> requiring immediate action, the PIC may deviate from any rule in Part 91 to the extent needed to meet that emergency, and must send a written report to the FAA if asked.</p><p><b>14 CFR 91.103</b>, preflight action, says that before every flight the PIC must become familiar with <b>all available information</b> concerning that flight. For any flight that is not in the vicinity of an airport (and for any IFR flight), that includes <b>weather reports and forecasts, fuel requirements, alternatives</b> if the flight cannot be completed as planned, and <b>known traffic delays</b> from ATC. For every flight, it includes <b>runway lengths</b> at airports of intended use and <b>takeoff and landing distance</b> information. A common memory aid is <b>NWKRAFT</b>: NOTAMs, Weather, Known ATC delays, Runway lengths, Alternatives, Fuel requirements, Takeoff and landing distances.</p><h3>Student pilot limits</h3><p>As a student pilot (61.89) you may not carry passengers, fly for compensation or hire, or fly in furtherance of a business. You may not fly solo with flight or surface visibility of less than <b>3 statute miles during the day or 5 statute miles at night</b>, or when you cannot see the surface. Every solo, solo cross-country, solo flight to another nearby airport, and solo flight in Class B airspace requires specific <b>instructor endorsements</b> in your logbook. Your instructor will set additional limits; follow them.</p>", "words": 246, "figs": []}, {"id": "d37c2", "day": 37, "dayTitle": "Regulations I: You as Pilot in Command", "title": "What you carry · Staying current · Medical certificates", "body": "<h3>What you carry</h3><p>When acting as pilot you must have with you your <b>pilot certificate</b>, your <b>medical certificate</b> (or BasicMed documentation), and a <b>government-issued photo ID</b> such as a driver's license (61.3). If you move, notify the FAA of your new address within <b>30 days</b> (61.60) to keep exercising your privileges. You must log the flight time used to meet certificate, rating, and currency requirements (61.51).</p><h3>Staying current</h3><p>A <b>flight review</b> (61.56) is required every <b>24 calendar months</b>: at least 1 hour of ground training and 1 hour of flight training with an instructor. Passing a checkride for a new certificate or rating counts. \"Calendar month\" means the deadline runs to the end of the month: a review done March 10, 2026 is good through March 31, 2028.</p><p>To carry <b>passengers</b> (61.57), you must have made <b>3 takeoffs and 3 landings within the preceding 90 days</b> in the same category, class, and type (if a type rating is required). In a tailwheel airplane those landings must be to a full stop. To carry passengers <b>at night</b>, those 3 takeoffs and landings must be <b>to a full stop</b> during the period beginning <b>1 hour after sunset and ending 1 hour before sunrise</b>. Note the different night definitions: you must turn on position lights from <b>sunset to sunrise</b> (91.209), and night time for logging is from the end of evening civil twilight to the beginning of morning civil twilight (61.1).</p><h3>Medical certificates</h3><p>For private pilot privileges, a third-class medical lasts <b>60 calendar months</b> if you were under 40 at the time of the exam, and <b>24 calendar months</b> if you were 40 or older (61.23). First- and second-class medicals also cover private privileges for those same periods. <b>BasicMed</b> is an alternative with its own limits, available only to pilots who have held an FAA medical at some point after July 14, 2006, so it is not a way to start training without one. Regardless of what certificate you hold, 61.53 forbids acting as pilot while you know or have reason to know of a medical condition that would make you unable to meet the medical requirements.</p>", "words": 347, "figs": []}, {"id": "d38c1", "day": 38, "dayTitle": "Regulations II: Is the Airplane Legal?", "title": "Airworthiness is your call · Documents aboard: ARROW · Required inspections", "body": "<h3>Airworthiness is your call</h3><p>Under <b>91.7</b>, no one may operate a civil aircraft unless it is in an airworthy condition, and the PIC is responsible for determining whether it is in condition for safe flight. If something unairworthy happens in flight (mechanical, electrical, or structural), you must discontinue the flight. In practice: a good preflight, a review of the maintenance records, and the confidence to say \"not today.\"</p><h3>Documents aboard: ARROW</h3><p>The aide-memoire <b>ARROW</b> covers the required papers. <b>A</b>irworthiness certificate, displayed where passengers or crew can see it. <b>R</b>egistration certificate (now valid for 7 years). <b>R</b>adio station license, required only for international flights. <b>O</b>perating limitations, found in the approved flight manual or POH, placards, and instrument markings. <b>W</b>eight and balance data for that specific airplane. (Your own certificate, medical, and ID are on top of this.)</p><h3>Required inspections</h3><p>Check the airplane's maintenance logbooks for these. An <b>annual inspection</b> within the preceding <b>12 calendar months</b> (91.409). A <b>100-hour inspection</b> if the airplane carries anyone for hire or is provided by an instructor or school for flight instruction for hire; the 100-hour limit may be exceeded by up to 10 hours only to reach a place where the inspection can be done. A <b>transponder</b> check within <b>24 calendar months</b> (91.413). An <b>ELT</b> (emergency locator transmitter, a beacon that activates in a crash) inspection within <b>12 calendar months</b>, with the battery replaced after 1 hour of cumulative use or when half its useful life has expired (91.207). The <b>altimeter and static system</b> check every 24 calendar months matters only for IFR. Plus compliance with <b>airworthiness directives</b> (ADs), which are mandatory safety fixes issued by the FAA. A memory aid many use is <b>AV1ATE</b>: Annual, VOR check (IFR only), 100-hour, Altimeter/pitot-static, Transponder, ELT.</p>", "words": 280, "figs": []}, {"id": "d38c2", "day": 38, "dayTitle": "Regulations II: Is the Airplane Legal?", "title": "Required equipment · Inoperative equipment, maintenance, and accidents", "body": "<h3>Required equipment</h3><p>For day VFR, 91.205 requires a set of instruments and equipment, often remembered as <b>A TOMATO FLAMES</b>: Altimeter, Tachometer for each engine, Oil pressure gauge, Manifold pressure gauge for each altitude engine, Airspeed indicator, Temperature gauge for each liquid-cooled engine, Oil temperature gauge for each air-cooled engine, Fuel gauge for each tank, Landing gear position indicator (if retractable), Anticollision lights (for airplanes certified after March 11, 1996), Magnetic direction indicator (compass), ELT, Safety belts (and shoulder harnesses for front seats in airplanes made after July 18, 1978). For night, add <b>FLAPS</b>: Fuses (a spare set, if fuses are used), Landing light (if operating for hire), Anticollision lights, Position lights, and a Source of electrical power.</p><h3>Inoperative equipment, maintenance, and accidents</h3><p>If something is broken and the airplane has no approved minimum equipment list, 91.213(d) lets you fly only if the item is not required by the airplane's type certificate or equipment list, by 91.205, or by an AD; and the item is removed or deactivated and placarded \"Inoperative,\" with a determination by a pilot or mechanic that it does not create a hazard. As a pilot you may perform certain <b>preventive maintenance</b> (Part 43 lists items such as changing oil or servicing tires) on an airplane you own or operate, and log it.</p><p>If there is an <b>accident</b> (death, serious injury, or substantial damage) or certain serious incidents such as an in-flight fire or flight control malfunction, NTSB Part 830 requires <b>immediate notification</b> to the NTSB, and a written report within <b>10 days</b> after an accident.</p>", "words": 251, "figs": []}, {"id": "d39c1", "day": 39, "dayTitle": "Regulations III: Rules of the Air", "title": "Right-of-way (91.113) · Minimum safe altitudes (91.119)", "body": "<h3>Right-of-way (91.113)</h3><p>First, the overarching duty: when weather permits, every pilot must <b>see and avoid</b> other aircraft, whether IFR or VFR. Then the rules. An aircraft <b>in distress</b> has the right-of-way over all others. When two aircraft of the same category are <b>converging</b>, the one <b>on the right</b> has the right-of-way. When they are of different categories, the less maneuverable one wins: a <b>balloon</b> has right-of-way over everything, a <b>glider</b> over an airship, airplane, or rotorcraft, and an <b>airship</b> over an airplane or rotorcraft. An aircraft towing or refueling another aircraft has right-of-way over other engine-driven aircraft.</p><p><b>Head-on</b>: each pilot alters course to the <b>right</b>. <b>Overtaking</b>: the aircraft being overtaken has the right-of-way, and the overtaking pilot passes well clear on the right. <b>Landing</b>: aircraft on final or landing have right-of-way over those in flight or on the surface; when two are approaching to land, the <b>lower</b> one has the right-of-way, but must not use that rule to cut in front of another aircraft that is on final.</p><h3>Minimum safe altitudes (91.119)</h3><p>Except for takeoff and landing, you must fly: <b>anywhere</b>, high enough that if the engine fails you can make an emergency landing without undue hazard to people or property; over a <b>congested area</b> (a city, town, or open-air gathering), at least <b>1,000 feet above the highest obstacle within 2,000 feet horizontally</b>; over <b>other than congested areas</b>, at least <b>500 feet above the surface</b>; and over open water or sparsely populated areas, no closer than <b>500 feet</b> to any person, vessel, vehicle, or structure. Also, 91.13 prohibits operating in a <b>careless or reckless</b> manner.</p>", "words": 259, "figs": []}, {"id": "d39c2", "day": 39, "dayTitle": "Regulations III: Rules of the Air", "title": "Alcohol and drugs (91.17) · Passengers, oxygen, and parachutes", "body": "<h3>Alcohol and drugs (91.17)</h3><p>No one may act as a crewmember within <b>8 hours</b> after drinking alcohol (\"8 hours bottle to throttle\"), while <b>under the influence</b>, with a blood or breath alcohol concentration of <b>0.04 or greater</b>, or while using any drug that affects your faculties in a way contrary to safety. You may not allow an obviously intoxicated person aboard, except a medical patient under proper care or in an emergency. A DUI or similar motor vehicle action must be reported to the FAA within <b>60 days</b> (61.15). Many pilots wait well beyond 8 hours, because a hangover is its own impairment.</p><h3>Passengers, oxygen, and parachutes</h3><p>The PIC must <b>brief passengers</b> on how to fasten and unfasten seat belts and shoulder harnesses and make sure they are buckled for taxi, takeoff, and landing (91.107); a child under 2 may be held by an adult. Supplemental <b>oxygen</b> (91.211): above <b>12,500 feet MSL up to 14,000</b>, the required crew must use oxygen for any time beyond 30 minutes at those altitudes; above <b>14,000</b>, the crew must use it the entire time; above <b>15,000</b>, each occupant must be provided with oxygen.</p><p>If any passenger is aboard (other than a crewmember), each occupant must wear an approved <b>parachute</b> for any intentional maneuver exceeding <b>60 degrees of bank or 30 degrees nose-up or nose-down</b>, unless it is flight training given by an instructor (91.307).</p><h3>A few more that show up</h3><p>You may drop objects only if you take reasonable precautions to avoid injury or damage (91.15). <b>Position lights</b> are required from sunset to sunrise (91.209). At nontowered airports, turns in the pattern are to the <b>left</b> unless markings or published procedures say otherwise (91.126). And the VFR fuel reserve (30 minutes day, 45 night) and VFR cruising altitudes from Day 35 are regulations too (91.151 and 91.159).</p>", "words": 289, "figs": []}, {"id": "d40c1", "day": 40, "dayTitle": "Aeromedical Factors", "title": "Hypoxia and hyperventilation · Carbon monoxide", "body": "<h3>Hypoxia and hyperventilation</h3><p><b>Hypoxia</b> is a lack of oxygen in the body's tissues. <b>Hypoxic hypoxia</b> comes from thin air at altitude. <b>Hypemic hypoxia</b> is when the blood cannot carry enough oxygen, for example from carbon monoxide or anemia. <b>Stagnant hypoxia</b> is poor circulation, such as from high G or cold. <b>Histotoxic hypoxia</b> is when cells cannot use oxygen, as from alcohol or some drugs. Symptoms creep up and feel pleasant, which is the danger: euphoria, headache, slowed thinking, poor judgment, blurred or tunnel vision, and bluish lips or fingernails. The fix is oxygen and descending.</p><p><b>Hyperventilation</b> is breathing too fast or deep, usually from stress, which blows off too much carbon dioxide. It feels similar to hypoxia: tingling, dizziness, lightheadedness. The fix is to slow your breathing deliberately; talking out loud helps.</p><h3>Carbon monoxide</h3><p>Carbon monoxide (CO) is a colorless, odorless gas in engine exhaust. It can enter the cabin through a cracked exhaust in the cabin heat system (Day 13). It binds to your blood far more readily than oxygen (over 200 times), causing hypemic hypoxia. Symptoms: headache, drowsiness, dizziness, nausea. If you suspect it, <b>turn off the cabin heat, open the fresh air vents, and land</b>. A CO detector is cheap insurance.</p>", "words": 199, "figs": []}, {"id": "d40c2", "day": 40, "dayTitle": "Aeromedical Factors", "title": "Spatial disorientation and illusions", "body": "<p>Your inner ear senses motion, but it is easily fooled, especially without a visible horizon. It cannot detect a slow, steady turn, so after a gradual bank you may feel level. Rolling level abruptly then gives the <b>leans</b>, a feeling of banking the other way. In a <b>graveyard spiral</b>, a pilot in a descending turn feels level, sees the altimeter unwind, pulls back, and only tightens the spiral. Moving your head sharply in a turn can produce the tumbling <b>Coriolis illusion</b>. Rapid acceleration can feel like a climb (somatogravic illusion), tempting you to push the nose down. The answer to all of them: if you lose outside references, <b>trust the instruments</b>, and as a VFR pilot, do not fly into clouds or dark, featureless conditions in the first place.</p><p>Approach illusions matter too. A <b>narrow runway</b> or an <b>upsloping runway</b> makes you feel high, so you tend to fly a low approach. A <b>wide runway</b> or a <b>downsloping runway</b> makes you feel low, so you tend to fly high. Featureless terrain, water, or rain on the windscreen can make you feel higher than you are. Visual glide slope lights (Day 20) are the cure.</p>", "words": 194, "figs": []}, {"id": "d40c3", "day": 40, "dayTitle": "Aeromedical Factors", "title": "Night vision and scanning · Everyday factors", "body": "<h3>Night vision and scanning</h3><p>The eye has <b>cones</b> in the center, for color and detail in good light, and <b>rods</b> around them, for dim light. At night the center of your vision is weak, so use <b>off-center viewing</b>: look slightly to the side of an object to see it. Full dark adaptation takes about <b>30 minutes</b>, and one bright light can undo it, so use dim red or white cockpit lighting. Oxygen deficiency affects night vision at lower altitudes than other effects, which is one more reason to stay rested and sober.</p><p>To spot traffic in daytime, scan in a series of short, regularly spaced eye movements, each bringing about <b>10 degrees</b> of sky into view, pausing about a second on each. A target that has no apparent motion in your windshield but is getting bigger is the one on a collision course.</p><h3>Everyday factors</h3><p><b>Fatigue</b>, <b>dehydration</b>, <b>stress</b>, and <b>medications</b> cause more trouble than exotic illness. Many over-the-counter medicines, like some antihistamines, cause drowsiness; check with an AME, and when in doubt, do not fly. Flying with a cold can block your ears or sinuses on descent, which can be very painful. After <b>scuba diving</b>, wait at least 12 hours before flying up to 8,000 feet, and at least 24 hours before flying above 8,000 feet or after any dive that required a controlled ascent, to avoid decompression sickness.</p>", "words": 223, "figs": []}, {"id": "d41c1", "day": 41, "dayTitle": "ADM, Risk Management, and Emergencies", "title": "Checking yourself: IMSAFE · Finding hazards: PAVE", "body": "<h3>Checking yourself: IMSAFE</h3><p>Before every flight, run <b>IMSAFE</b> on yourself: <b>I</b>llness, <b>M</b>edication, <b>S</b>tress, <b>A</b>lcohol, <b>F</b>atigue, and <b>E</b>motion (some versions say Eating). A \"yes, but\" on any item is a reason to reconsider. You are allowed to cancel; examiners respect pilots who do.</p><h3>Finding hazards: PAVE</h3><p><b>PAVE</b> sorts risks into four groups. <b>P</b>ilot: your health, currency, and experience. <b>A</b>ircraft: its condition, fuel, performance, and equipment. en<b>V</b>ironment: weather, terrain, airports, airspace, and day or night. <b>E</b>xternal pressures: schedules, passengers waiting, the urge to get home. External pressures are the quiet killer, the \"get-there-itis\" that pushes pilots to continue into weather. Set <b>personal minimums</b> (for ceiling, visibility, wind, and crosswind) on the ground, when you are calm, and stick to them in the air.</p><h3>Making decisions: 3P and DECIDE</h3><p>The FAA's <b>3P model</b> is a loop you run all flight: <b>Perceive</b> the hazards, <b>Process</b> them to judge the risk, and <b>Perform</b> by reducing or eliminating the risk, then start again. The longer <b>DECIDE</b> model is: <b>D</b>etect a change, <b>E</b>stimate the need to react, <b>C</b>hoose a desirable outcome, <b>I</b>dentify actions to get there, <b>D</b>o the action, and <b>E</b>valuate the result. Use whichever sticks; the point is a habit of deliberate thinking.</p><h3>Five hazardous attitudes</h3><p>The FAA identifies five attitudes that lead to bad decisions, each with an antidote to say to yourself. <b>Anti-authority</b> (\"Don't tell me\"): <i>Follow the rules; they are usually right.</i> <b>Impulsivity</b> (\"Do it quickly\"): <i>Not so fast; think first.</i> <b>Invulnerability</b> (\"It won't happen to me\"): <i>It could happen to me.</i> <b>Macho</b> (\"I can do it\"): <i>Taking chances is foolish.</i> <b>Resignation</b> (\"What's the use?\"): <i>I'm not helpless; I can make a difference.</i> The knowledge test loves to describe a pilot's thinking and ask which attitude it shows.</p>", "words": 271, "figs": []}, {"id": "d41c2", "day": 41, "dayTitle": "ADM, Risk Management, and Emergencies", "title": "When things go wrong", "body": "<p>If the engine fails in flight, remember <b>ABC</b>. <b>A</b>irspeed: pitch for best glide speed immediately and trim. <b>B</b>est field: pick a landing spot within gliding distance, ideally into the wind. <b>C</b>hecklist: run the engine failure checklist (fuel selector, mixture, carb heat, magnetos, primer locked) to try a restart. If it will not restart, set up the landing, tell someone on the radio (the current frequency or 121.5), squawk 7700, and secure the airplane before touchdown per the POH. If the engine fails right after takeoff at low altitude, lower the nose immediately and land mostly straight ahead; turning back to the runway from low altitude is a well-known killer.</p><p>On the radio, <b>\"Mayday, Mayday, Mayday\"</b> declares distress (grave and imminent danger) and <b>\"Pan-pan\"</b> declares urgency. Declaring an emergency gets you priority and help; paperwork is rarely an issue. The 91.3 authority from Day 37 is there for exactly these moments.</p>", "words": 150, "figs": []}, {"id": "d42c1", "day": 42, "dayTitle": "Your Knowledge Test and Checkride Oral Preview", "title": "The knowledge test · How the ACS is organized · What to bring to the checkride", "body": "<h3>The knowledge test</h3><p>The private pilot airplane knowledge test is a computer-based, multiple-choice exam at an FAA-approved testing center. It has <b>60 questions</b>, you have <b>2.5 hours</b>, and the passing score is <b>70 percent</b>. To take it you need an instructor's endorsement (or proof of completing an approved home-study course) and an FAA Tracking Number from <b>IACRA</b>, the FAA's online application system. Your result is valid for <b>24 calendar months</b>, so you must pass the checkride within that window. The test report lists codes for any topics you missed; your instructor reviews them with you before the checkride.</p><h3>How the ACS is organized</h3><p>The <b>Private Pilot Airplane ACS</b> (the FAA's Airman Certification Standards) is the rulebook for both exams. It is divided into <b>Areas of Operation</b>, such as Preflight Preparation, Preflight Procedures, Airport Operations, Takeoffs and Landings, Performance and Ground Reference Maneuvers, Navigation, Slow Flight and Stalls, Basic Instrument Maneuvers, Emergency Operations, Night Operations, and Postflight Procedures. Each area contains <b>Tasks</b>, and each task lists <b>knowledge</b>, <b>risk management</b>, and <b>skill</b> elements. Read it; the examiner will. Most of the oral comes from Area I, Preflight Preparation: pilot qualifications, airworthiness, weather, cross-country planning, the airspace system, performance and limitations, systems, and human factors. You studied every one of those in this course.</p><h3>What to bring to the checkride</h3><p>Expect to need: a completed application in IACRA; your <b>student pilot certificate</b>, <b>medical</b>, and <b>photo ID</b>; your <b>knowledge test report</b>; your <b>logbook</b> with the required aeronautical experience (Day 2) and endorsements, including the instructor's sign-off that you received the 3 hours of test prep within the preceding 2 calendar months and are prepared for the test; and the <b>airplane's documents and maintenance logbooks</b> to show it is airworthy (Day 38). The examiner usually assigns a cross-country flight to plan in advance, so bring your completed navigation log, weather briefing, and weight and balance.</p>", "words": 296, "figs": []}, {"id": "d42c2", "day": 42, "dayTitle": "Your Knowledge Test and Checkride Oral Preview", "title": "How the oral works", "body": "<p>The oral is a conversation, not an interrogation, and it is usually <b>scenario-based</b>: \"You are taking two friends to this airport on Saturday. Walk me through your planning.\" From that one scenario come questions on currency, airworthiness, weather, airspace, performance, and decision-making. The examiner wants to see <b>correlation</b>: that you can apply knowledge to a real flight, not just recite it.</p><p>Tips: answer the question asked, then stop. Use your references (the FAR/AIM, POH, Chart Supplement, chart legend); knowing <b>where to look something up</b> is a skill the ACS values. If you do not know, say so, then say how you would find out. And bring the same decision-making from Day 41: when the examiner adds a twist, like worsening weather, choosing the conservative option is often exactly the right answer.</p>", "words": 131, "figs": []}, {"id": "d43c1", "day": 43, "dayTitle": "Reading NOTAMs", "title": "What a NOTAM is · The anatomy of a NOTAM line", "body": "<h3>What a NOTAM is</h3><p>A <b>NOTAM</b> (Notice to Air Missions) is a time-critical notice about something that changed and is not yet on your charts: a closed runway, a crane near the approach path, a navigation aid out of service, no fuel, or restricted airspace. You get them in your Flight Service briefing, on the FAA's NOTAM Search website, and in EFB apps. A runway closed by NOTAM is closed whether or not you read it, so reading them is part of every preflight, not an optional extra.</p><h3>The anatomy of a NOTAM line</h3><p>Most NOTAMs a student sees follow the same left-to-right order. First comes an exclamation mark and the <b>accountability location</b> (the facility that issued it), then the <b>NOTAM number</b> (month and sequence, such as 10/045). Next is the <b>affected location</b>, usually an airport identifier, then a <b>keyword</b> that tells you the category, then the <b>condition</b> in abbreviated plain language. Last come the <b>effective times</b>.</p><p>The times are in <b>UTC (Zulu)</b>, written as ten digits: <b>year, month, day, hour, minute</b> (YYMMDDHHMM). So 2610101300 means 2026, October 10, 1300Z. Convert to local time yourself: Central Daylight Time is UTC minus 5 hours, Central Standard Time is UTC minus 6. Some NOTAMs end with <b>EST</b> (the end time is an estimate) or <b>PERM</b> (permanent, until charts are updated). Common abbreviations in the condition: <b>CLSD</b> closed, <b>OTS</b> out of service, <b>UNAVBL</b> unavailable, <b>LGTD</b> lighted.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Part of the example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>!ABC</td><td>Issued by (accountability location) ABC</td></tr><tr><td>10/045</td><td>NOTAM number: October, notice 045</td></tr><tr><td>ABC</td><td>Affected location: ABC airport</td></tr><tr><td>RWY 18/36</td><td>Keyword RWY: runway 18/36</td></tr><tr><td>CLSD</td><td>Condition: closed</td></tr><tr><td>2610101300</td><td>Starts 2026 Oct 10 at 1300Z (8:00 a.m. CDT)</td></tr><tr><td>2610102100</td><td>Ends 2026 Oct 10 at 2100Z (4:00 p.m. CDT)</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">Example NOTAM, made up for teaching (ABC is a fictional airport):<br><span class=\"code\">!ABC 10/045 ABC RWY 18/36 CLSD 2610101300-2610102100</span></p>", "words": 223, "figs": []}, {"id": "d43c2", "day": 43, "dayTitle": "Reading NOTAMs", "title": "Keywords you will meet · NOTAM (D) versus FDC NOTAMs", "body": "<h3>Keywords you will meet</h3><p>The keyword is the quickest way to scan a long list. <b>RWY</b> is a runway (closures, shortened lengths, lighting). <b>TWY</b> is a taxiway. <b>AD</b> means aerodrome, a condition affecting the whole airport, such as an airport closure or an inoperative beacon. <b>OBST</b> is an obstacle, often a crane or a tower whose lights are out. <b>NAV</b> covers navigation aids, such as a VOR out of service. <b>SVC</b> covers services, such as fuel unavailable or a control tower operating on different hours. You will also see <b>AIRSPACE</b> for things like parachute jumping or aerobatic activity, and <b>COM</b> for communication outages.</p><h3>NOTAM (D) versus FDC NOTAMs</h3><p>A <b>NOTAM (D)</b>, for \"distant,\" is distributed nationwide and covers the everyday items above: runways, taxiways, airport lighting, navigation aids, obstacles, and services at public-use airports. An <b>FDC NOTAM</b> is issued by the FAA's National Flight Data Center for <b>regulatory</b> information: changes to instrument approach procedures and charts, airspace changes, and, most important for you, <b>temporary flight restrictions</b>. FDC NOTAMs begin with \"!FDC\" followed by a number made of the last digit of the year and a sequence (for example, FDC 6/1234 would be a 2026 notice).</p>", "words": 186, "figs": []}, {"id": "d43c3", "day": 43, "dayTitle": "Reading NOTAMs", "title": "TFRs: the ones you must not bust", "body": "<p>A <b>TFR</b> (temporary flight restriction) closes a piece of airspace for a time: wildfires and disasters, VIP and presidential movements, space launches, and major sporting events. Flying into an active TFR can bring an interception by military aircraft, enforcement action, and the loss of your certificate. Check the FAA's TFR list (tfr.faa.gov) or your EFB's graphical TFR layer before every flight, and again before the return leg, because TFRs can pop up with short notice.</p><p>One TFR you will meet often is the <b>stadium TFR</b>. For qualifying events, such as Major League Baseball, NFL, and major college football games, the airspace within <b>3 nautical miles</b> of the stadium, from the surface up to and including <b>3,000 feet AGL</b>, is restricted from <b>1 hour before the scheduled start until 1 hour after the event ends</b>. It is a standing restriction, so it may not appear as a separate NOTAM for each game. You must know the schedule of stadiums near your route.</p>", "words": 161, "figs": [{"day": 43, "sec": 4, "num": "43", "src": "img/fig43-tfr.svg", "caption": "Stadium TFR dimensions and an example timeline. The end time depends on when the event actually ends.", "w": 480.0, "h": 300.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d44c1", "day": 44, "dayTitle": "Plan One Cross-Country, Start to Finish", "title": "The airplane, the trip, and the chart · Weather: is it a go?", "body": "<h3>The airplane, the trip, and the chart</h3><p>Our airplane is a <b>hypothetical trainer similar to a Cessna 172</b>. Its numbers are made up for teaching: cruise true airspeed 110 knots, cruise fuel burn 8 gallons per hour, 40 gallons of usable fuel aboard, and the same weight and balance data as the Day 11 example. For a real flight, use your airplane's actual POH. The trip: from Alder Field (KAAA, fictional, a towered Class D airport, elevation 900 feet) to Birch Municipal (KBBB, fictional, nontowered), <b>90 nautical miles</b> away. Leaving at 1600Z (11:00 a.m. CDT), back home the same afternoon.</p><p>On the sectional you draw the line and measure a <b>true course of 060</b> degrees. Along it you choose checkpoints you can easily see: a lake at 30 nm and a town with a highway crossing at 60 nm. You note a MOA a few miles north of the line, a football stadium 2 nm south of the line near the town, and the Class D airspace around departure.</p><h3>Weather: is it a go?</h3><p>Departure METAR: <b>KAAA 101450Z 03010KT 10SM FEW050 18/06 A3010</b>. Wind 030 at 10, visibility 10 miles, a few clouds at 5,000, temperature 18 °C, dew point 6 °C, altimeter 30.10. No ceiling at all: solid VFR. Destination TAF: <b>KBBB 101120Z 1012/1112 03012KT P6SM SCT045 FM102000 20015G25KT 5SM -SHRA BKN030</b>. Good VFR until <b>2000Z</b>, then a front arrives with gusts to 25 knots, rain showers, and a 3,000-foot ceiling. The winds aloft forecast at 6,000 feet: <b>0312+08</b>, which is 030 degrees at 12 knots, plus 8 °C. No AIRMETs or SIGMETs along the route in the morning.</p><p>The outbound leg is clearly fine. The key fact is the deadline: the return should be finished well before 2000Z, when the gusts and showers are forecast to arrive.</p>", "words": 287, "figs": [{"day": 44, "sec": 0, "num": "44", "src": "img/fig44-route.svg", "caption": "Route sketch for today's fictional trip, with the checkpoints, the MOA to avoid, and the stadium TFR that matters on the return leg.", "w": 480.0, "h": 280.0, "pos": 0}]}, {"id": "d44c2", "day": 44, "dayTitle": "Plan One Cross-Country, Start to Finish", "title": "NOTAMs and airspace on the route", "body": "<h3>NOTAMs and airspace on the route</h3><p>The briefing shows <b>!BBB 10/012 BBB TWY A CLSD 2610101400-2610102200</b>: taxiway A at the destination is closed, so plan to back-taxi on the runway. Nothing affects the destination runway. The stadium near the town has a game scheduled to start at <b>1900Z</b>, so the stadium TFR is active from <b>1800Z</b> until 1 hour after the game ends. Outbound, you pass at about 1636Z: no problem. On the way home after 1800Z, you would have to route at least 3 nm from the stadium or stay above 3,000 feet AGL.</p><p>Airspace: you will talk to Alder Tower to leave Class D, then cruise in Class E. You check the MOA's hours in the chart margin and plan to stay south of it anyway. At Birch, you will self-announce on the CTAF.</p><h3>Performance and weight and balance at a glance</h3><p>Departure pressure altitude: 900 + (29.92 − 30.10) × 1,000 = 900 − 180 = <b>720 feet</b>. Standard temperature there is about 14 °C, so 18 °C is about 4 degrees warm, and density altitude is about 720 + 4 × 120 ≈ <b>1,200 feet</b>: no performance worries. An illustrative takeoff chart gives 1,000 feet of ground roll and 1,800 feet to clear a 50-foot obstacle. Runway 03 has a 10-knot headwind, so take off the 10 percent allowed for 9 knots: about 900 and 1,620 feet, against a 5,000-foot runway. Birch's 3,200-foot runway is ample for an illustrative 1,300-foot landing distance over a 50-foot obstacle, even with a 50 percent margin (1,950 feet).</p><p>Weight and balance, using the Day 11 hypothetical airplane: empty 1,650 lb at 39.0 in (64,350 lb-in); you and a friend, 350 lb at 37.0 (12,950); a 30-lb bag at 95.0 (2,850); 40 gallons of fuel, 240 lb at 48.0 (11,520). Total <b>2,270 lb</b> and <b>91,670 lb-in</b>, so CG = 91,670 ÷ 2,270 ≈ <b>40.4 in</b>. That is under the 2,550-lb maximum and inside the example's 35.0 to 47.3 inch range. After burning 10 gallons (60 lb at 48.0, 2,880 lb-in), you land at 2,210 lb with a CG of about 40.2 in: still fine.</p>", "words": 339, "figs": []}, {"id": "d44c3", "day": 44, "dayTitle": "Plan One Cross-Country, Start to Finish", "title": "Nav log math · Fuel and the reserve", "body": "<h3>Nav log math</h3><p>With the E6B, a true course of 060, true airspeed of 110, and wind from 030 at 12 give a wind correction of about <b>3 degrees left</b> and a groundspeed of about <b>100 knots</b> (a 12-knot wind from 30 degrees off the nose is mostly headwind). True heading = 060 − 3 = 057. The chart shows <b>2 degrees east</b> variation, and east is least, so subtract: magnetic heading = <b>055</b>. The compass card says steer 056 for 055, so compass heading is <b>056</b>.</p><p>The magnetic course is 058, which falls between 0 and 179, so above 3,000 feet AGL you fly odd thousands plus 500: you pick <b>5,500 feet</b>. At 100 knots, each 30-nm leg takes 30 ÷ 100 = 0.3 hour, or 18 minutes, and burns 0.3 × 8 = 2.4 gallons. The table below shows the whole trip: 90 nm, 54 minutes, 7.2 gallons in cruise.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Distance</th><th>Groundspeed</th><th>Time</th><th>Fuel</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>KAAA to the lake</td><td>30 nm</td><td>100 kt</td><td>18 min</td><td>2.4 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Lake to the town</td><td>30 nm</td><td>100 kt</td><td>18 min</td><td>2.4 gal</td></tr><tr><td>Town to KBBB</td><td>30 nm</td><td>100 kt</td><td>18 min</td><td>2.4 gal</td></tr><tr><td><b>Cruise total</b></td><td><b>90 nm</b></td><td></td><td><b>54 min</b></td><td><b>7.2 gal</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">Illustrative nav log for the hypothetical trainer. True course 060, wind correction 3° left, variation 2° E, compass heading 056, 5,500 ft. Climb and taxi fuel are added separately.</p><h3>Fuel and the reserve</h3><p>Add illustrative allowances of 1.5 gallons for start, taxi, and takeoff and 1.3 gallons extra for the climb: 7.2 + 1.5 + 1.3 = <b>10 gallons</b> for the trip. The legal VFR day reserve under <b>91.151</b> is enough to fly <b>30 minutes</b> beyond the first point of intended landing at normal cruise (it would be 45 minutes at night). At 8 gallons per hour, 30 minutes is 4 gallons, so the legal minimum at takeoff is 14 gallons. A personal one-hour reserve makes it 18. You have 40 gallons, leaving about 30 gallons on landing: enough for the return trip with a comfortable reserve, or you can top off at Birch.</p>", "words": 260, "figs": []}], "quiz": [{"id": "d1c1q1", "card": "d1c1", "q": "In general, a private pilot may NOT", "opts": ["act as pilot in command for compensation or hire", "carry passengers", "fly at night"], "ans": 0, "why": "Private privileges do not include flying for pay, with a few narrow exceptions."}, {"id": "d1c1q2", "card": "d1c1", "q": "The expenses a private pilot may share pro rata with passengers are limited to", "opts": ["fuel, oil, airport expenditures, and rental fees", "the pilot's salary", "the cost of the pilot's training"], "ans": 0, "why": "61.113(c) lists the shareable expenses."}, {"id": "d1c2q1", "card": "d1c2", "q": "Flying in clouds, by reference to instruments alone, requires", "opts": ["an instrument rating", "a high-performance endorsement", "a commercial certificate"], "ans": 0, "why": "The private certificate is VFR; flying in clouds takes an instrument rating."}, {"id": "d1c2q2", "card": "d1c2", "q": "The checkride (practical test) is graded against", "opts": ["the Airman Certification Standards (ACS)", "the airplane's POH", "the knowledge test report"], "ans": 0, "why": "Both the oral and the flight portion are graded to the ACS."}, {"id": "d2c1q1", "card": "d2c1", "q": "Private pilot night training must include", "opts": ["3 hours, one cross-country of over 100 nm, and 10 takeoffs and landings to a full stop", "1 hour and 3 landings", "5 hours and 5 landings"], "ans": 0, "why": "61.109(a)(2)."}, {"id": "d2c1q2", "card": "d2c1", "q": "Under Part 61, a private pilot applicant (airplane) needs at least", "opts": ["40 hours of flight time, including 20 hours of instruction and 10 hours of solo", "35 hours, including 15 dual and 5 solo", "60 hours, including 30 dual and 20 solo"], "ans": 0, "why": "61.109(a)."}, {"id": "d2c2q1", "card": "d2c2", "q": "The required long solo cross-country is at least", "opts": ["150 nm total, with full-stop landings at three points and one leg of at least 50 nm straight-line distance", "100 nm total with two landings", "50 nm total"], "ans": 0, "why": "61.109(a)(5)(ii)."}, {"id": "d3c1q1", "card": "d3c1", "q": "The upward force on a wing as it deflects air downward is an example of which of Newton's laws?", "opts": ["The third law: every action has an equal and opposite reaction", "The first law: inertia", "The law of gravitation"], "ans": 0, "why": "Turning air downward produces an equal and opposite upward reaction, which is lift."}, {"id": "d3c1q2", "card": "d3c1", "q": "Angle of attack is the angle between the wing's chord line and the", "opts": ["relative wind", "horizon", "airplane's longitudinal axis"], "ans": 0, "why": "Angle of attack is measured between the chord line and the relative wind."}, {"id": "d3c2q1", "card": "d3c2", "q": "What causes a wing to stall?", "opts": ["Exceeding its critical angle of attack", "Slowing below a fixed airspeed that never changes", "Raising the nose above a specific pitch attitude"], "ans": 0, "why": "A stall happens whenever the critical angle of attack is exceeded, at any airspeed or attitude."}, {"id": "d4c1q1", "card": "d4c1", "q": "In steady, straight-and-level, unaccelerated flight,", "opts": ["lift equals weight and thrust equals drag", "lift is greater than weight", "thrust is greater than drag"], "ans": 0, "why": "Unaccelerated flight means the opposing forces are in balance."}, {"id": "d4c1q2", "card": "d4c1", "q": "Which type of drag increases as airspeed increases?", "opts": ["Parasite drag", "Induced drag", "Neither; all drag decreases with speed"], "ans": 0, "why": "Parasite drag (form, friction, interference) rises roughly with the square of airspeed."}, {"id": "d5c1q1", "card": "d5c1", "q": "What actually turns an airplane in a coordinated turn?", "opts": ["The horizontal component of lift", "The rudder", "Centrifugal force"], "ans": 0, "why": "Banking tilts lift; its horizontal part pulls the airplane around the turn."}, {"id": "d5c1q2", "card": "d5c1", "q": "The positive limit load factor for a normal category airplane is", "opts": ["3.8 G", "4.4 G", "6.0 G"], "ans": 0, "why": "Normal category airplanes are designed for +3.8 G (utility +4.4, acrobatic +6.0)."}, {"id": "d5c2q1", "card": "d5c2", "q": "In a level 60-degree bank (2 G), stall speed rises by about", "opts": ["41 percent", "100 percent", "10 percent"], "ans": 0, "why": "Stall speed rises with the square root of the load factor, and the square root of 2 is about 1.41."}, {"id": "d6c1q1", "card": "d6c1", "q": "The first and most important action to recover from a stall is to", "opts": ["reduce the angle of attack", "add right rudder", "retract the flaps"], "ans": 0, "why": "Lowering the angle of attack below critical restores smooth airflow and lift."}, {"id": "d6c2q1", "card": "d6c2", "q": "During spin recovery in most light airplanes, rudder is applied", "opts": ["fully opposite the direction of rotation", "in the same direction as the rotation", "to neutral and held there"], "ans": 0, "why": "Full opposite rudder stops the rotation; neutralize it once rotation stops."}, {"id": "d6c2q2", "card": "d6c2", "q": "In the common PARE spin recovery, the throttle is moved to", "opts": ["idle", "full power", "cruise power"], "ans": 0, "why": "Power to idle first; power tends to flatten the spin and worsen it."}, {"id": "d7c1q1", "card": "d7c1", "q": "Adverse yaw during a roll into a left turn tends to yaw the nose", "opts": ["to the right, away from the turn", "to the left, into the turn", "up, raising the pitch"], "ans": 0, "why": "The rising (right) wing's down aileron makes more drag, pulling the nose away from the turn."}, {"id": "d7c1q2", "card": "d7c1", "q": "An airplane whose initial tendency after a disturbance is to return to its original attitude has", "opts": ["positive static stability", "neutral static stability", "negative static stability"], "ans": 0, "why": "Positive static stability means the airplane initially tends back toward where it was."}, {"id": "d7c2q1", "card": "d7c2", "q": "The left-turning tendency caused by the descending propeller blade taking a bigger bite of air at high angle of attack is", "opts": ["P-factor", "torque", "gyroscopic precession"], "ans": 0, "why": "Asymmetric blade loading (P-factor) yaws a clockwise-turning propeller airplane to the left."}, {"id": "d7c2q2", "card": "d7c2", "q": "In a full-power climb, a typical single-engine trainer with a clockwise-turning propeller (seen from the cockpit) usually needs", "opts": ["right rudder", "left rudder", "no rudder at all"], "ans": 0, "why": "Left-turning tendencies are strongest at high power and low airspeed, so add right rudder."}, {"id": "d8c1q1", "card": "d8c1", "q": "Design maneuvering speed (VA) is best described as", "opts": ["the maximum speed at which full, abrupt control movement should not overstress the airplane", "the maximum speed with flaps extended", "the speed that must never be exceeded"], "ans": 0, "why": "Below VA the wing stalls before a full control input can exceed the load limit."}, {"id": "d8c1q2", "card": "d8c1", "q": "Which speed gives the greatest gain in altitude in the shortest time?", "opts": ["VY, best rate of climb", "VX, best angle of climb", "VNO"], "ans": 0, "why": "VY is best rate: most feet per minute."}, {"id": "d8c2q1", "card": "d8c2", "q": "On the airspeed indicator, the white arc marks the", "opts": ["flap operating range", "normal operating range", "caution range, smooth air only"], "ans": 0, "why": "White is the flap operating range, from VSO to VFE."}, {"id": "d8c2q2", "card": "d8c2", "q": "The yellow arc on the airspeed indicator is", "opts": ["the caution range, to be flown only in smooth air", "the normal operating range", "the flap operating range"], "ans": 0, "why": "Yellow runs from VNO to VNE: smooth air only, with caution."}, {"id": "d9c1q1", "card": "d9c1", "q": "Density altitude is", "opts": ["pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature", "height above the ground", "indicated altitude corrected for instrument error"], "ans": 0, "why": "Density altitude is the altitude in the standard atmosphere that matches the air's actual density."}, {"id": "d9c1q2", "card": "d9c1", "q": "On a high-density-altitude day, the indicated airspeed for liftoff is the same as usual, but the takeoff run is longer because", "opts": ["true airspeed and groundspeed at liftoff are higher", "the indicated liftoff speed is higher", "the wing produces more lift"], "ans": 0, "why": "Thin air means a higher true airspeed (and groundspeed) for the same indicated speed."}, {"id": "d10c1q1", "card": "d10c1", "q": "Compared with a dry paved runway, a takeoff from tall, wet grass will usually need", "opts": ["a longer ground roll", "a shorter ground roll", "the same ground roll"], "ans": 0, "why": "Soft or grass surfaces add rolling resistance; POH charts give a correction."}, {"id": "d10c1q2", "card": "d10c1", "q": "Compared with no wind, a headwind on takeoff", "opts": ["shortens the ground roll", "lengthens the ground roll", "does not change the ground roll"], "ans": 0, "why": "Less groundspeed is needed to reach liftoff airspeed."}, {"id": "d10c2q1", "card": "d10c2", "q": "When landing, ground effect tends to make the airplane", "opts": ["float if it is too fast in the flare", "drop suddenly onto the runway", "stall at a higher airspeed"], "ans": 0, "why": "Lower induced drag near the surface lets excess speed carry the airplane down the runway."}, {"id": "d10c2q2", "card": "d10c2", "q": "Ground effect is caused mainly by", "opts": ["the ground interfering with wingtip vortices and downwash, which reduces induced drag", "an increase in parasite drag near the runway", "warm air rising from the pavement"], "ans": 0, "why": "Within about one wingspan of the surface, reduced downwash cuts induced drag."}, {"id": "d11c1q1", "card": "d11c1", "q": "The center of gravity is found by", "opts": ["dividing the total moment by the total weight", "dividing the total weight by the total moment", "adding all the arms together"], "ans": 0, "why": "CG = total moment / total weight."}, {"id": "d11c1q2", "card": "d11c1", "q": "In weight and balance, moment equals", "opts": ["weight times arm", "arm divided by weight", "weight divided by arm"], "ans": 0, "why": "Moment is weight multiplied by its distance from the datum."}, {"id": "d12c1q1", "card": "d12c1", "q": "Aircraft magnetos", "opts": ["make their own electricity for ignition, independent of the battery and alternator", "run off the airplane's battery", "are used only for starting the engine"], "ans": 0, "why": "Once the engine turns, magnetos keep firing the plugs even with the master switch off."}, {"id": "d12c1q2", "card": "d12c1", "q": "A main benefit of a dual ignition system is", "opts": ["redundancy and more complete combustion", "lower fuel burn at idle only", "the ability to start without a battery"], "ans": 0, "why": "Two magnetos and two plugs per cylinder give a backup and a better burn."}, {"id": "d12c2q1", "card": "d12c2", "q": "Applying carburetor heat makes the mixture", "opts": ["richer", "leaner", "unchanged"], "ans": 0, "why": "Warm air is less dense, so the same fuel meets less air."}, {"id": "d12c2q2", "card": "d12c2", "q": "Carburetor ice is possible", "opts": ["at outside temperatures as warm as about 70 °F in humid air", "only below freezing", "only in visible moisture below 32 °F"], "ans": 0, "why": "Fuel vaporization and the venturi's pressure drop can cool the air 60 °F or more."}, {"id": "d12c3q1", "card": "d12c3", "q": "In an airplane with a fixed-pitch propeller, engine power is shown by the", "opts": ["tachometer (RPM)", "manifold pressure gauge", "oil pressure gauge"], "ans": 0, "why": "Fixed-pitch: RPM indicates power."}, {"id": "d13c1q1", "card": "d13c1", "q": "Low-wing airplanes generally need fuel pumps because", "opts": ["the tanks are below the engine, so gravity cannot feed it", "the fuel is heavier in low-wing airplanes", "the engine is mounted higher"], "ans": 0, "why": "High-wing trainers can be gravity fed; low-wing airplanes must pump fuel up."}, {"id": "d13c1q2", "card": "d13c1", "q": "Jet A fuel normally looks", "opts": ["clear or straw colored", "blue", "green"], "ans": 0, "why": "Jet fuel is undyed; never put it in a piston engine."}, {"id": "d13c2q1", "card": "d13c2", "q": "In many trainers, the engine-driven vacuum pump powers the", "opts": ["attitude indicator and heading indicator", "airspeed indicator and altimeter", "turn coordinator"], "ans": 0, "why": "The turn coordinator is usually electric, as a backup."}, {"id": "d13c2q2", "card": "d13c2", "q": "While the engine runs, the alternator", "opts": ["supplies electrical power and charges the battery", "only charges the battery on the ground", "only runs the magnetos"], "ans": 0, "why": "The alternator powers the system and recharges the battery."}, {"id": "d14c1q1", "card": "d14c1", "q": "At and above 18,000 feet MSL, the altimeter is set to", "opts": ["29.92", "the nearest station setting", "field elevation"], "ans": 0, "why": "Above the transition altitude everyone uses standard pressure."}, {"id": "d14c1q2", "card": "d14c1", "q": "The static port supplies pressure to the", "opts": ["airspeed indicator, altimeter, and VSI", "altimeter only", "attitude and heading indicators"], "ans": 0, "why": "All three pitot-static instruments use static pressure."}, {"id": "d14c2q1", "card": "d14c2", "q": "If both the pitot tube's ram opening and its drain hole become blocked, the airspeed indicator will", "opts": ["act like an altimeter, reading higher as you climb", "read zero", "read correctly"], "ans": 0, "why": "Trapped pitot pressure stays constant while static pressure drops in a climb."}, {"id": "d14c2q2", "card": "d14c2", "q": "In an unpressurized airplane, using the alternate static source inside the cabin usually makes the altimeter and airspeed read", "opts": ["slightly higher than normal", "slightly lower than normal", "exactly the same"], "ans": 0, "why": "Cabin pressure is a little lower than outside pressure in flight."}, {"id": "d15c1q1", "card": "d15c1", "q": "Because of precession, the heading indicator should be checked against the magnetic compass about every", "opts": ["15 minutes in straight, level, unaccelerated flight", "2 hours", "flight only, before takeoff"], "ans": 0, "why": "Friction makes the gyro drift; reset it when the compass is steady."}, {"id": "d15c1q2", "card": "d15c1", "q": "The turn coordinator shows", "opts": ["rate of roll and rate of turn", "bank angle directly", "pitch attitude"], "ans": 0, "why": "The miniature airplane responds to roll rate and turn rate; the ball shows coordination."}, {"id": "d15c2q1", "card": "d15c2", "q": "Turning to a heading of north by the magnetic compass alone in the northern hemisphere, roll out", "opts": ["before the compass reaches north", "after the compass passes north", "exactly as the compass reads north"], "ans": 0, "why": "Undershoot North, Overshoot South: the compass lags near north."}, {"id": "d15c2q2", "card": "d15c2", "q": "Flying east in the northern hemisphere, you accelerate. The magnetic compass will briefly indicate", "opts": ["a turn toward north", "a turn toward south", "no change"], "ans": 0, "why": "Accelerate North, Decelerate South (ANDS)."}, {"id": "d16c1q1", "card": "d16c1", "q": "On most round gauges, a red radial line marks", "opts": ["a limit you must not exceed", "the normal operating range", "the flap operating range"], "ans": 0, "why": "Green is normal, yellow is caution, and red lines are limits."}, {"id": "d16c2q1", "card": "d16c2", "q": "The bottom of the white arc on the airspeed indicator is", "opts": ["VSO, the power-off stall speed in the landing configuration", "VFE, the maximum flap extended speed", "VS1, the stall speed with flaps up"], "ans": 0, "why": "The white arc runs from VSO at the bottom to VFE at the top."}, {"id": "d16c2q2", "card": "d16c2", "q": "Which speed is NOT marked on the airspeed indicator?", "opts": ["VY, best rate of climb", "VNE, never exceed", "VNO, maximum structural cruising speed"], "ans": 0, "why": "VA, VX, VY, and best glide come from the POH, not from the gauge markings."}, {"id": "d16c3q1", "card": "d16c3", "q": "On a three-hand altimeter, which hand should you read first?", "opts": ["The short, wide hand (thousands)", "The long hand (hundreds)", "The thin hand with the triangle (tens of thousands)"], "ans": 0, "why": "Reading the short hand first avoids the classic error of taking the long hand for thousands."}, {"id": "d16c3q2", "card": "d16c3", "q": "A 1-inch error in the altimeter setting causes about how much altitude error?", "opts": ["1,000 feet", "100 feet", "10 feet"], "ans": 0, "why": "Each inch of setting error is about 1,000 feet of altitude error."}, {"id": "d16c4q1", "card": "d16c4", "q": "With the static port blocked, the vertical speed indicator reads", "opts": ["zero, no matter what the airplane is doing", "a constant climb", "double the actual rate"], "ans": 0, "why": "The VSI works from changing static pressure, so a blocked port freezes it at zero."}, {"id": "d16c4q2", "card": "d16c4", "q": "The VSI shows the trend at once, but the exact rate lags by about", "opts": ["6 to 9 seconds", "1 second", "30 seconds"], "ans": 0, "why": "Set a pitch attitude and let the needle settle rather than chasing it."}, {"id": "d17c1q1", "card": "d17c1", "q": "The attitude indicator shows a turn, but the turn coordinator and heading indicator do not. Suspect", "opts": ["the attitude indicator", "the turn coordinator", "the magnetic compass"], "ans": 0, "why": "Cross-check: when one instrument disagrees with the others, suspect that one."}, {"id": "d17c1q2", "card": "d17c1", "q": "When a vacuum-driven attitude indicator fails, the display usually", "opts": ["tilts or drifts slowly, often enough to fool you", "snaps instantly to a red OFF flag", "freezes perfectly level"], "ans": 0, "why": "The gyro winds down gradually, which is why cross-checking matters."}, {"id": "d17c2q1", "card": "d17c2", "q": "With its wingtip on the lower L or R mark, the turn coordinator shows", "opts": ["a standard-rate turn of 3 degrees per second", "a 30-degree bank", "a 45-degree bank"], "ans": 0, "why": "Standard rate is 3 degrees per second, a full circle in 2 minutes."}, {"id": "d17c2q2", "card": "d17c2", "q": "The inclinometer ball is off to the right in a turn. To coordinate, press", "opts": ["the right rudder", "the left rudder", "forward on the yoke"], "ans": 0, "why": "Step on the ball: press the rudder on the side the ball has moved to."}, {"id": "d17c3q1", "card": "d17c3", "q": "The heading indicator should be rechecked against the compass about every", "opts": ["15 minutes", "2 minutes", "hour"], "ans": 0, "why": "Precession makes it drift slowly, so reset it to the compass about every 15 minutes."}, {"id": "d17c4q1", "card": "d17c4", "q": "The magnetic compass is accurate only", "opts": ["in straight, level, unaccelerated flight", "in turns", "during climbs"], "ans": 0, "why": "Acceleration and turning errors make it swing at other times."}, {"id": "d17c4q2", "card": "d17c4", "q": "A compass correction card lists", "opts": ["the deviation for that airplane's compass", "the magnetic variation for the area", "the airplane's V-speeds"], "ans": 0, "why": "For example, \"For 30, steer 029.\""}, {"id": "d18c1q1", "card": "d18c1", "q": "In an airplane with a fixed-pitch propeller, your power indication is", "opts": ["the tachometer (RPM)", "the manifold pressure gauge", "the oil pressure gauge"], "ans": 0, "why": "With a fixed-pitch prop you set power by RPM."}, {"id": "d18c1q2", "card": "d18c1", "q": "An unexplained, gradual loss of RPM in a carbureted engine suggests", "opts": ["carburetor ice", "a failed tachometer", "a vacuum failure"], "ans": 0, "why": "A gradual RPM loss is the classic first sign of carburetor ice."}, {"id": "d18c2q1", "card": "d18c2", "q": "With a constant-speed propeller, the throttle sets", "opts": ["manifold pressure", "RPM", "fuel pressure"], "ans": 0, "why": "The throttle sets manifold pressure; the propeller control sets RPM."}, {"id": "d18c2q2", "card": "d18c2", "q": "When adding power with a constant-speed propeller, the common habit is to", "opts": ["increase RPM first, then manifold pressure", "increase manifold pressure first, then RPM", "move both at the same time"], "ans": 0, "why": "This avoids high manifold pressure at low RPM; reduce in the reverse order."}, {"id": "d18c3q1", "card": "d18c3", "q": "After start, oil pressure does not rise into the normal range within the POH time (about 30 seconds). You should", "opts": ["shut the engine down", "take off and watch it", "add power to raise it"], "ans": 0, "why": "Oil pressure is the first gauge to check after start; no pressure means shut down."}, {"id": "d18c3q2", "card": "d18c3", "q": "Low oil pressure together with rising oil temperature is a classic sign of", "opts": ["oil loss or an impending engine failure", "a normal cold start", "carburetor ice"], "ans": 0, "why": "Reduce power, pick a landing spot, and land as soon as possible."}, {"id": "d18c4q1", "card": "d18c4", "q": "With a simple EGT gauge, what matters most is", "opts": ["the change in EGT as you lean", "the exact number of degrees", "whether the needle is in the green arc"], "ans": 0, "why": "You lean to peak, set the reference pointer, then enrich as the POH says."}, {"id": "d18c4q2", "card": "d18c4", "q": "High CHT in a long climb is a sign the engine needs more cooling. One fix is to", "opts": ["lower the nose for more airspeed", "lean the mixture further", "pull the power to idle abruptly"], "ans": 0, "why": "Enrich, lower the nose, or reduce power gradually to avoid shock cooling."}, {"id": "d19c1q1", "card": "d19c1", "q": "Fuel quantity gauges are certificated only to read accurately", "opts": ["at empty, when just unusable fuel remains", "at full", "at half tanks"], "ans": 0, "why": "That is why you check the tanks visually and plan fuel by time and burn rate."}, {"id": "d19c2q1", "card": "d19c2", "q": "A steady discharge on a center-zero ammeter in flight means", "opts": ["the alternator is not carrying the load and the battery is draining", "the battery is being charged normally", "the vacuum pump has failed"], "ans": 0, "why": "Turn off nonessential equipment and land as soon as practical."}, {"id": "d19c2q2", "card": "d19c2", "q": "With the alternator working, a 14-volt system's voltmeter reads about", "opts": ["14 volts", "12 volts", "28 volts"], "ans": 0, "why": "A reading at battery voltage or falling means the alternator is off line."}, {"id": "d19c3q1", "card": "d19c3", "q": "A low suction gauge reading means", "opts": ["the attitude and heading indicator gyros may spin too slowly and drift", "the turn coordinator will fail", "the airspeed indicator will read high"], "ans": 0, "why": "The vacuum pump spins those gyros; cross-check with the electric turn coordinator."}, {"id": "d19c3q2", "card": "d19c3", "q": "The flaps should never be extended above", "opts": ["VFE", "VNO", "VS1"], "ans": 0, "why": "VFE is the maximum flap extended speed, the top of the white arc."}, {"id": "d20c1q1", "card": "d20c1", "q": "A sign with a red background and white letters, such as '9-27', is a", "opts": ["runway holding position sign", "taxiway location sign", "direction sign"], "ans": 0, "why": "Red and white signs are mandatory instruction signs."}, {"id": "d20c1q2", "card": "d20c1", "q": "A sign with a black background and yellow letters tells you", "opts": ["which taxiway or runway you are on", "which way to turn to reach a taxiway", "that you must hold short"], "ans": 0, "why": "Black with yellow is a location sign."}, {"id": "d20c2q1", "card": "d20c2", "q": "A four-light PAPI shows two white and two red lights. You are", "opts": ["on the glide path", "slightly high", "well below the glide path"], "ans": 0, "why": "Two and two is on path; more red is low, more white is high."}, {"id": "d20c2q2", "card": "d20c2", "q": "The FAA's recommended traffic pattern altitude for most piston airplanes is", "opts": ["1,000 feet AGL", "500 feet AGL", "2,000 feet AGL"], "ans": 0, "why": "Unless the airport publishes otherwise, 1,000 feet AGL is recommended."}, {"id": "d21c1q1", "card": "d21c1", "q": "A runway pointing 087 degrees magnetic is numbered", "opts": ["9", "8", "87"], "ans": 0, "why": "Divide the magnetic direction by 10 and round, with no leading zero."}, {"id": "d21c1q2", "card": "d21c1", "q": "The two ends of a single runway always have numbers that differ by", "opts": ["18", "9", "36"], "ans": 0, "why": "They point in opposite directions, 180 degrees apart."}, {"id": "d21c2q1", "card": "d21c2", "q": "A runway with 12 threshold stripes (in the width-coded layout) is", "opts": ["150 feet wide", "100 feet wide", "75 feet wide"], "ans": 0, "why": "4 stripes = 60 ft, 6 = 75, 8 = 100, 12 = 150, 16 = 200."}, {"id": "d21c3q1", "card": "d21c3", "q": "The aiming point marking is about how far past the threshold?", "opts": ["1,000 feet", "500 feet", "3,000 feet"], "ans": 0, "why": "Two broad white rectangles, about 1,000 feet in."}, {"id": "d21c3q2", "card": "d21c3", "q": "On the rollout, touchdown zone bars with three stripes per side mark", "opts": ["500 feet from the threshold", "2,500 feet from the threshold", "the runway midpoint"], "ans": 0, "why": "Three bars at 500, two at 1,500 and 2,000, one at 2,500 and 3,000."}, {"id": "d21c4q1", "card": "d21c4", "q": "The area before a displaced threshold (marked with white arrows) may be used for", "opts": ["taxiing and the takeoff roll, but not touchdown toward the arrows", "nothing at all", "landing in either direction"], "ans": 0, "why": "Arrows mean the pavement can be used for some things, just not touching down."}, {"id": "d21c4q2", "card": "d21c4", "q": "Yellow chevrons on pavement lined up with a runway mark", "opts": ["a blast pad or stopway, unusable for landing, takeoff, and taxiing", "a displaced threshold", "a closed taxiway"], "ans": 0, "why": "Yellow means it is not runway."}, {"id": "d21c5q1", "card": "d21c5", "q": "Approaching a runway on a taxiway, where do you stop at the hold line?", "opts": ["Behind the solid lines on your side", "Past the dashed lines", "Between the solid and dashed lines"], "ans": 0, "why": "The solid lines face you; no part of the airplane may cross without clearance."}, {"id": "d21c5q2", "card": "d21c5", "q": "Yellow dashes on both sides of a taxiway centerline (the enhanced centerline) warn you that", "opts": ["a runway hold line is coming within about 150 feet", "the taxiway is closed", "the pavement beyond is not for aircraft"], "ans": 0, "why": "Slow down and confirm your clearance."}, {"id": "d22c1q1", "card": "d22c1", "q": "A continuous double yellow taxiway edge line means the pavement beyond it is", "opts": ["not for aircraft", "a ramp you may cross", "a runway"], "ans": 0, "why": "Continuous means not for aircraft; dashed double means aircraft may cross."}, {"id": "d22c2q1", "card": "d22c2", "q": "A ladder-like marking across a taxiway with a red sign reading ILS is", "opts": ["the ILS critical area holding position", "the runway holding position", "a taxiway/taxiway hold line"], "ans": 0, "why": "You hold there only when ATC tells you to hold short of the ILS critical area."}, {"id": "d22c2q2", "card": "d22c2", "q": "At a towered airport, \"taxi to runway 9\" clears you across other runways on the way", "opts": ["No, each runway crossing needs its own explicit clearance", "Yes, always", "Only if they are inactive"], "ans": 0, "why": "Every runway crossing needs its own clearance; read back hold-short instructions."}, {"id": "d22c3q1", "card": "d22c3", "q": "After landing, you are clear of the runway when", "opts": ["every part of the airplane, tail included, is past the solid lines", "the nosewheel crosses the dashed lines", "you turn onto the taxiway"], "ans": 0, "why": "Taxi completely past the marking, then stop."}, {"id": "d22c4q1", "card": "d22c4", "q": "A sign with black background and yellow letters and border is a", "opts": ["location sign: where you are", "mandatory sign: stop", "direction sign: where to go"], "ans": 0, "why": "Black with yellow = location; yellow with black and an arrow = direction; red with white = mandatory."}, {"id": "d22c4q2", "card": "d22c4", "q": "A red sign reading \"9-27\" at a hold line means", "opts": ["the runway 9 threshold is to your left and the runway 27 threshold to your right", "runway 9 is closed", "you are on taxiway 9"], "ans": 0, "why": "Hold-sign numbers are arranged like the runway in front of you."}, {"id": "d22c5q1", "card": "d22c5", "q": "At a nonmovement area boundary, the dashed yellow line faces", "opts": ["the movement area", "the ramp", "the runway hold line"], "ans": 0, "why": "At a towered field, call ground before crossing from solid to dashed."}, {"id": "d23c1q1", "card": "d23c1", "q": "To operate in Class B airspace, an airplane needs", "opts": ["a two-way radio, a Mode C transponder, and ADS-B Out", "only a two-way radio", "only a VOR receiver"], "ans": 0, "why": "Class B requires radio, altitude-reporting transponder, and ADS-B Out."}, {"id": "d23c1q2", "card": "d23c1", "q": "Class G airspace is", "opts": ["uncontrolled airspace, where ATC does not control traffic", "airspace reserved for military use", "controlled airspace above 18,000 feet"], "ans": 0, "why": "G is uncontrolled."}, {"id": "d23c2q1", "card": "d23c2", "q": "To enter Class B airspace, a VFR pilot must", "opts": ["receive an explicit clearance, such as 'cleared into the Class Bravo'", "only establish two-way radio contact", "only have an operating transponder"], "ans": 0, "why": "Radar contact or a frequency change is not a clearance."}, {"id": "d23c2q2", "card": "d23c2", "q": "The maximum indicated airspeed when flying beneath the lateral limits of Class B airspace, or in a VFR corridor through it, is", "opts": ["200 knots", "250 knots", "156 knots"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.117(c)."}, {"id": "d23c3q1", "card": "d23c3", "q": "Before entering Class C airspace, a VFR pilot must", "opts": ["establish two-way radio communication with the ATC facility", "receive an explicit clearance", "file a flight plan"], "ans": 0, "why": "Hearing your call sign in the controller's reply establishes communication."}, {"id": "d23c3q2", "card": "d23c3", "q": "Entering Class D airspace requires", "opts": ["two-way radio communication with the tower", "an explicit clearance into Class D", "a Mode C transponder"], "ans": 0, "why": "Class D needs two-way communication; no transponder is required by the airspace itself."}, {"id": "d23c4q1", "card": "d23c4", "q": "Victor airways are Class E airspace extending from 1,200 feet AGL up to but not including", "opts": ["18,000 feet MSL", "14,500 feet MSL", "10,000 feet MSL"], "ans": 0, "why": "Federal airways are Class E from 1,200 AGL to below 18,000 MSL."}, {"id": "d23c4q2", "card": "d23c4", "q": "Near many airports with instrument approaches, Class E airspace begins at", "opts": ["700 feet AGL", "1,200 feet AGL", "the surface, always"], "ans": 0, "why": "A magenta vignette on the sectional shows the 700 AGL floor."}, {"id": "d24c1q1", "card": "d24c1", "q": "Basic VFR minimums in Class C, Class D, and Class E below 10,000 feet MSL are", "opts": ["3 SM; 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal", "1 SM and clear of clouds", "3 SM and clear of clouds"], "ans": 0, "why": "Pilots remember it as \"3-152.\""}, {"id": "d24c1q2", "card": "d24c1", "q": "VFR minimums in Class B airspace are", "opts": ["3 SM and clear of clouds", "3 SM; 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal", "1 SM and clear of clouds"], "ans": 0, "why": "ATC is already separating everyone in Class B."}, {"id": "d24c2q1", "card": "d24c2", "q": "At 10,500 feet MSL and 3,000 feet AGL in Class G airspace, the VFR minimums are", "opts": ["5 SM; 1,000 feet below, 1,000 above, 1 SM horizontal", "1 SM and clear of clouds", "3 SM; 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal"], "ans": 0, "why": "Above 1,200 AGL and at or above 10,000 MSL, Class G uses the same 5-111 rule as Class E."}, {"id": "d24c2q2", "card": "d24c2", "q": "Special VFR (91.157) allows operation in a surface area with at least", "opts": ["1 SM visibility and clear of clouds, with an ATC clearance", "3 SM visibility and clear of clouds, with no clearance", "1/2 SM visibility and 500 feet below clouds"], "ans": 0, "why": "Special VFR: 1 mile, clear of clouds, ATC clearance."}, {"id": "d25c1q1", "card": "d25c1", "q": "Flying through an active restricted area requires", "opts": ["permission from the controlling or using agency", "only a transponder", "only two-way radio with any facility"], "ans": 0, "why": "Restricted areas contain hazards like artillery firing."}, {"id": "d25c1q2", "card": "d25c1", "q": "A VFR pilot flying through an active Military Operations Area (MOA)", "opts": ["may do so without a clearance but should use extreme caution and check its status", "must get a clearance", "must stay at least 3,000 feet above it"], "ans": 0, "why": "MOAs separate military training from IFR traffic; VFR flight is allowed."}, {"id": "d25c2q1", "card": "d25c2", "q": "The Mode C veil is the airspace within 30 nm of a Class B primary airport from the surface up to", "opts": ["10,000 feet MSL", "the top of the Class B only", "18,000 feet MSL"], "ans": 0, "why": "In the veil, a Mode C transponder (and ADS-B Out) is required."}, {"id": "d25c2q2", "card": "d25c2", "q": "A transponder with altitude reporting is required in and above Class C airspace up to", "opts": ["10,000 feet MSL", "the top of the Class C only", "18,000 feet MSL"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.215 covers the Class C lateral limits up to 10,000 MSL."}, {"id": "d26c1q1", "card": "d26c1", "q": "The scale of a VFR terminal area chart (TAC) is", "opts": ["1:250,000", "1:500,000", "1:100,000"], "ans": 0, "why": "TACs show more detail around Class B airspace."}, {"id": "d26c1q2", "card": "d26c1", "q": "Isogonic lines on a sectional connect points of", "opts": ["equal magnetic variation", "equal elevation", "equal air pressure"], "ans": 0, "why": "They are dashed magenta lines labeled like 3°E."}, {"id": "d26c2q1", "card": "d26c2", "q": "An obstacle labeled 1249 in bold with (350) below it is", "opts": ["1,249 feet MSL at its top and 350 feet above the ground", "350 feet MSL and 1,249 feet AGL", "1,249 feet AGL"], "ans": 0, "why": "The bold number is MSL; the number in parentheses is AGL."}, {"id": "d26c2q2", "card": "d26c2", "q": "A larger obstruction symbol on a sectional is used for obstacles that are", "opts": ["1,000 feet or more above the ground", "under 200 feet tall", "lighted only"], "ans": 0, "why": "Obstacles 1,000 feet AGL and higher get a bigger symbol."}, {"id": "d26c3q1", "card": "d26c3", "q": "On a sectional, a filled-in airport circle with no runway shown inside means the airport", "opts": ["has no hard-surfaced runways", "has runways over 8,069 feet", "is closed"], "ans": 0, "why": "Hard-surfaced runways 1,500 to 8,069 feet are drawn inside an open circle."}, {"id": "d26c3q2", "card": "d26c3", "q": "An airport data block reads '900 L 50 122.95.' The 50 means", "opts": ["the longest runway is about 5,000 feet", "the airport elevation is 50 feet", "the UNICOM frequency is 50"], "ans": 0, "why": "Runway length is given in hundreds of feet."}, {"id": "d26c4q1", "card": "d26c4", "q": "A fuzzy blue band on a sectional marks where Class E airspace with a floor of", "opts": ["1,200 feet AGL or higher abuts Class G airspace", "700 feet AGL begins", "the surface begins"], "ans": 0, "why": "Blue vignettes mark the edge of Class E with a 1,200-foot or higher floor."}, {"id": "d26c4q2", "card": "d26c4", "q": "A fuzzy magenta band (vignette) on a sectional shows where", "opts": ["Class E airspace begins at 700 feet AGL", "Class E begins at 1,200 feet AGL next to Class G", "a TFR is active"], "ans": 0, "why": "Inside the magenta vignette, the Class E floor is 700 AGL."}, {"id": "d27c1q1", "card": "d27c1", "q": "For a course that runs nearly north or south, measure it with the plotter against a", "opts": ["parallel of latitude, using the inner scale", "meridian at the departure airport", "Victor airway"], "ans": 0, "why": "A course close to a meridian is easier to read against a parallel."}, {"id": "d27c1q2", "card": "d27c1", "q": "When measuring a true course with a plotter, it is best to read the angle where the course line crosses a", "opts": ["meridian near the middle of the route", "parallel at the starting point", "compass rose on a VOR"], "ans": 0, "why": "Meridians converge, so the midpoint gives the best average."}, {"id": "d27c2q1", "card": "d27c2", "q": "Victor airways are shown on a sectional as", "opts": ["light blue lines between VORs", "magenta dashed lines", "solid red lines"], "ans": 0, "why": "Victor airways are Class E, 4 nm each side of centerline."}, {"id": "d27c2q2", "card": "d27c2", "q": "To confirm a destination's runway lengths, pattern information, and lighting before a flight, the best source is the", "opts": ["Chart Supplement", "sectional legend only", "winds aloft forecast"], "ans": 0, "why": "The Chart Supplement gives airport details the chart cannot."}, {"id": "d28c1q1", "card": "d28c1", "q": "ATIS is", "opts": ["a recorded broadcast of weather and airport information, identified by a phonetic letter", "a live controller frequency", "a weather briefing from Flight Service"], "ans": 0, "why": "Tell ATC you have the information letter on first contact."}, {"id": "d28c1q2", "card": "d28c1", "q": "The CTAF is", "opts": ["the frequency used for self-announced traffic calls at an airport without an operating tower", "the ATIS frequency", "an emergency frequency"], "ans": 0, "why": "Common Traffic Advisory Frequency: tell others where you are and what you're doing."}, {"id": "d28c2q1", "card": "d28c2", "q": "An alternating red and green light from the tower means", "opts": ["exercise extreme caution", "cleared for takeoff", "return to the ramp"], "ans": 0, "why": "Alternating red and green: caution, in the air or on the ground."}, {"id": "d28c2q2", "card": "d28c2", "q": "On the ground, a flashing green light from the tower means", "opts": ["cleared to taxi", "cleared for takeoff", "stop"], "ans": 0, "why": "Flashing green on the ground: cleared to taxi."}, {"id": "d29c1q1", "card": "d29c1", "q": "Compared with the wind a few thousand feet up, surface wind is usually", "opts": ["lighter and blowing more toward low pressure", "stronger and parallel to the isobars", "exactly the same"], "ans": 0, "why": "Friction slows surface wind and weakens the Coriolis turn."}, {"id": "d29c1q2", "card": "d29c1", "q": "In the northern hemisphere, the Coriolis force deflects moving air", "opts": ["to the right", "to the left", "upward"], "ans": 0, "why": "That is why wind aloft flows nearly parallel to the isobars."}, {"id": "d29c2q1", "card": "d29c2", "q": "On a sunny afternoon along a large lake or coast, a sea breeze usually blows", "opts": ["from the water toward the land", "from the land toward the water", "parallel to the shore"], "ans": 0, "why": "Land heats faster; air rises over land and is replaced from the water."}, {"id": "d29c2q2", "card": "d29c2", "q": "In the northern hemisphere, air circulates around a high-pressure area", "opts": ["clockwise and outward", "counterclockwise and inward", "straight toward the center"], "ans": 0, "why": "Highs: clockwise, outward, sinking air."}, {"id": "d29c3q1", "card": "d29c3", "q": "The dew point is", "opts": ["the temperature to which air must cool to become saturated", "the temperature at which water boils at altitude", "the lowest temperature reached overnight"], "ans": 0, "why": "At the dew point, relative humidity is 100 percent."}, {"id": "d29c3q2", "card": "d29c3", "q": "A small and decreasing temperature/dew point spread warns of", "opts": ["fog or low clouds", "clear skies", "strong winds"], "ans": 0, "why": "As the spread closes, the air nears saturation."}, {"id": "d30c1q1", "card": "d30c1", "q": "Unsaturated air that is forced to rise cools at about", "opts": ["3 °C per 1,000 feet (the dry adiabatic rate)", "1 °C per 1,000 feet", "5 °C per 1,000 feet"], "ans": 0, "why": "The dry adiabatic lapse rate is about 3 °C (5.4 °F) per 1,000 feet."}, {"id": "d30c1q2", "card": "d30c1", "q": "The standard (average) temperature lapse rate in the lower atmosphere is about", "opts": ["2 °C per 1,000 feet", "3 °C per 1,000 feet", "1 °C per 1,000 feet"], "ans": 0, "why": "The standard lapse rate is about 2 °C (3.5 °F) per 1,000 feet."}, {"id": "d30c2q1", "card": "d30c2", "q": "When cold air moves over a warmer surface, the air becomes", "opts": ["more unstable, with cumulus clouds and showers", "more stable, with fog", "unchanged"], "ans": 0, "why": "Heating from below makes air unstable (lake-effect snow is an example)."}, {"id": "d30c2q2", "card": "d30c2", "q": "A temperature inversion is a layer in which temperature", "opts": ["increases with altitude", "decreases rapidly with altitude", "stays constant all the way up"], "ans": 0, "why": "Inversions are very stable and trap haze and smoke."}, {"id": "d30c3q1", "card": "d30c3", "q": "A warm front typically brings", "opts": ["widespread stratiform clouds, steady rain or drizzle, and poor visibility well ahead of the front", "a narrow line of thunderstorms and rapid clearing", "clear skies"], "ans": 0, "why": "Warm air rides up a gentle slope over a large area."}, {"id": "d30c3q2", "card": "d30c3", "q": "A fast-moving cold front typically brings", "opts": ["a narrow band of cumuliform clouds, gusty winds, and showers, then rapid clearing", "a wide area of stratus and drizzle lasting days", "calm winds and fog"], "ans": 0, "why": "Cold fronts are steep and fast; the weather is intense but brief."}, {"id": "d31c1q1", "card": "d31c1", "q": "Advection fog forms when", "opts": ["moist air moves over a colder surface, often along coasts", "the ground cools on a calm night", "cold air moves over warm water"], "ans": 0, "why": "It needs wind and can be widespread and persistent."}, {"id": "d31c1q2", "card": "d31c1", "q": "Upslope fog forms when", "opts": ["moist, stable air is pushed up sloping terrain and cools", "cold air moves over warm water", "rain falls through cool air"], "ans": 0, "why": "Lifting cools the air adiabatically to its dew point."}, {"id": "d31c2q1", "card": "d31c2", "q": "The dissipating stage of a thunderstorm is characterized by", "opts": ["downdrafts dominating the cell", "continuous updrafts", "rapid upward growth"], "ans": 0, "why": "The storm rains itself out."}, {"id": "d31c2q2", "card": "d31c2", "q": "The mature stage of a thunderstorm begins when", "opts": ["precipitation reaches the surface", "the anvil top first appears", "downdrafts take over the entire cell"], "ans": 0, "why": "Mature cells have both updrafts and downdrafts: the most violent stage."}, {"id": "d31c3q1", "card": "d31c3", "q": "The AIM advises avoiding a severe thunderstorm or intense radar echo by at least", "opts": ["20 nautical miles", "5 nautical miles", "2 nautical miles"], "ans": 0, "why": "Hail and turbulence can extend well beyond the visible storm."}, {"id": "d32c1q1", "card": "d32c1", "q": "Clear ice, which is glossy and heavy, forms mostly from", "opts": ["large supercooled droplets, as in cumuliform clouds or freezing rain", "small droplets in stratus clouds", "snow"], "ans": 0, "why": "Large drops flow back before freezing."}, {"id": "d32c1q2", "card": "d32c1", "q": "Rime ice, which is rough and milky, forms mostly from", "opts": ["small supercooled droplets in stratiform clouds", "large droplets in freezing rain", "frost on the ground"], "ans": 0, "why": "Small droplets freeze instantly and trap air."}, {"id": "d32c2q1", "card": "d32c2", "q": "Mountain wave turbulence is most likely when", "opts": ["strong winds blow roughly perpendicular to a ridge in stable air", "winds are light and variable", "the air is very unstable with thunderstorms"], "ans": 0, "why": "Stable air flowing across a ridge sets up standing waves and rotors."}, {"id": "d32c2q2", "card": "d32c2", "q": "Mechanical turbulence is caused by", "opts": ["wind flowing over buildings, trees, and rough terrain", "rising thermals on a hot day", "the wake of a large airplane"], "ans": 0, "why": "It is worse in strong wind and on the downwind side of obstacles."}, {"id": "d32c3q1", "card": "d32c3", "q": "On a calm, cold morning, a pilot may meet sudden wind shear shortly after takeoff because", "opts": ["strong wind may be blowing above a surface temperature inversion", "the inversion always causes thunderstorms", "calm air never has shear"], "ans": 0, "why": "Climbing through the top of the inversion can mean a sudden wind change."}, {"id": "d33c1q1", "card": "d33c1", "q": "If your flight is six or more hours away, the appropriate Flight Service briefing is", "opts": ["an outlook briefing", "an abbreviated briefing", "a standard briefing"], "ans": 0, "why": "Get a standard briefing closer to departure."}, {"id": "d33c2q1", "card": "d33c2", "q": "The ceiling is", "opts": ["the lowest broken or overcast layer, or the vertical visibility into an obscuration", "the lowest layer of any coverage", "the highest cloud layer reported"], "ans": 0, "why": "Scattered and few layers are not ceilings."}, {"id": "d33c2q2", "card": "d33c2", "q": "In a METAR, BKN means the sky is", "opts": ["5/8 to 7/8 covered", "3/8 to 4/8 covered", "fully covered"], "ans": 0, "why": "FEW 1-2/8, SCT 3-4/8, BKN 5-7/8, OVC 8/8."}, {"id": "d33c3q1", "card": "d33c3", "q": "In a TAF, TEMPO means", "opts": ["temporary changes, each lasting less than an hour, within the stated period", "a permanent change starting at a specific time", "a gradual change over the period"], "ans": 0, "why": "FM marks a lasting change; BECMG a gradual one."}, {"id": "d33c3q2", "card": "d33c3", "q": "A METAR shows a ceiling of 2,500 feet and visibility of 6 SM. The flight category is", "opts": ["MVFR", "VFR", "IFR"], "ans": 0, "why": "Ceiling 1,000 to 3,000 feet makes it marginal VFR."}, {"id": "d33c4q1", "card": "d33c4", "q": "In the METAR group BKN014 OVC025, the ceiling is", "opts": ["1,400 feet", "2,500 feet", "140 feet"], "ans": 0, "why": "The ceiling is the lowest broken or overcast layer."}, {"id": "d33c4q2", "card": "d33c4", "q": "In a TAF, \"FM042000 29015G25KT\" after a south wind indicates", "opts": ["a wind shift to the northwest as a cold front passes, from 2000Z on the 4th", "a temporary thunderstorm", "the forecast ends at 2000Z"], "ans": 0, "why": "FM marks a lasting change; the south-to-northwest shift with clearing is the front."}, {"id": "d34c1q1", "card": "d34c1", "q": "The Graphical Forecasts for Aviation (GFA) provides", "opts": ["forecast and observed clouds, weather, winds, icing, and turbulence on maps", "only airport METARs", "only winds aloft in text"], "ans": 0, "why": "The GFA replaced the old text area forecast."}, {"id": "d34c1q2", "card": "d34c1", "q": "A (non-convective) SIGMET warns of", "opts": ["severe icing, severe or extreme turbulence, dust or sand storms, or volcanic ash", "moderate icing only", "IFR ceilings"], "ans": 0, "why": "SIGMETs cover hazards to all aircraft."}, {"id": "d34c2q1", "card": "d34c2", "q": "Winds aloft are forecast at 6,000 feet as 2715+05 and at 9,000 feet as 2825+00. At 7,500 feet, the best estimate is about", "opts": ["275 degrees at 20 knots, +2 to +3 °C", "270 degrees at 15 knots, +5 °C", "280 degrees at 25 knots, 0 °C"], "ans": 0, "why": "Interpolate halfway between the two levels."}, {"id": "d34c2q2", "card": "d34c2", "q": "A winds aloft forecast shows 2714-08 at 6,000 feet. This means", "opts": ["270 degrees true at 14 knots, temperature -8 °C", "270 degrees magnetic at 14 knots, -8 °C", "27 knots from 140 degrees, -8 °C"], "ans": 0, "why": "Direction (true), speed, temperature."}, {"id": "d35c1q1", "card": "d35c1", "q": "In the heading calculation chain, true course corrected for wind correction angle gives", "opts": ["true heading", "magnetic course", "compass heading"], "ans": 0, "why": "TC +/- WCA = TH; then variation gives MH; deviation gives CH."}, {"id": "d35c1q2", "card": "d35c1", "q": "Navigating by computing heading, time, and distance from known airspeed and wind is called", "opts": ["dead reckoning", "pilotage", "radio navigation"], "ans": 0, "why": "Dead reckoning uses math; pilotage uses landmarks."}, {"id": "d35c2q1", "card": "d35c2", "q": "If a VFR flight plan is not closed, search and rescue procedures normally begin about", "opts": ["30 minutes after the estimated arrival time", "5 minutes after the estimated arrival time", "24 hours after departure"], "ans": 0, "why": "Close your flight plan with Flight Service on arrival."}, {"id": "d35c2q2", "card": "d35c2", "q": "A 75 nm leg at a groundspeed of 100 knots takes about", "opts": ["45 minutes", "75 minutes", "33 minutes"], "ans": 0, "why": "Time = distance / groundspeed = 0.75 hour."}, {"id": "d36c1q1", "card": "d36c1", "q": "A VOR radial is", "opts": ["a magnetic bearing extending outward FROM the station", "a true bearing TO the station", "a line of constant latitude"], "ans": 0, "why": "Radials are magnetic courses from the station."}, {"id": "d36c1q2", "card": "d36c1", "q": "Before using a VOR for navigation, you should", "opts": ["identify it by listening to its Morse code identifier", "check it against the GPS only", "confirm the frequency is a whole number"], "ans": 0, "why": "The identifier confirms you have the right station and it is working."}, {"id": "d36c2q1", "card": "d36c2", "q": "If you become lost, the five Cs start with", "opts": ["climb", "confess", "conserve"], "ans": 0, "why": "Climb, communicate, confess, comply, conserve."}, {"id": "d36c2q2", "card": "d36c2", "q": "When diverting to an alternate airport in flight, a good first step is to", "opts": ["turn promptly toward the alternate, then work out heading, time, and fuel", "finish calculations before turning", "climb to the highest possible altitude"], "ans": 0, "why": "Start toward the new destination and refine the numbers en route."}, {"id": "d37c1q1", "card": "d37c1", "q": "Before any flight, 91.103 requires the PIC to become familiar with", "opts": ["runway lengths at airports of intended use and takeoff and landing distance information", "the airplane's maintenance cost", "the names of the passengers"], "ans": 0, "why": "For flights away from the airport, add weather, fuel, alternatives, and known delays."}, {"id": "d37c1q2", "card": "d37c1", "q": "A pilot who deviates from a rule during an in-flight emergency must", "opts": ["send a written report to the FAA if requested", "file a report within 24 hours in every case", "surrender the pilot certificate"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.3(c)."}, {"id": "d37c2q1", "card": "d37c2", "q": "To carry passengers, a pilot must have made at least three takeoffs and landings in the same category and class within the preceding", "opts": ["90 days", "30 days", "6 calendar months"], "ans": 0, "why": "61.57(a)."}, {"id": "d37c2q2", "card": "d37c2", "q": "A private pilot under age 40 holding a third-class medical may use it for private privileges until the end of the", "opts": ["60th calendar month after the exam month", "24th calendar month", "12th calendar month"], "ans": 0, "why": "61.23(d)."}, {"id": "d38c1q1", "card": "d38c1", "q": "An annual inspection is required within the preceding", "opts": ["12 calendar months", "24 calendar months", "100 hours"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.409(a)."}, {"id": "d38c1q2", "card": "d38c1", "q": "A 100-hour inspection is required when the airplane is", "opts": ["carrying a person for hire or used for flight instruction for hire provided by the instructor", "flown at night", "more than 10 years old"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.409(b)."}, {"id": "d38c2q1", "card": "d38c2", "q": "Under 91.213(d), if inoperative equipment is not required, the pilot may fly after the equipment is", "opts": ["removed or deactivated and placarded 'Inoperative'", "noted on a napkin", "reported to ATC"], "ans": 0, "why": "Deactivation needing maintenance must be done by a qualified person."}, {"id": "d38c2q2", "card": "d38c2", "q": "Which item is NOT required by 91.205 for day VFR flight?", "opts": ["A landing light", "An airspeed indicator", "A fuel gauge for each tank"], "ans": 0, "why": "A landing light is only required at night when operating for hire."}, {"id": "d39c1q1", "card": "d39c1", "q": "Over open water or sparsely populated areas, an aircraft may not be operated closer than", "opts": ["500 feet to any person, vessel, vehicle, or structure", "1,000 feet to any person", "100 feet to any structure"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.119(c)."}, {"id": "d39c1q2", "card": "d39c1", "q": "When overtaking another aircraft, you should pass", "opts": ["well clear on the right; the aircraft being overtaken has the right-of-way", "on the left", "directly above it"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.113(f)."}, {"id": "d39c2q1", "card": "d39c2", "q": "Required flight crew must use oxygen for flight of more than 30 minutes at cabin pressure altitudes above", "opts": ["12,500 feet up to and including 14,000 feet MSL", "10,000 feet MSL", "15,000 feet MSL"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.211(a)(1)."}, {"id": "d39c2q2", "card": "d39c2", "q": "Each occupant must be provided supplemental oxygen at cabin pressure altitudes above", "opts": ["15,000 feet MSL", "12,500 feet MSL", "10,000 feet MSL"], "ans": 0, "why": "91.211(a)(3)."}, {"id": "d40c1q1", "card": "d40c1", "q": "Carbon monoxide poisoning is an example of", "opts": ["hypemic hypoxia", "hypoxic hypoxia", "stagnant hypoxia"], "ans": 0, "why": "CO keeps the blood from carrying oxygen."}, {"id": "d40c1q2", "card": "d40c1", "q": "You smell exhaust and feel a headache while using the cabin heater. You should", "opts": ["turn off the heater, open fresh air vents, use oxygen if available, and land", "turn up the heater", "climb higher"], "ans": 0, "why": "These are signs of carbon monoxide poisoning."}, {"id": "d40c2q1", "card": "d40c2", "q": "A rapid acceleration, such as on takeoff in low visibility, can make you feel the nose is pitching up. This is", "opts": ["the somatogravic illusion", "the leans", "the graveyard spin"], "ans": 0, "why": "A pilot may push the nose down dangerously."}, {"id": "d40c2q2", "card": "d40c2", "q": "In a graveyard spiral, a pilot who has lost visual references", "opts": ["feels level while descending in a tightening turn and may pull back, steepening the spiral", "feels a climb while level", "feels nothing unusual"], "ans": 0, "why": "Level the wings on instruments before pulling back."}, {"id": "d40c3q1", "card": "d40c3", "q": "After scuba diving that does not require a controlled ascent, the AIM recommends waiting before flying up to 8,000 feet for at least", "opts": ["12 hours", "1 hour", "3 hours"], "ans": 0, "why": "Wait 24 hours after dives requiring controlled ascent or for flight above 8,000 feet."}, {"id": "d40c3q2", "card": "d40c3", "q": "Eyes adapt to darkness most fully in about", "opts": ["30 minutes", "2 minutes", "2 hours"], "ans": 0, "why": "Avoid bright white light once adapted."}, {"id": "d41c1q1", "card": "d41c1", "q": "The FAA's 3P model of risk management is", "opts": ["Perceive, Process, Perform", "Plan, Pilot, Proceed", "Pause, Pray, Push"], "ans": 0, "why": "Perceive hazards, process risk, perform to reduce it."}, {"id": "d41c1q2", "card": "d41c1", "q": "A pilot thinks, 'Accidents happen to other people, not me.' This is", "opts": ["invulnerability", "anti-authority", "resignation"], "ans": 0, "why": "Antidote: it could happen to me."}, {"id": "d41c2q1", "card": "d41c2", "q": "The radio call that indicates distress (grave and imminent danger) is", "opts": ["'Mayday, Mayday, Mayday'", "'Pan-pan, pan-pan, pan-pan'", "'Emergency, emergency'"], "ans": 0, "why": "Pan-pan indicates urgency."}, {"id": "d41c2q2", "card": "d41c2", "q": "If the engine fails just after takeoff at low altitude, the generally recommended action is to", "opts": ["lower the nose and land mostly straight ahead", "turn back to the runway immediately", "climb to gain altitude"], "ans": 0, "why": "Turning back from low altitude is a well-known killer."}, {"id": "d42c1q1", "card": "d42c1", "q": "The private pilot knowledge test result is valid for", "opts": ["24 calendar months", "12 calendar months", "6 calendar months"], "ans": 0, "why": "You must pass the checkride within that window."}, {"id": "d42c1q2", "card": "d42c1", "q": "The passing score on the private pilot knowledge test is", "opts": ["70 percent", "80 percent", "60 percent"], "ans": 0, "why": "60 questions, 2.5 hours, 70 percent to pass."}, {"id": "d42c2q1", "card": "d42c2", "q": "On the oral, if you do not know an answer, the best response is to", "opts": ["say so, then explain how you would find out", "guess confidently", "change the subject"], "ans": 0, "why": "Knowing where to look something up is a skill the ACS values."}, {"id": "d43c1q1", "card": "d43c1", "q": "A NOTAM that says 'RWY 18/36 CLSD' means", "opts": ["runway 18/36 is closed", "runway 18/36 is closed to night operations only", "runway 18/36 has new lighting"], "ans": 0, "why": "CLSD means closed."}, {"id": "d43c1q2", "card": "d43c1", "q": "NOTAM times are given in", "opts": ["UTC (Zulu)", "local time", "Eastern time"], "ans": 0, "why": "Convert to local; CDT is UTC minus 5."}, {"id": "d43c2q1", "card": "d43c2", "q": "TFRs are published as", "opts": ["FDC NOTAMs", "AIRMETs", "routine METAR remarks"], "ans": 0, "why": "FDC NOTAMs are regulatory."}, {"id": "d43c3q1", "card": "d43c3", "q": "A stadium TFR applies from", "opts": ["1 hour before the scheduled start until 1 hour after the event ends", "30 minutes before until the event ends", "the event start until the event ends"], "ans": 0, "why": "Check the actual end time; it can run late."}, {"id": "d43c3q2", "card": "d43c3", "q": "A standard stadium TFR covers the area within", "opts": ["3 nm of the stadium, up to and including 3,000 feet AGL", "5 nm of the stadium up to 5,000 feet MSL", "1 nm of the stadium up to 1,000 feet AGL"], "ans": 0, "why": "This is the published special security instruction for major events."}, {"id": "d44c1q1", "card": "d44c1", "q": "The destination TAF calls for gusts and showers from 2000Z. The key planning fact is", "opts": ["finish the return well before 2000Z", "cancel the outbound leg", "depart after 2000Z"], "ans": 0, "why": "The outbound leg is fine; the deadline drives the return."}, {"id": "d44c1q2", "card": "d44c1", "q": "The winds aloft entry 0312+08 means", "opts": ["030 degrees at 12 knots, plus 8 °C", "300 degrees at 12 knots, minus 8 °C", "030 degrees at 8 knots, plus 12 °C"], "ans": 0, "why": "First two digits are direction in tens of degrees, next two are speed, then temperature."}, {"id": "d44c2q1", "card": "d44c2", "q": "Field elevation 900 feet, altimeter 30.10. Pressure altitude is about", "opts": ["720 feet", "1,080 feet", "900 feet"], "ans": 0, "why": "900 + (29.92 - 30.10) x 1,000 = 720 feet."}, {"id": "d44c2q2", "card": "d44c2", "q": "Total weight 2,270 lb and total moment 91,670 lb-in give a CG of about", "opts": ["40.4 inches", "37.0 inches", "48.0 inches"], "ans": 0, "why": "CG = total moment / total weight."}, {"id": "d44c3q1", "card": "d44c3", "q": "The legal VFR day fuel reserve under 91.151 is enough to fly", "opts": ["30 minutes beyond the first point of intended landing at normal cruise", "45 minutes beyond the destination", "1 hour beyond the destination"], "ans": 0, "why": "It would be 45 minutes at night."}, {"id": "d44c3q2", "card": "d44c3", "q": "True heading 057 with 2 degrees east variation gives a magnetic heading of", "opts": ["055", "059", "057"], "ans": 0, "why": "East is least: subtract easterly variation."}]};
